window.VCI_SITE_INDEX = [{"href":"claim-path.html","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"Your claim, step by step","sub":"Free, independent 7-lesson path through a VA disability claim, from service connection to your decision: the key rules in plain English with public sources, and links to the ClaimReady guides and tools for each step. Progress stays on your device.","text":"Seven short lessons that follow a VA disability claim in order, from how service connection works to what to do after a decision. Each lesson gives you three key points in plain English, the public law behind each one, and the ClaimReady guides to read next. Each takes about five minutes. Not sure where you are in your claim? Start with Your claim roadmap."},{"href":"claim-path.html#l1","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"How service connection works","text":"What you'll learn: The three things VA looks for in most claims, and the different ways a condition can be connected to service. Three key points Most claims need three things: a current disability, an event, injury, or disease in service, and a link between the two. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.303 Court decision Shedden v. Principi, 381 F.3d 1163 (Fed. Cir. 2004) For some conditions the law presumes the link, so no medical opinion on it is needed. Examples are certain chronic diseases that show up within a set time after discharge, and conditions tied to toxic exposures, including those added by the PACT Act. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.307; 38 C.F.R. § 3.309 A condition can also be connected when a service-connected condition caused or worsened it (secondary), or when service made a condition you already had worse (aggravation). Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.310; 38 C.F.R. § 3.306 Read next on ClaimReady Began in service Direct service connection Presumptive conditions When the link is presumed PACT Act overview Toxic exposure presumptions Secondary conditions Caused or worsened by a rated condition Existed before service Aggravation and the presumption of soundness Mark lesson 1 done Next: Before you file →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l2","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"Before you file","text":"What you'll learn: How to protect your start date, and what evidence to gather before you file. Three key points An intent to file gives you up to one year to file the full claim and can protect an earlier effective date. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.155(b) VA must help you get records. It requests federal records, such as service treatment records and VA treatment records. You can let VA request private records with VA Form 21-4142, or send them yourself. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A; 38 C.F.R. § 3.159 Your own statements, and statements from people who know you, are evidence of what you saw, did, and felt. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1154(a) Court decision Jandreau v. Nicholson, 492 F.3d 1372 (Fed. Cir. 2007) Read next on ClaimReady Intent to file How it protects your date What evidence does VA need? Evidence rules and extra forms How VA weighs evidence Including lay statements Form 21-4142 guide Private medical records, section by section Character of discharge For veterans with a bad paper discharge Mark lesson 2 done Next: Filing your claim →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l3","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"Filing your claim","text":"What you'll learn: How to fill out the claim form, and how to describe your conditions so nothing is missed. Three key points A complete claim is filed on VA's required form. For disability compensation that is VA Form 21-526EZ. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.155; 38 C.F.R. § 3.160 Describe each condition and its symptoms in plain words. VA reads a claim by what you describe, not only by the name you give it. Court decision Clemons v. Shinseki, 23 Vet. App. 1 (2009) You can file online, by mail, in person, or with free help from an accredited representative. No one may charge you for help preparing an initial claim. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5904 Read next on ClaimReady Form 21-526EZ guide The claim form, section by section Claim checklist Every step to consider before you file Everything VA must decide The scope of your claim How to file and evidence Two ways to file Mark lesson 3 done Next: Your C&P exam →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l4","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"Your C&P exam","text":"What you'll learn: When VA orders an exam, how to prepare, and what makes an exam or medical opinion adequate. Three key points VA orders an exam when the record shows a current condition, something in service, and a sign they may be linked, but not enough to decide the claim. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A(d); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4) Court decision McLendon v. Nicholson, 20 Vet. App. 79 (2006) Go to the exam. If you miss it without good cause, VA may decide the claim on the record it has, or deny it. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.655 A medical opinion needs a reasoned explanation, not just a conclusion. For joints, the exam should address painful motion and flare-ups. Court decision Nieves-Rodriguez v. Peake, 22 Vet. App. 295 (2008); Sharp v. Shulkin, 29 Vet. App. 26 (2017) Read next on ClaimReady C&P exams: the complete guide From the day you file to the report Quick sheet for your doctor One page of what VA needs What VA asks your examiner Extra questions VA adds What makes a medical opinion count Rationale and adequacy Exam opinion check Check an opinion you received Painful motion and flare-ups Joint exams Toxic exposure exams (TERA) When VA orders one Mark lesson 4 done Next: How ratings work →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l5","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"How ratings work","text":"What you'll learn: How VA matches your symptoms to rating criteria, and why ratings combine instead of adding up. Three key points Each condition is rated under a diagnostic code. When your symptoms fall between two levels, VA assigns the higher one if your disability more nearly matches it. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.7 Ratings combine instead of adding up. For example, 50% and 30% combine to 65%, which rounds to 70%, not 80%. The same symptoms can't be rated twice under different codes. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.25; 38 C.F.R. § 4.14 When the evidence for and against is about equal, the veteran gets the benefit of the doubt. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5107(b) Read next on ClaimReady How VA assigns a rating The steps VA follows Key rating rules (Part 4) Rules for every rating Rating-criteria explainer Each level in plain words Combine ratings See how ratings combine Mark lesson 5 done Next: You got a decision →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l6","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"You got a decision","text":"What you'll learn: How to read your decision, check that it covers everything, and pick a review option without losing your effective date. Three key points VA's decision notice must tell you what was decided, the reasons, and your review options. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104; 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f) There are three review options: a Supplemental Claim with new and relevant evidence (VA Form 20-0995), a Higher-Level Review of the same record (VA Form 20-0996), or an appeal to the Board (VA Form 10182). Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500; 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501; 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601; 38 C.F.R. § 20.202 Filing a review option within one year of the decision generally keeps your original effective date. This is called continuous pursuit. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h) Read next on ClaimReady Decode your decision letter Featured: what common phrases mean Reading your rating decision Each section and the codes Does your decision cover everything? Nine questions to ask Supplemental Claims Featured article Form 20-0995 guide Supplemental Claim Form 20-0996 guide Higher-Level Review Form 10182 guide Board appeal Continuous Pursuit Review Beta: check your timeline Effective dates When payments can start Mark lesson 6 done Next: Other benefits to know about →"},{"href":"claim-path.html#l7","page":"Your Claim, Step by Step","kind":"Lesson","title":"Other benefits to know about","text":"What you'll learn: The benefits beyond your rating percentage that are worth asking about. Three key points If service-connected conditions keep you from holding a steady job, you may be paid at the 100% rate through Individual Unemployability (TDIU), claimed on VA Form 21-8940. The usual threshold is one condition at 60%, or 70% combined with one condition at 40%. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.16 Special monthly compensation is added for things like loss of use, needing help with daily activities, or being housebound. VA Form 21-2680 is the exam form for aid and attendance or housebound status. A spouse who needs aid and attendance can add to the payment too. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1114(k)–(s); 38 U.S.C. § 1115; 38 C.F.R. § 3.350; 38 C.F.R. § 3.351; 38 C.F.R. § 3.352 A temporary 100% rating can apply during a hospital stay of more than 21 days or recovery from certain surgery. Family benefits, such as Chapter 35 education and CHAMPVA, may follow a permanent and total rating. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.29; 38 C.F.R. § 4.30 Read next on ClaimReady Individual Unemployability TDIU explained TDIU calculator Check the percentage rules Form 21-8940 guide The TDIU form Special monthly compensation SMC explained SMC checklist Which levels may fit Form 21-2680 guide Aid and attendance exam Spouse aid and attendance Featured article Temporary 100% Timeline Hospital stays and surgery Chapter 35 education (DEA) For families CHAMPVA for families Health coverage Veteran Readiness and Employment VR&E Mark lesson 7 done Back to the start ↑"},{"href":"competency.html","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"VA competency decisions: who manages your VA payments, and how to keep or get back that right","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA competency decisions: what competency means, the presumption of competency, notice and hearing rights, 100% mental health ratings, court guardianships, and restoring competency. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"When VA decides a claim, it may also decide whether you can manage your own VA benefit payments. This is called a competency decision. VA starts from the presumption that you can. This article explains what competency means, the evidence VA needs before finding otherwise, your right to notice and a hearing, why a 100% mental health rating does not by itself mean you are incompetent, and how to get competency restored. Based on public VA information: the regulation at 38 C.F.R. § 3.353, VA's due process rules at 38 C.F.R. § 3.103, VA's procedures manual (M21-1), VA's public Fiduciary Program page, and a published court decision. For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"competency.html#basics","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Competency in six points","text":"It is about money. Competency means having the mental capacity to contract or to manage your own affairs, including spending your benefit payments without limits. You are presumed competent. VA may find you incompetent only on clear and convincing evidence. Reasonable doubt is resolved in favor of competency. A doctor's clear statement is normally needed. Unless the medical evidence leaves no doubt, VA needs a definite statement from a responsible medical authority. You get notice and a chance to be heard first. VA must propose the finding, tell you why, give you time to send evidence, and hold a hearing before deciding if you ask for one. A 100% rating is not the same thing. A total rating for a mental condition does not automatically mean you cannot manage your payments. It can be restored. Credible medical or other evidence can restore competency, and restoring it does not by itself lower your rating. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.353(a), (c)–(e); § 3.103(b)(2) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.1–4"},{"href":"competency.html#meaning","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"What \"incompetent\" means to VA","text":"VA's rule says a person is mentally incompetent when, because of injury or disease, they lack the mental capacity to contract or to manage their own affairs, including spending funds without limitation. What it covers Whether you can handle your VA benefit payments: paying bills, meeting basic needs, and not losing the money to poor judgment or to people who take advantage. It must come from an injury or disease, not from disagreements about how you choose to spend. What it does not cover VA's Fiduciary Program page says a VA fiduciary manages VA benefits only. It says your non-VA finances, your right to vote, and your ability to sign legal documents are not affected. It is not a diagnosis, and it is not a disability rating. If VA finds a veteran incompetent, it appoints a fiduciary, often a family member or other trusted person, to receive and manage the VA payments. VA's page says it considers your preference when possible, and that a spouse, dependent, or relative serving as fiduciary cannot be paid a fee. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.353(a); Part 13 (fiduciary rules) VA guidance VA.gov, VA Fiduciary Program"},{"href":"competency.html#standard","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"How strong the evidence must be","text":"Most VA decisions turn on whether the evidence is at least evenly balanced. A finding of incompetency needs much more. If the evidence VA's guidance says Raises a question, but is not clear and convincing VA does not propose incompetency. The decision should say there was no clear and convincing evidence of incompetency. Is close, so reasonable doubt exists The doubt goes in your favor. You stay competent. Clearly and convincingly shows you cannot manage your payments without limits VA proposes a finding of incompetency and gives you notice. It does not make a final finding yet. VA must look at all the evidence, not one exam. The rule says the percentage of disability, facts about hospitalization or commitment, and the competency finding should be consistent with each other. A finding that does not fit the rest of the record deserves a hard look. Unless the medical evidence leaves no doubt, VA needs a definite expression from a responsible medical authority. A vague note that you \"may have trouble with finances\" is not that. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102, 3.353(c)–(d) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.1.c–d; 6.A.2.a–b"},{"href":"competency.html#total","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Why your decision may mention competency","text":"VA's guidance tells decision makers to address competency whenever a mental condition is rated 100%, or a 100% rating is continued. That is a routine check, not a sign VA thinks something is wrong. When VA addresses it A mental condition rated or continued at 100%. Individual unemployability granted or continued based on a single mental health condition. A temporary 100% rating for a hospital stay for a mental disorder (38 C.F.R. § 4.29). What VA must weigh VA's guidance says a total rating does not automatically mean you cannot manage your benefits, even though many symptoms behind a 100% rating could affect it. VA must look at the facts of your case against the clear and convincing standard. If VA finds you competent, your decision letter may include a sentence like this one: There is no evidence of record that shows that you are unable to manage your financial affairs. (38 CFR 3.353) That sentence means VA considered competency and found no reason to question it. Nothing more is needed from you. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.353, 4.29 VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.2.a"},{"href":"competency.html#proposal","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When VA proposes finding you incompetent","text":"A proposal is not a final decision. It is VA's notice that it intends to make the finding, and your chance to respond first. VA sends the proposal. It should explain the facts and reasons, and tell you about your right to a hearing. Your representative, if you have one, is notified too. You have 60 days to send evidence. VA's due process rule gives you 60 days to show why the action should not be taken. Check the letter for your exact dates. You can ask for a hearing. If you ask, the hearing must be held before VA makes a final finding. Hearings on proposed adverse actions are held before VA employees who did not take part in the proposal. VA decides on all the evidence. If you do not respond or do not take part in a hearing after proper notice, VA can decide on the evidence it has. If VA makes the finding, a fiduciary follows. VA's Fiduciary Program page says VA waits 60 days after the decision letter before notifying the Fiduciary Program, to give time to ask for a hearing or send more evidence. You can also ask for a decision review within one year. Evidence that can speak to it A statement from a treating doctor or psychologist who knows you, saying whether you can manage your payments and why, with examples. Facts about how you actually handle money: bills paid on time, a budget, autopay, no unpaid rent or utility shutoffs. Statements from family or others who see how you manage day to day, with how they know. Evidence that a hospitalization or crisis behind the proposal has passed, if that is the case. ClaimReady note, an educational tip rather than a rule: the strongest responses speak to money management itself, not just to diagnosis or symptoms, because that is what the definition asks. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(b)(2), (d); § 3.353(e) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.3.a; VA.gov, VA Fiduciary Program"},{"href":"competency.html#family","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When someone asks VA to look at competency","text":"A veteran asking for help A veteran or other beneficiary can ask VA to be rated incompetent or to have a fiduciary appointed, even without medical or court records. VA reviews its treatment records and may order an exam if the request and the record call for one. VA's guidance says a request like this is not automatically a claim for a higher mental health rating, though VA looks at whether the facts raise one. A family member or other person asking With medical or court evidence, VA treats it as evidence and can act on it. No special form is required. Without that evidence, VA checks its own treatment records. If they show nothing about competency, VA does not start a determination. VA's reply to someone else will not include the veteran's personal information. Competency decisions are not claims for benefits, though they often come up while a claim is being decided. The same rules apply to surviving spouses, parents, VA insurance beneficiaries, and adult children who became permanently unable to support themselves before 18. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.353(b), 3.356 VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.1.a, f; 6.A.2.c–d"},{"href":"competency.html#court","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When a court has found someone incompetent","text":"It counts as notice and hearing. If a court found the person incompetent, or appointed a guardian because of incompetency, VA does not have to send its own proposal first. It is not binding on VA. VA still makes its own decision. Its guidance says to give the court decree great weight, and still to require clear and convincing evidence. VA may not need more. Exams or field visits are not routine when a court finding is in the record. VA develops more only if the record raises doubt. A temporary appointment is different. Without a court finding of incompetency, VA must propose a finding and give the usual notice if it has enough evidence, or tell the sender what more it needs. If VA finds the person competent anyway, the decision should say whether the court decided the same way or differently. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.353(e) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.1.a–b; 6.A.5.a–b"},{"href":"competency.html#restore","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Getting control of your payments back","text":"What you can send Credible medical or other evidence that you can now manage your payments, such as a doctor's report. VA's Fiduciary Program page says to send a written request with supporting medical evidence to the Fiduciary Intake Center, PO Box 5211, Janesville, WI 53547-5211. A court decree restoring competency is also evidence VA must process. How VA decides The decision rests with VA's rating decision makers, not with a doctor. VA's manual cites Sims v. Nicholson on that authority. Reasonable doubt favors competency here too. VA should not routinely require a psychiatric exam or field exam first, though it can ask for one if needed. VA should also look at restoring competency on its own when a hospital summary, discharge from care, a field examiner's report, or another real change suggests it. Restoring competency does not by itself lower your rating. It also does not, on its own, show that a parent or surviving spouse no longer needs aid and attendance. Each of those is a separate question with its own rules. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.353(b)(3), (d) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.4.b–d; 6.A.5.c; VA.gov, VA Fiduciary Program Court Sims v. Nicholson, 19 Vet. App. 453 (2006)"},{"href":"competency.html#review","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Asking VA to look again","text":"A final competency finding can be reviewed like other decisions: a Higher-Level Review (no new evidence), a Supplemental Claim (new and relevant evidence), or a Board appeal. Disagreeing with who VA picked as fiduciary is a separate decision, and can be reviewed too. VA's page says to ask within one year of the decision letter. You can also ask for restoration at any time by sending new evidence, as described above. VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.6.A.1.a; VA.gov, VA Fiduciary Program"},{"href":"competency.html#prepare","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"If you received a proposal","text":"Mark the 60-day date from the proposal letter. Read the reasons VA gives. Note which records VA relied on. Decide whether to ask for a hearing, and ask in writing. Ask a treating provider for a statement about managing money, with reasons. Gather everyday proof: paid bills, a budget, statements from people who see you manage. Contact a free accredited representative. They can help you respond. This checklist is not saved. Print the page to keep it."},{"href":"competency.html#example","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look for Dana","text":"Fictional example Dana is rated 100% for PTSD. Each decision so far has said there is no evidence Dana cannot manage financial affairs. After a two-week hospital stay, a discharge note says Dana \"should have help with finances.\" VA sends a proposal to find Dana incompetent. Is the note enough? It is a medical statement, but VA must weigh whether it is a definite expression, whether it fits the rest of the record, and whether any reasonable doubt remains. Dana's response: within 60 days, Dana asks for a hearing and sends a letter from the outpatient psychiatrist who has treated Dana for three years, explaining with examples that Dana pays rent and bills on autopay and has not missed payments. Dana's spouse writes a statement too. What VA does: VA holds the hearing before deciding, and decides on all the evidence. Dana's rating: the competency question does not change the 100% rating either way. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"competency.html#myths","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"A 100% mental health rating means VA will take over my money.\" No. VA must address competency at 100%, but a total rating does not automatically mean incompetency. \"My family can call VA and have a fiduciary appointed.\" Not on a call alone. Without medical or court evidence, VA checks its records and does not start a determination if they show nothing. \"A proposal means it's already decided.\" No. A proposal comes before the decision, with time to send evidence and the right to a hearing. \"If I get my competency back, VA will cut my rating.\" No. Restored competency does not by itself justify lowering a rating. \"A court guardianship means VA has to agree.\" No. VA gives it great weight but makes its own decision. \"Being found incompetent means I lose control of everything.\" VA's page says a VA fiduciary manages VA benefits only. Other effects, such as under federal firearms law, are outside VA benefits; ask an accredited representative or attorney about them."},{"href":"competency.html#related","page":"VA Competency Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Related on ClaimReady","text":"Competency and fiduciaries, the short version Proposed reductions, how due process works when VA proposes an adverse change Individual unemployability and temporary 100% ratings Supplemental Claims, Higher-Level Review, and Board appeals What makes a medical opinion count"},{"href":"continuous-pursuit.html","page":"Continuous Pursuit Review","kind":"Tool","title":"VA Continuous Pursuit Review","sub":"Free, independent educational tool (beta): describe your VA claim history in your own words and see whether each review was filed in time to keep your original claim date. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":""},{"href":"continuous-pursuit.html#exwrap","page":"Continuous Pursuit Review","kind":"Tool","title":"View an example history","text":"Fictional example, for illustration only. Use this example"},{"href":"continuous-pursuit.html#feedback","page":"Continuous Pursuit Review","kind":"Tool","title":"Help improve this beta","text":"This tool is being tested. Your answers below build a short note you can copy and send to whoever shared this tool with you. The note never includes your claim history, dates, or conditions, and nothing is sent automatically. Did the timeline match what you wrote? Yes, all of it Mostly, I fixed a few things No, it missed or misread a lot Was the explanation easy to understand? Yes Partly No Who are you testing as? Veteran or family member VSO or advocate Other What was confusing or wrong? Please do not include names, numbers, or medical details. Copy feedback note"},{"href":"decision-letter.html","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Decode your decision letter","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to the common phrases in VA rating decision letters: what each one means in plain English, the law behind it, and which next step fits. Nothing is uploaded, saved, or sent.","text":"VA rating decisions use the same phrases again and again: \"no nexus,\" \"not shown in service,\" \"evaluation continued.\" Look up a phrase from your letter to see what it means in plain English, the law behind it, and which next step may fit. This guide explains common wording; it does not read your letter or decide your claim. You don't upload anything, and nothing you type here is saved or sent."},{"href":"decision-letter.html#must","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"What every decision notice must tell you","text":"Federal law lists what VA's written notice of a decision must include. If one is missing, that is worth asking about. The issues decided A summary of the evidence considered A summary of the laws and regulations that apply Any findings in your favor For a denial, the element or elements not met If applicable, what it takes to grant service connection or the next higher rating How to get the evidence used in the decision Your review options How to read a denial Most service connection denials turn on one of three questions: Do you have a current condition? Did something happen in service? Is there a link between them? The favorable findings tell you which ones are already answered yes. The reasons tell you which one is not. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(1)–(8) Court decision Shedden v. Principi, 381 F.3d 1163 (Fed. Cir. 2004)"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#g-parts","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Parts of the letter","text":"The sections every rating decision has, and what to look for in each."},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-decision","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Decision","text":"What it means Each issue is numbered with its outcome: granted, denied, continued, or deferred. A grant shows the percentage and the effective date. VA usually names the condition with the examiner's diagnosis and puts your words in parentheses, for example \"tinnitus (claimed as ringing in the ears).\" A next step that may fit Check that every condition you claimed appears, either decided or deferred. A claimed issue with no outcome is worth asking about. If the name looks different from what you claimed, the words in parentheses tell you it is the same claim. Read Does your decision cover everything? for the nine questions to ask. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b)(1); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(1) VA guidance M21-1, Part V, Subpart iv, Chapter 1 (rating decision format)","sub":"The list of what VA decided","kw":"decision decision the list of what va decided issues adjudicated granted outcome claimed as"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-evidence","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Evidence","text":"You may also see: Evidence considered What it means A list of everything VA reviewed, usually with dates: service records, VA and private treatment records, exam reports, statements, and forms. VA must give a summary of the evidence it considered. A next step that may fit Make sure everything you sent is on the list. If a key record is missing, that matters for the next step you choose. If VA had the record but misread it, a Higher-Level Review guide (20-0996) fits. If the record was never in the file, a Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995) lets you add it. You can ask VA for a copy of the evidence used. The notice must explain how to get it. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b)(2), (6); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(2), (7) VA guidance M21-1, Part V, Subpart iv, Chapter 1 (rating decision format)","sub":"What VA looked at","kw":"evidence evidence evidence considered what va looked at evidence list records reviewed exam report"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-notreceived","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Requested, but not received","text":"You may also see: We asked for … but did not receive What it means VA asked someone, such as a private doctor or a records office, for records and did not get them. When VA cannot get records, it must tell you which ones, what it tried, what it will do next, and that you are ultimately responsible for getting them to VA. A next step that may fit If you can get those records yourself, sending them with a Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995) puts them in the record. If you told VA about records and the letter does not show that VA tried to get them, that may be a duty-to-assist question. A Higher-Level Review guide (20-0996) can look at it. The Form 21-4142 guide explains how to authorize VA to request private records. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c), (e)(1)","sub":"Records VA tried and failed to get","kw":"requested, but not received requested, but not received we asked for … but did not receive records va tried and failed to get duty to assist records missing private doctor 4142"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-reasons","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Reasons for decision","text":"What it means This is the most important part of the letter. For each issue, VA explains the law it applied and how the evidence fits. For a grant, it explains the rating, what the next higher rating requires, and why it picked the effective date. For a denial, it names the requirement that was not met. A next step that may fit For each denial, underline the sentence that names the missing element. The entries under Why a claim was denied explain the common ones. For each grant, find the sentence about the next higher rating. That is the gap between your symptoms and the next level. If a theory you raised (such as secondary) is not discussed, that is worth raising in a review. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b)(3), (5), (7); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(3), (5), (6) VA guidance M21-1, Part V, Subpart iv, Chapter 1 (rating decision format)","sub":"Why VA decided each issue the way it did","kw":"reasons for decision reasons for decision why va decided each issue the way it did explanation analysis why denied"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-favorable","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Favorable findings","text":"You may also see: The evidence shows … ; We have made the following favorable findings What it means A favorable finding is a fact or conclusion VA decided for you, even in a denial. For example: \"Your service treatment records show you injured your left knee in June 1998,\" or \"You have a current diagnosis of sleep apnea.\" VA must list them in the notice. A next step that may fit These are already proven. Later VA decision makers and the Board are bound by them, so new evidence can focus only on what is still missing. Example: if the findings say you have a diagnosis and an injury in service, the denial was about the link. A medical opinion on the link is what would matter. See Medical opinions explained. The statute lets a later decision maker set a finding aside only with clear and convincing evidence to the contrary. VA's regulation phrases the bar as evidence showing clear and unmistakable error in the finding. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b)(4); 38 U.S.C. § 5104A; 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(4); 38 C.F.R. § 3.104(c)","sub":"Facts VA already decided in your favor","kw":"favorable findings favorable findings the evidence shows … we have made the following favorable findings facts va already decided in your favor binding favorable finding proven 5104a"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-refs","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"References","text":"You may also see: Laws and regulations ; Title 38 Code of Federal Regulations What it means A list or summary of the statutes and regulations VA used. \"38 C.F.R.\" is the Code of Federal Regulations, Title 38, where VA's rules live. Part 3 covers service connection and effective dates; Part 4 is the rating schedule. A next step that may fit Look up any section on eCFR (links in this guide go there). The rule's exact words often show what evidence would meet it. If the letter cites a rating code (for example 5260 for limited knee bending), the criteria for each percentage are in Part 4. See Lesson 5: How ratings work. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(b)(3); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(3)","sub":"The rules VA applied","kw":"references references laws and regulations title 38 code of federal regulations the rules va applied cfr part 3 part 4 regulation statute"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-options","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Your review options","text":"You may also see: What you should do if you disagree with our decision What it means The notice must summarize your review options: a Supplemental Claim, a Higher-Level Review, or a Board appeal. Usually you have one year from the date of the notice to choose one and keep your original effective date. A next step that may fit See Which review fits below, and Lesson 6: You got a decision. Only one review option at a time can be pending for the same issue. An accredited representative (VSO, agent, or attorney) can help you choose. VSOs help at no cost. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104(a); 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(8); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(b), (c)","sub":"How to ask for another look","kw":"your review options your review options what you should do if you disagree with our decision how to ask for another look disagree appeal review lane one year"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#g-denied","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Why a claim was denied","text":"Each denial phrase points to one missing piece. Find the piece, and you know what evidence would matter."},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-nodx","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"No current diagnosis","text":"You may also see: The evidence does not show a current disability ; not shown by the evidence of record What it means Service connection needs a disability that exists now (or at some point while the claim was pending). VA found no diagnosis, or found symptoms alone without an underlying condition. Pain alone can count as a disability if it causes functional impairment, according to a 2018 federal court decision. A next step that may fit Evidence that would matter: a diagnosis from a treating provider, or treatment records showing the condition during the claim period. If an exam was not done, or the examiner did not test the claimed condition, see Medical opinions explained and Supplemental Claims and new exams. New evidence goes with a Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995). Law 38 U.S.C. § 1110; 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(a) Court decision Brammer v. Derwinski, 3 Vet. App. 223 (1992); McClain v. Nicholson, 21 Vet. App. 319 (2007); Saunders v. Wilkie, 886 F.3d 1356 (Fed. Cir. 2018)","sub":"Missing piece: a current condition","kw":"no current diagnosis no current diagnosis the evidence does not show a current disability not shown by the evidence of record missing piece: a current condition diagnosis current disability symptoms only pain"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-noservice","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Not shown in service","text":"You may also see: There is no record of … during military service ; The evidence does not show an event, disease, or injury in service What it means VA did not find an injury, disease, or event during service. Service treatment records are one source, but not the only one: VA must consider all lay and medical evidence, including your own statement and statements from people who served with you. A next step that may fit Evidence that would matter: buddy statements, letters home, personnel records, unit records, or your own detailed statement of what happened, when, and where. For combat veterans, VA accepts satisfactory lay evidence of an injury in combat if it fits the circumstances of that service. For conditions VA presumes, such as PACT Act conditions, the in-service piece may be met by where and when you served. See Presumptive service connection. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1154(a), (b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(a); 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(d) VA guidance What evidence does VA need?","sub":"Missing piece: something in service","kw":"not shown in service not shown in service there is no record of … during military service the evidence does not show an event, disease, or injury in service missing piece: something in service in-service event injury service treatment records buddy statement combat"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-nonexus","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"No nexus","text":"You may also see: not related to your military service ; The evidence does not show a link ; less likely than not What it means VA accepted (or did not dispute) that you have a condition and that something happened in service, but found no link between them. Usually this follows a medical opinion that said the link is \"less likely than not\" (below a 50% probability). A next step that may fit Read the examiner's rationale, not just the conclusion. An opinion that ignores your records or your statements, or gives no reasons, carries less weight. See Medical opinions explained. Evidence that would matter: a medical opinion from a provider who reviewed your history and explains why the link is at least as likely as not. That goes with a Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995). If VA relied on an opinion with a clear gap that was already visible in the file, a Higher-Level Review guide (20-0996) can look at it. Check the favorable findings first: they tell you the other pieces are already proven. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(a), (d); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4) Court decision Shedden v. Principi, 381 F.3d 1163 (Fed. Cir. 2004) (the three elements); Nieves-Rodriguez v. Peake, 22 Vet. App. 295 (2008) (weight of opinions)","sub":"Missing piece: the link to service","kw":"no nexus no nexus not related to your military service the evidence does not show a link less likely than not missing piece: the link to service nexus link medical opinion less likely than not examiner rationale"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-secondary","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"not proximately due to, the result of, or aggravated by","text":"You may also see: Service connection on a secondary basis is denied What it means You claimed the condition was caused by a condition that is already service connected. VA found neither cause nor aggravation (being made worse beyond its natural course). Both questions must be answered; an opinion that addresses only \"caused by\" is incomplete. A next step that may fit Check whether the opinion answered both caused-by and aggravated-by. If it skipped aggravation, that is a known gap a review can raise. Evidence that would matter: a medical opinion explaining how the service-connected condition caused or worsened this one. See Secondary service connection. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.310(a), (b) Court decision El-Amin v. Shinseki, 26 Vet. App. 136 (2013) (aggravation must be addressed)","sub":"Missing piece: the link to a service-connected condition","kw":"not proximately due to, the result of, or aggravated by not proximately due to, the result of, or aggravated by service connection on a secondary basis is denied missing piece: the link to a service-connected condition secondary aggravation proximately due caused by"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-preexist","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"existed prior to service","text":"You may also see: preexisting condition ; not aggravated by service What it means VA found the condition began before you entered service. If it was not noted on your entrance exam, VA presumes you were sound, and VA must show by clear and unmistakable evidence both that it existed before service and that service did not aggravate it. If it was noted, the question is whether it got worse in service beyond its natural progress. A next step that may fit Look at your entrance exam. Whether the condition was written down there changes the rules. Evidence that would matter: records or statements showing it got worse during service, and a medical opinion on aggravation. See Existed before service. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1111; 38 U.S.C. § 1153; 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.306(a), (b) Court decision Wagner v. Principi, 370 F.3d 1089 (Fed. Cir. 2004)","sub":"Before service: was it made worse?","kw":"existed prior to service existed prior to service preexisting condition not aggravated by service before service: was it made worse? preexisting sound condition entrance exam aggravation"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-notdisability","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"not a disability for VA compensation purposes","text":"You may also see: congenital or developmental defect ; refractive error What it means Some conditions are not \"diseases or injuries\" under VA's rules: congenital or developmental defects, refractive error of the eye (such as nearsightedness), and personality disorders. They cannot be service connected by themselves. A next step that may fit A disease (as opposed to a defect) that began in or was worsened by service can still be service connected, and so can a new disability added on top of a defect during service. Ask whether the letter treated your condition as a defect or a disease. Evidence that would matter: a medical opinion explaining whether the condition is a disease and whether service added to it. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(c); 38 C.F.R. § 4.9; 38 C.F.R. § 4.127 VA guidance VA General Counsel opinions 82-90 and 67-90 (defect vs. disease)","sub":"A condition the law does not compensate by itself","kw":"not a disability for va compensation purposes not a disability for va compensation purposes congenital or developmental defect refractive error a condition the law does not compensate by itself congenital developmental defect refractive error personality disorder"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-noshow","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"failed to report for a VA examination","text":"You may also see: did not attend the exam ; without good cause What it means If you miss a required exam without good cause, VA decides an original compensation claim on the evidence it has, which often leads to a denial. A claim for increase or a Supplemental Claim is denied. Good cause includes illness, hospitalization, or a death in the immediate family. A next step that may fit If you had a good reason, or never got the exam notice (for example, it went to an old address), tell VA, and say you are willing to report. Update your address on VA.gov. A Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995) can restart the claim if a new exam is what is needed. See Lesson 4: Your C&P exam. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.655(a), (b)","sub":"The exam did not happen","kw":"failed to report for a va examination failed to report for a va examination did not attend the exam without good cause the exam did not happen missed exam failure to report no show good cause address"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-notnew","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"no new and relevant evidence","text":"You may also see: insufficient evidence to readjudicate ; not new and relevant What it means On a Supplemental Claim, VA decides the claim again only if it gets evidence that is new (not already in the record) and relevant (tends to prove or disprove a matter at issue, or raises a new theory). VA found nothing that met that bar, so it did not look at the claim again on its merits. A next step that may fit Evidence that would matter: evidence aimed at the specific element the earlier denial named. A copy of a record VA already had is not new. A new theory, such as secondary or presumptive service connection, can count as relevant. See Supplemental Claims and new exams and the Supplemental Claim guide (20-0995). Law 38 U.S.C. § 5108; 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501(a), (b)","sub":"A Supplemental Claim was not reopened","kw":"no new and relevant evidence no new and relevant evidence insufficient evidence to readjudicate not new and relevant a supplemental claim was not reopened new and relevant supplemental readjudicate reopen"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-presumpt","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"The presumption does not apply","text":"You may also see: not a presumptive condition ; not within the presumptive period What it means Presumptions remove the need to prove a link, but only for listed conditions, for qualifying service, and sometimes within a set time after service. VA found one of those requirements was not met. A next step that may fit A failed presumption does not end the claim. VA must still consider direct service connection with a medical link. Check the dates and places of service in the letter against your records. See Presumptive service connection and PACT Act. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1112; 38 U.S.C. § 1116; 38 C.F.R. § 3.307; 38 C.F.R. § 3.309; 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(d) Court decision Combee v. Brown, 34 F.3d 1039 (Fed. Cir. 1994)","sub":"A presumption did not fit","kw":"the presumption does not apply the presumption does not apply not a presumptive condition not within the presumptive period a presumption did not fit presumptive pact agent orange burn pit chronic disease one year"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#g-rating","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Rating phrases","text":"What the words around your percentage mean."},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-continued","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"continued","text":"You may also see: evaluation is continued ; remains at What it means You asked for a higher rating (or VA reviewed the condition) and VA kept the same percentage. The letter should say what the next higher rating requires. A next step that may fit Compare your symptoms with the next higher rating's criteria quoted in the letter. That is the gap. Evidence that would matter: treatment records or a statement showing symptoms that match the higher criteria, including flare-ups and how the condition limits work and daily life. If the exam left out flare-ups or functional loss, see Pain and motion. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.1; 38 C.F.R. § 4.7; 38 C.F.R. § 3.103(f)(6)","sub":"Your rating stayed the same","kw":"continued continued evaluation is continued remains at your rating stayed the same continued same rating increase denied stays"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-nexthigher","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"A higher evaluation of … is not warranted unless","text":"You may also see: The next higher evaluation requires What it means This sentence quotes the rating schedule for the next percentage up. When symptoms fall between two levels, VA assigns the higher one if the picture more nearly approximates it. A next step that may fit Treat it as a checklist. Which of those symptoms do you have, and do your records show them? VA must consider all of your symptoms, not only the ones listed, for some conditions such as mental health. See Lesson 5: How ratings work. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.7; 38 C.F.R. § 4.21 Court decision Mauerhan v. Principi, 16 Vet. App. 436 (2002) (mental health symptoms are examples)","sub":"What the next level needs","kw":"a higher evaluation of … is not warranted unless a higher evaluation of … is not warranted unless the next higher evaluation requires what the next level needs next higher criteria more nearly approximates 4.7"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-staged","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"from … to …, and … from …","text":"You may also see: staged What it means VA can assign different ratings for different stretches of time when the evidence shows the condition changed. This is called a staged rating. A next step that may fit Check each date range against your records. If you were worse earlier than VA found, records from that period would matter. Court decision Fenderson v. West, 12 Vet. App. 119 (1999); Hart v. Mansfield, 21 Vet. App. 505 (2007)","sub":"Different percentages for different periods","kw":"from … to …, and … from … from … to …, and … from … staged different percentages for different periods staged ratings date ranges periods"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-combined","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Combined evaluation","text":"You may also see: Combined rating ; bilateral factor What it means VA combines ratings with a table that applies each rating to the \"remaining\" efficiency, then rounds to the nearest 10. So 50% and 30% combine to 70%, not 80%. When both arms or both legs (or paired muscles) are rated, a bilateral factor of 10% of their combined value is added first. A next step that may fit Check the math with Combine ratings. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.25; 38 C.F.R. § 4.26","sub":"Why the total is not a simple sum","kw":"combined evaluation combined evaluation combined rating bilateral factor why the total is not a simple sum combined rating table math bilateral factor 4.25"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-future","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"future examination","text":"You may also see: routine future exam ; not static What it means VA may schedule a reexamination if the condition is likely to improve. A future exam is not a reduction. A rating can only be reduced after VA proposes it and gives you notice and time to respond. A next step that may fit Keep treatment records current so a later exam reflects your whole history. If you get a proposal to reduce, deadlines are short. See Proposed reductions. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.327; 38 C.F.R. § 3.105(e), (i); 38 C.F.R. § 3.344","sub":"VA may check the condition again","kw":"future examination future examination routine future exam not static va may check the condition again future exam reexamination static permanent reduction"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-deferred","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Deferred","text":"You may also see: deferred pending What it means VA has not decided this issue yet, usually because it is waiting for an exam, an opinion, or records. A deferred issue is still pending, so there is nothing to appeal for it yet. A next step that may fit Watch for a later decision on that issue. Its review window starts from that later notice. Answer any VA request for information promptly. VA guidance M21-1, Part V, Subpart iv, Chapter 1 (rating decision format) Background \"Deferred\" is a VA decision-writing practice, not a term defined in the statute or regulations.","sub":"Not decided yet","kw":"deferred deferred deferred pending not decided yet deferred pending later decision not decided"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-pt","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"permanent and total","text":"You may also see: P&T ; no future exams What it means VA found your disability totally disabling and reasonably certain to continue for life. Some family benefits, such as Chapter 35 education and CHAMPVA, depend on this finding. A next step that may fit See Chapter 35 education and CHAMPVA. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.340(a), (b); 38 U.S.C. § 3501","sub":"Rated total and not expected to improve","kw":"permanent and total permanent and total p&t no future exams rated total and not expected to improve permanent and total p&t 100 percent family dea champva"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#g-dates","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Dates, doubt, and what comes next","text":"When payments start, how close calls are decided, and how to keep your date."},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-effdate","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Effective date","text":"You may also see: We granted … effective What it means Usually the later of the date VA received your claim (or intent to file) and the date the condition began or met the criteria. If you claimed within one year after leaving service, it can be the day after discharge. Payment starts the first day of the month after the effective date. A next step that may fit Compare the date with your intent to file, your claim, and any earlier claim that was continuously pursued. An earlier date is worth asking about if VA overlooked an earlier filing. See Effective dates and the Continuous Pursuit Review tool. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5110(a), (b)(1); 38 U.S.C. § 5111(a); 38 C.F.R. § 3.400(b)(2)(i); 38 C.F.R. § 3.155(b)","sub":"The date benefits start counting from","kw":"effective date effective date we granted … effective the date benefits start counting from effective date start date intent to file back pay retro"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-arose","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"date entitlement arose","text":"You may also see: the date the evidence first showed What it means If the evidence shows the condition met the criteria only after your claim arrived, VA uses that later date. For an increase, VA can go back up to one year before the claim if records show the increase happened then. A next step that may fit Records from before the date VA picked, showing the same symptoms, would matter. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5110(b)(3); 38 C.F.R. § 3.400(o)(2)","sub":"Why VA picked a date later than your claim","kw":"date entitlement arose date entitlement arose the date the evidence first showed why va picked a date later than your claim entitlement arose later date increase one year"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-doubt","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"benefit of the doubt","text":"You may also see: reasonable doubt ; approximate balance What it means When the evidence for and against an issue is in approximate balance, VA must resolve the doubt in your favor. It does not apply when the evidence clearly weighs against the claim. A next step that may fit If the letter says the evidence was against you, look for whether it explains why the opposing evidence carried more weight. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5107(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.102 Court decision Gilbert v. Derwinski, 1 Vet. App. 49 (1990); Lynch v. McDonough, 21 F.4th 776 (Fed. Cir. 2021) (en banc)","sub":"Ties go to the veteran","kw":"benefit of the doubt benefit of the doubt reasonable doubt approximate balance ties go to the veteran benefit of doubt reasonable doubt equipoise balance"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#p-final","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"This decision becomes final","text":"You may also see: within one year from the date of this letter What it means You generally have one year from the date of the notice to file a Supplemental Claim, Higher-Level Review, or Board appeal and keep the original effective date. After that, a Supplemental Claim can still be filed, but the effective date generally can be no earlier than when VA receives it. A next step that may fit Write the deadline down the day the letter arrives. Use the date on the letter, not the date you opened it. Track your filings with the Continuous Pursuit Review tool. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(a), (h)(1)–(2); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501","sub":"Your window to ask for review","kw":"this decision becomes final this decision becomes final within one year from the date of this letter your window to ask for review one year deadline final continuous pursuit"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#which","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"Which review fits what you found","text":"These match the problem to the option. They don't predict a result. An accredited representative can help you choose, and VSOs help at no cost. What you found Option that fits Guide You have new evidence, such as a medical opinion, records VA didn't have, or a new theory Supplemental Claim 20-0995 guide VA misread or overlooked evidence it already had, or applied the law wrong Higher-Level Review (no new evidence) 20-0996 guide You want a Veterans Law Judge to decide, with or without new evidence or a hearing Board appeal 10182 guide The decision is more than a year old Supplemental Claim; the effective date generally starts later Late options A claimed condition got worse since the decision A new claim for increase When it got worse Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(b), (c), (h); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501; 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601; 38 C.F.R. § 20.202 Go deeper: Lesson 6: You got a decision · Reading your rating decision · Does your decision cover everything? · Continuous Pursuit Review"},{"href":"decision-letter.html#mistakes","page":"Decode Your Decision Letter","kind":"Guide","title":"What often goes wrong after a decision","text":"Reading only the first page. The percentage is on page one, but the reasons and favorable findings, which tell you what is missing, come later. Sending the same records again. A copy of what VA already had is not new evidence. Aim new evidence at the element the denial named. Ignoring the favorable findings. They are already decided in your favor. Building evidence for something already proven wastes time. Missing the one-year window. Filing within a year of the notice generally keeps your original effective date. Treating \"deferred\" as \"denied.\" A deferred issue is still pending. Wait for its decision. Paying someone to file a first claim. It is illegal to charge for help preparing an initial claim. Accredited VSOs help for free at every stage. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5904(c)(1); 38 C.F.R. § 3.104(c); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501"},{"href":"doctor-guide.html","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"What VA asks your examiner","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to what VA's public instructions ask C&P examiners to cover: medication effects on joint exams, rating increases, Gulf War illness patterns, digestive conditions, and changed rating rules. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"For some kinds of claims, VA adds extra questions to the exam request it sends to the examiner. VA publishes that wording. This guide explains it in plain English, so you know what the examiner is asked to cover, what to bring, and what to look for in the report. You can also share it with your own doctor. Based on public VA guidance: the Supplemental Language Matrix (KnowVA, updated Apr 9, 2026 ) and M21-1, Part IV, Subpart i, Chapter 2, Section A (updated Aug 18, 2026 ). ClaimReady checks both for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save for your doctor"},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#basics","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"Three things to know first","text":"The exam gathers evidence. It is not treatment. VA also does not order an exam just to make a diagnosis that would need sedation or an invasive procedure, such as a biopsy. If that kind of diagnosis is needed, records from your own treatment matter. Who examines you depends on the condition. Hearing, vision, dental, and psychiatric exams must be done by a specialist. Most other exams are done by a clinician who is not a specialist, using the form (DBQ) for the claimed condition. You have no legal right to bring a lawyer into the exam or to record it. For your rights, exam day, and what a complete report needs, see C&P exams: the complete guide. VA guidance M21-1 IV.i.2.A.1.d–h"},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#meds","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"If medicine helps: how bad is it without it?","text":"When you take medicine for a joint, spine, muscle, bone, or jaw condition, VA asks the examiner to estimate how severe the condition would be without the medicine (VA calls this the \"unmedicated state\"), when that can be done without guessing. Amputations Ankle Non-degenerative arthritis and decompression illness Back (thoracolumbar spine) Bones and other skeletal conditions Elbow and forearm Foot, including flatfoot Hand and fingers Hip and thigh Knee and lower leg Muscle injuries Neck (cervical spine) Osteomyelitis Shoulder and arm Jaw (TMD) Wrist The questions the examiner is asked Do you take any medicine for this condition? Prescription or over the counter. If no, the questions stop here. Does it relieve or reduce your symptoms? If no, the questions stop here. Can the examiner estimate the severity as if you were not taking it, without resorting to mere speculation? If no, the examiner must explain why in the report. VA's wording says the reason should not be the examiner's own shortcomings or a general reluctance to estimate something not seen in the room. If yes, which kinds of functional loss would be worse, and by how much? The examiner checks each that applies and estimates the added loss, ideally as added loss of motion in degrees: Pain Active range of motion Passive range of motion Repetitive use Repeated use over time Flare-ups Ankylosis (a joint fixed in place) Flail joints Other factors, such as trouble standing or sitting, walking problems, weakness, instability, swelling, deformity, or muscle wasting from disuse Nerve symptoms from the spine are different. VA is not required to consider nerve problems from the spine, such as radiculopathy, in an unmedicated state, so the examiner skips these questions for them on the neck and back forms. Fictional example Dana takes a daily prescription anti-inflammatory for a service-connected knee. At the exam, Dana explains that it takes the edge off the pain, and that on days a dose was missed, Dana could not kneel or handle stairs. That history gives the examiner something concrete to base an estimate on, or to explain why an estimate is not possible. This is an illustration, not a VA decision. Before your exam Bring a list of every medicine you take for the condition, prescription and over the counter, with the dose and how often. Be ready to say how much each one helps, and what the condition is like "},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#increase","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"If your condition got worse, or got a new name","text":"For an increase, the examiner is asked to evaluate how severe your service-connected condition is now. Sometimes the examiner's diagnosis is different from the one VA service connected. VA's wording then asks the examiner to say which of these is true: The new diagnosis is a progression of the service-connected condition. For example, a condition that developed or changed over time. The original diagnosis was in error. Only with a rationale, supported by the clinical evidence in the record, that refutes the earlier exam or exams that made the diagnosis. \"If the diagnosis was in error, please provide a rationale supported by the clinical evidence of record that refutes the previous exam(s) which diagnosed the condition.\" Before your exam Be ready to describe what has changed since your last rating: new symptoms, more frequent flare-ups, more treatment, work or daily tasks you can no longer do. Bring or submit recent treatment records, especially any that use a different name for the condition. If the report calls the old diagnosis an error, check that it explains why, using the record. For your own doctor Describe current severity in the terms of the matching exam form. If your diagnosis differs from the rated one, say whether it is a progression of that condition, and why. VA guidance Supplemental Language Matrix, examination for increase"},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#gulf","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"Symptoms without a clear cause","text":"VA's laws allow service connection for certain chronic disability patterns linked to environmental hazards in Southwest Asia. VA gives the examiner definitions so the report can say which pattern fits. Smoke and particles from oil well fires Pesticides and insecticides Infectious diseases found in the region Solvent and fuel fumes Pyridostigmine bromide tablets, taken as a nerve gas antidote The combined effect of multiple vaccines given at deployment Very fine sand particles Smoke and particles from burn pits The four patterns Undiagnosed illness. Findings that cannot be attributed to a known, clearly defined diagnosis after likely diagnoses have been ruled out. Diagnosed but medically unexplained chronic multisymptom illness (MUCMI). A diagnosis with no conclusive cause or mechanism, with overlapping symptoms such as fatigue, pain, disability out of proportion to physical findings, and inconsistent lab results. Examples include chronic fatigue syndrome, fibromyalgia, and irritable bowel syndrome. Chronic multisymptom illness with a partially explained cause. VA's examples: diabetes and multiple sclerosis. These are not MUCMIs. A disease with a clear and specific cause and diagnosis. Patterns 1 and 2 (shaded) are the ones the Gulf War presumption covers. Patterns 3 and 4 can still be service connected in other ways, such as direct service connection or another presumption. VA decides which pattern applies based on the whole record. Chronic means Lasting 6 months or more, or coming and going over a 6-month period. The 6 months count from the earliest date the evidence shows the symptoms began. Objective indications Signs an examiner can observe, and also non-medical indicators that can be independently verified. Signs and symptoms that may qualify Fatigue Skin Headache Muscle pain Joint pain Neurological Neuropsychological Upper or lower respiratory Sleep disturbances Gastrointestinal Cardiovascular Abnormal weight loss Menstrual disorders Still in service? For claims filed before discharge (Benefits Delivery at Discharge or the Integrated Disability Evaluation System), VA's wording asks the examiner for a medical statement, with rationale, placing the disability pattern in one of the four groups. For pattern 1 or 2, the examiner identifies which signs and symptoms belong to each illness. For pattern 3 or 4, no further opinion is requested. Before your exam "},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#digest","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"Functional or structural?","text":"VA asks examiners to tell apart two kinds of digestive conditions. For Gulf War claims, it matters because functional disorders can be MUCMIs and structural diseases cannot. Functional Chronic or recurring symptoms that are not explained by structural, endoscopic, lab, or other objective signs of injury or disease. Examples: irritable bowel syndrome, functional dyspepsia, functional vomiting, functional constipation, functional bloating, functional abdominal pain syndrome, and functional dysphagia. Structural A disease that testing does show, for example on imaging, endoscopy, or lab work. VA's Gulf War guidance names GERD, ulcerative colitis, and Crohn's disease as structural (M21-1 VIII.ii.1.A.1.h). Common symptoms of functional disorders include abdominal pain, burning or pain behind the breastbone, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea or constipation, indigestion, bloating, feeling full after eating, and painful or difficult swallowing. Diagnosis generally requires symptoms that began at least 6 months before diagnosis and were enough to diagnose the disorder at least 3 months before. When a digestive disorder is found in a pre-discharge Gulf War exam, the examiner is asked to explain whether it is functional or structural, and to discuss the testing used to identify it. Before your exam Write down when your symptoms started and how often they happen. Bring results of any scopes, imaging, or lab work, including tests that came back normal. For your own doctor Say whether the disorder is functional or structural, and which tests support that. VA also has separate added wording for every digestive system exam form, published as an attachment to the matrix. This guide does not cover that attachment yet. VA guidance Supplemental Language Matrix, Gulf War definitions and pre-discharge row Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.317 · Digestive conditions"},{"href":"doctor-guide.html#revised","page":"What VA Asks Your Examiner","kind":"Guide","title":"If VA changed the rating criteria while your claim was pending","text":"VA sometimes rewrites the criteria for a group of conditions. When that happens while an initial or increased rating claim is pending, VA's guidance says the exam request for affected conditions should include wording for the old (historical) criteria too, and VA must consider the old criteria and the related exam results when it decides. This also applies when the claim came in after the change, but an intent to file submitted before the change was still active. Check your exam report: it should give the findings each version of the criteria needs. Example: knee and hip replacement ratings changed on February 7, 2021. The Temporary 100% Timeline shows both versions. VA guidance M21-1 IV.i.2.A.1.j · How VA assigns a rating"},{"href":"form-0995.html","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 20-0995, Supplemental Claim, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 20-0995, Supplemental Claim (May 2024 edition), section by section: timing and effective dates, new and relevant evidence, the duty to assist, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 20-0995 asks VA to look at a decided issue again because you have new and relevant evidence. This guide explains what each section asks for and why it matters under public law. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained MAY 2024 Replaces SEP 2022 OMB number 2900-0886, exp. 05/31/2027 Length Instructions, then 29 items VA's form page listed May 2024 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . Always get the form from VA's form page or file online at VA.gov. For the law on what new evidence does, read ClaimReady's Supplemental Claims guide. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-0995.html#date","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Timing and your date","text":"Within one year If you file within one year of the decision notice, and keep filing the next review within a year of each decision, VA treats the issue as continuously pursued. A grant can then go back to your original claim date, or when entitlement arose if later. ClaimReady: Continuous Pursuit Review After one year You can still file, but the effective date is generally no earlier than the date VA receives the Supplemental Claim. One lane at a time. While one review of an issue is pending, you can't file a different review of that same issue. Each issue has its own year. Issues you don't list on this form still have one year from the decision notice for their own Supplemental Claim. One benefit type per form. Use a separate form for each benefit type, such as compensation and pension. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(b), (c), (h)(1)–(2) VA form 20-0995 instructions, Item 1 and Section IV"},{"href":"form-0995.html#summary","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 20-0995 instructions say","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. When to use it You disagree with a VA decision and have new and relevant evidence. Worse condition: use 21-526EZ. Other choices: 20-0996 Higher-Level Review, 10182 Board appeal. 2. Where to send it To the intake center for your benefit type. VA recommends filing electronically. One benefit type per form. 3. Help An accredited representative, or VA at 1-800-827-1000. You can ask for a hearing at any time. Attorneys and agents generally may charge only after VA's initial decision. 4. Issues (Section IV) List each issue and the date of the decision notice. Only listed issues are reviewed. Unlisted issues keep their own one-year window. Within a year of a court decision: list that date and attach a copy. 5. Evidence (Section V) Send new and relevant evidence, or identify records for VA to get. VA medical center, VA clinic, and military treatment records need no consent form. Private and Vet Center records: 21-4142 and 21-4142a. A change in law needs no new evidence. 6. Notice and signature Sign Item 25A. Witnesses only if you sign with an \"X.\" Check Yes in Item 23 (5103 notice) after reviewing VA's evidence notice. Required more than a year after the decision for compensation, pension, DIC, and accrued claims. \"No\" means VA mails the notice first, which causes delay. VA form VA Form 20-0995 (May 2024), instructions, summarized in plain language. The form's own wording controls."},{"href":"form-0995.html#check","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What a reviewer checks first","text":"The first two matter most. A problem with either one stops or delays the review. 1. It is signed and dated (Item 25). An unsigned form is not a complete claim. Section VIII 2. The 5103 notice box in Section VI is checked \"Yes\" (Item 23). Checking it confirms you reviewed VA's notice of the evidence your claim needs, so VA does not have to send that notice first. Leaving it blank, or checking \"No,\" means VA mails the notice before it moves forward, which delays the review. The form requires it for compensation, pension, DIC, and accrued claims filed more than a year after the decision. Section VI Also check One benefit type is checked in Item 1. Item 1 Each issue is listed with its decision date in Item 21, copied from the decision letter. Section IV New and relevant evidence is sent or identified. Section V VA treatment is listed; private treatment has a 21-4142. Item 22 VA form 20-0995 instructions and Items 1, 21–25"},{"href":"form-0995.html#map","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The sections at a glance","text":"1 Benefit type Item 1 I–II Veteran and claimant information Items 2–19 III Homeless information Item 20 IV Issues for review Item 21, the heart of the form V New and relevant evidence Item 22 VI Evidence notice acknowledgment Item 23 VII Health care notification option Item 24 VIII–XI Signatures Items 25–29 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, or insurance policy number. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-0995.html#s1","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Benefit type","text":"What it asks Which benefit the decision was about: compensation; pension, DIC, or survivors benefits; fiduciary; education; loan guaranty; life insurance; Veteran Readiness and Employment; National Cemetery Administration; or Veterans Health Administration. Why it matters Each benefit type goes to a different intake center. Check only one box, and use a separate form for another benefit type. VA form 20-0995 instructions, Item 1 and \"Where to Submit This Form\"","sub":"Section 1 · Item 1: check only one"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s2","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Veteran and claimant information","text":"What it asks Section I is the veteran's information. Section II is only for a claimant who is not the veteran, such as a surviving spouse, child, parent, or fiduciary. Why it matters The instructions say that a last name with a Social Security number or VA file number lets VA identify you even if other fields are blank. Keep your mailing address current: VA may schedule an exam or ask for records by mail. VA form 20-0995 instructions, Sections I and II","sub":"Section I–II · Items 2–19"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s3","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Homeless information","text":"Why it matters It gives VA a point of contact who can reach you. The options include living somewhere not meant for sleeping, a shelter, staying with friends or family, or losing your home or a facility placement in the next 30 days. Skip to Section IV if none applies. VA form 20-0995 Item 20","sub":"Section III · Item 20: only if you are homeless or at risk"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s4","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Issues for review","text":"What it asks Each issue VA decided that you want reviewed (21A), and the date of the VA decision notice for it (21B). Why it matters VA reviews only the issues you list. Copy each issue the way the decision letter states it, such as \"Service connection for sleep apnea\" or \"Evaluation of PTSD,\" so VA can match it. The decision date tells VA whether you filed within a year. Watch for An issue left off still has its own year from the decision notice, but filing it later means another form. Within a year of a decision from the Court of Appeals for Veterans Claims or a higher court, list the court decision date and attach a copy. For an older (legacy) appeal, the instructions describe a 60-day window after a Statement of the Case to opt in. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h) VA form 20-0995 instructions, Section IV","sub":"Section IV · Item 21: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s5","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"New and relevant evidence","text":"What counts New means it was not part of the record VA had before. Relevant means it tends to prove or disprove something at issue, including a new theory of how the condition is connected. If you don't send or identify any, VA issues a decision saying there was not enough evidence to readjudicate. What Item 22 asks Where you were treated: private providers, Vet Centers, VA-paid community care, VA medical centers and clinics, military treatment facilities, or other. Give the name and location and approximate dates, or check the box if you don't have dates. VA's duty to assist A substantially complete Supplemental Claim triggers VA's duty to help get the evidence. VA can get VA medical center, VA clinic, and military treatment records without a consent form. For private and Vet Center records, sign VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a, or send the records yourself. If a record holder refuses or charges a fee, VA will tell you. For how new evidence can lead to a new exam, read ClaimReady's Supplemental Claims guide. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.2501, 3.159(c) VA form 20-0995 instructions, Section V and Item 22","sub":"Section V · Item 22: where VA should look"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s6","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Evidence notice acknowledgment","text":"What it asks For compensation, pension, DIC, and accrued benefits filed more than one year after the decision notice: review VA's online notice of the evidence needed, then check Yes in Item 23. If you can't review it online, check No and VA will mail it to you. Why it matters VA must tell you what evidence is needed to support a claim. This box is how you confirm you got that notice. If it is left blank or answered \"No,\" VA mails the notice first, which delays the review. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103 VA form 20-0995 Section VI","sub":"Section VI · Item 23: check \"Yes\" to avoid delay"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s7","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Health care notification option","text":"What it asks If your claim is for a condition from a personal traumatic event, such as military sexual trauma, and you are enrolled in VA health care, you can let VA's benefits side tell VA health care about upcoming exams, Board hearings, and and decision notices. You can consent, decline, revoke an earlier consent, or mark it not applicable. Why it matters An answer isn't required, and the form says your choice does not affect the outcome of the claim. VA form 20-0995 Section VII","sub":"Section VII · Item 24: claims tied to a personal traumatic event"},{"href":"form-0995.html#s8","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Signatures","text":"Section VIII: your signature You certify the statements are true and correct to the best of your knowledge and belief. An unsigned form isn't a complete claim. Sections IX–XI Witnesses: only if you sign with an \"X.\" Alternate signer: only if Item 25A is blank, with VA Form 21-0972 on file. Representative: needs VA Form 21-22 or 21-22a on file, and asks for the accreditation number and when that form was submitted. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.160(a)(2), 3.2130 VA form 20-0995 Sections VIII–XI","sub":"Section VIII–XI · Items 25–29: you, witnesses, alternate signer, representative"},{"href":"form-0995.html#mistakes","page":"Form 20-0995 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Not signing, or skipping the 5103 notice box. These are the two most common causes of delay. Sign Item 25 and check \"Yes\" in Item 23. Sending the same evidence again. Evidence already in the record isn't new. Point to something VA didn't have, or a theory it didn't address. Using this form for a condition that got worse. A worsening is a claim for increase on the 21-526EZ. Leaving off the decision date. VA uses it to match the issue and to see whether you're within the year. Filing a Higher-Level Review and a Supplemental Claim on the same issue at once. Only one review of an issue can be pending at a time. Missing the one-year window. Filing within a year keeps the earliest effective date; after that, the date is generally when VA receives the form. Not signing a 21-4142 for private records. VA needs your consent to request them, or you can send them yourself."},{"href":"form-0996.html","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 20-0996, Higher-Level Review, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 20-0996, Higher-Level Review (March 2024 edition), section by section: how the review works, the informal conference, the one-year deadline, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 20-0996 asks a more experienced VA reviewer to take a fresh look at a decision using the same evidence. You can't add new evidence, but you can point out errors, including in an optional informal conference. This guide explains what each section asks for and why it matters under public law. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained MAR 2024 Replaces SEP 2022 OMB number 2900-0862, exp. 03/31/2027 Length Instructions, then 20 items VA's form page listed March 2024 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . Always get the form from VA's form page or file online at VA.gov. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-0996.html#how","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"How a Higher-Level Review works","text":"What the reviewer does An experienced adjudicator who took no part in the prior decision reviews it fresh, giving it no deference. Favorable findings already made still stand. Usually at a different office. You can ask for the same office, and VA will agree unless it has good cause not to. Can grant based on a difference of opinion, but won't make the outcome worse on that basis alone. What the reviewer can't do Consider evidence received after the decision notice. Gather new evidence, except to fix a duty-to-assist error. If VA missed a step it owed you, such as getting a record or an adequate exam, and can't grant the full benefit, the reviewer sends the claim back to correct the error and decide again. One year. VA must receive the form within one year of the decision notice. Filing on time keeps the issue continuously pursued and protects your effective date. One lane at a time. You can't have a Supplemental Claim and a Higher-Level Review pending on the same issue. Incomplete form? VA tells you what's missing. A complete form within 60 days of that notice, or before the year ends, keeps the original date. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.2500(b), (h)(1); 3.2601(d)–(g), (i)–(j); 3.104(c)"},{"href":"form-0996.html#summary","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 20-0996 instructions say","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. When to use it To ask for a Higher-Level Review of a decision you received. One benefit type per form. 2. Deadline VA must receive it within one year of the date of the decision notice. Responding to an older Statement of the Case: within 60 days, which withdraws those issues from the legacy system. 3. Where to send it To the office or processing center named on your decision letter, or online at VA.gov. Keep a copy of everything you send. 4. Informal conference Optional. One per review. To point out errors of fact or law, not to add evidence. Check 16A and a morning or afternoon preference in 16B. VA makes two attempts to call. Want VA to call your representative? Give their name and phone in Item 17. A written statement of the errors is an alternative that can avoid delay. 5. Issues List each issue (18A) and the decision letter date (18B). Both are required. Only listed issues are reviewed. No evidence received after the decision notice is considered. 6. Signatures Sign Item 19A (not in pencil). Alternate signer: attach VA Form 21-0972. Representative: VA Form 21-22 or 21-22a must be on file or attached. VA form VA Form 20-0996 (Mar 2024), instructions, summarized in plain language. The form's own wording controls."},{"href":"form-0996.html#check","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What a reviewer checks first","text":"The form marks these as required. A problem with either one stops or delays the review. 1. It is signed and dated (Item 19). A request must be signed by you or someone authorized to sign for you to be complete. Section VI 2. Every issue is listed with its decision letter date (Items 18A and 18B). The reviewer looks only at the issues you list, and the date shows the request is within one year. Section V Also check One benefit type is checked in Item 15. Section III It reaches VA within one year of the decision notice. If you asked for an informal conference, a contact preference is checked and your representative's phone is listed if VA should call them. Section IV No new evidence is attached. It won't be considered here; use a Supplemental Claim for that. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601(c)–(d) VA form 20-0996 Items 15–19"},{"href":"form-0996.html#map","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The sections at a glance","text":"I–II Veteran and claimant information Items 1–14 III Benefit type Item 15 IV Optional informal conference Items 16–17 V Issues for review Item 18, the heart of the form VI–VII Signatures Items 19–20 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, or insurance policy number. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-0996.html#s1","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Veteran and claimant information","text":"What it asks Section I is the veteran's information. Section II is only for a claimant who is not the veteran, such as a surviving spouse. There is also a box to tell VA if you are homeless. Why it matters A complete request must identify the claimant and their relationship to the veteran. A current phone number matters if you ask for an informal conference: VA makes two attempts to reach you. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601(c) VA form 20-0996 Sections I–II","sub":"Section I–II · Items 1–14"},{"href":"form-0996.html#s3","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Benefit type","text":"Why it matters Each form covers one benefit type, such as compensation or pension. Use a separate form for each benefit type you want reviewed. VA form 20-0996 Item 15","sub":"Section III · Item 15: select only one"},{"href":"form-0996.html#s4","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Optional informal conference","text":"What it is A phone call in which you or your representative explain to the reviewer the errors of fact or law you see in the decision. VA holds only one per request and won't accept new evidence or new facts during it. What it asks 16A: check the box if you want one. 16B: pick one contact preference, mornings or afternoons in your time zone, for you or your representative. VA makes two attempts to reach the person chosen. 17: if VA should call your representative, give their name, phone, and email. Worth knowing The instructions say a written statement explaining the errors is an alternative that can avoid delays from scheduling a call. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601(h) VA form 20-0996 Section IV","sub":"Section IV · Items 16–17"},{"href":"form-0996.html#s5","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Issues for review","text":"What it asks Each specific issue you disagree with (18A) and the date of the VA decision notification letter (18B). The form marks both required. Why it matters Only the issues you list are reviewed. Copy each issue the way the decision letter states it, such as \"Evaluation of left knee condition\" or \"Service connection for tinnitus.\" The date shows the request is within one year. Watch for If you're responding to a legacy Statement of the Case, you have 60 days, and filing this form withdraws those issues from the legacy appeal. Describe the error, if you can, in a short statement. It helps the reviewer see what you think went wrong. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601(c), (i) VA form 20-0996 Section V","sub":"Section V · Item 18: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-0996.html#s6","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Signatures","text":"Section VI: your signature Sign and date Item 19. The instructions ask you not to sign in pencil. A request must be signed by the claimant or a person authorized to sign for them. Section VII: representative A representative can sign only if VA Form 21-22 or 21-22a is already on file or attached. If someone else signs for a claimant who can't, include VA Form 21-0972, Alternate Signer Certification. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2601(c) VA form 20-0996 Sections VI–VII","sub":"Section VI–VII · Items 19–20"},{"href":"form-0996.html#mistakes","page":"Form 20-0996 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Not signing, or leaving out an issue's decision date. Both are required for a complete request. Attaching new evidence. The reviewer can't consider it. If the new evidence is the point, file a Supplemental Claim instead. Bringing new facts to the informal conference. The conference is for pointing out errors in the decision as it stands. Missing the one-year deadline. After a year, a Higher-Level Review isn't available for that decision. Filing a Supplemental Claim on the same issue at the same time. Only one review of an issue can be pending at once. Worrying the review will lower your rating. The reviewer won't change the outcome to your disadvantage based only on a difference of opinion, and favorable findings stand. A clear and unmistakable error is handled under a separate rule. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.2500(b), 3.2601(c), (f), (h), (j); 3.104(c); 3.105(a)"},{"href":"form-10182.html","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 10182, Board Appeal, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 10182, Board Appeal (Notice of Disagreement), June 2026 edition, section by section: the three dockets, the one-year deadline, evidence windows, changing dockets, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 10182, the Notice of Disagreement, asks the Board of Veterans' Appeals to have a Veterans Law Judge decide your appeal. On it you pick one of three dockets: direct review, evidence submission, or a hearing. That choice decides what evidence the judge sees and how long you may wait. This guide explains what each part asks for and why it matters under public law. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained JUN 2026 Title Decision Review Request: Board Appeal (Notice of Disagreement) OMB number 2900-0674, exp. 05/31/2029 Length 1 page, 13 items, then 2 pages of instructions VA's form page listed June 2026 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 25, 2026 . Always get the form from VA's form page or file online at VA.gov. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-10182.html#dockets","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The three dockets","text":"Part II asks you to pick one. Each docket is its own line, and appeals are generally decided in the order they are docketed. The three Board dockets compared Docket (Part II) What the judge sees Your evidence window VA's goal 10A Direct review What the judge sees: Only the evidence VA had when it decided. No hearing. Evidence window: None VA's goal: 365 days (1 year) 10B Evidence submission What the judge sees: That record plus new evidence you send. No hearing. Evidence window: With the form, or within 90 days after the Board receives it VA's goal: 550 days (1.5 years) 10C Hearing What the judge sees: That record plus your testimony and new evidence. Evidence window: At the hearing, or within 90 days after it VA's goal: 730 days (2 years) The docket sets the record. On direct review, the Board generally won't consider evidence you send later. Evidence sent outside a 90-day window isn't part of the record for that appeal. Hearing changes. If you withdraw your hearing request, you have 90 days from the Board's receipt of the withdrawal to send evidence. If you miss the hearing and it isn't rescheduled, you have 90 days from the scheduled date. The goals are goals. VA.gov lists them as targets, not promised wait times. Remands. If the judge finds VA missed a duty-to-assist step, such as a record or an adequate exam, and can't grant the benefit, the Board remands it to VA to fix the error and decide again. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 20.202(b), (c)(1); 20.302(a)–(c); 20.303; 20.800(a)–(b); 20.802 VA guidance VA.gov Board Appeal page (goals)"},{"href":"form-10182.html#summary","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 10182 instructions say","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. When to use it For VA decisions dated on or after February 19, 2019. Not for a Supplemental Claim (20-0995) or Higher-Level Review (20-0996). 2. Deadline Generally within one year of the date VA mailed the decision. After a legacy SOC or SSOC: 60 days from that letter or one year from the original decision, whichever is later. Contested claim: 60 days. 3. Where to send it Board of Veterans' Appeals, P.O. Box 27063, Washington, DC 20038. Or online at VA.gov, or in person at a VA regional office. 4. Pick one docket Check only one of 10A, 10B, or 10C. Different dockets for different issues? Use a separate form for each. Hearing: pick Central Office, videoconference, or virtual telehearing. 5. Issues List each issue (11A) and the date of VA's decision (11B). Extra sheets: put the veteran's last name and file number on each. An unclear form: the Board contacts you to clarify. 6. Changing docket and signing Change docket with a new 10182 within one year of the decision or 60 days of Board receipt, whichever is later, and before sending evidence or testimony. Sign Item 12 in ink. An appointed representative may sign. VA form VA Form 10182 (Jun 2026), instructions, summarized in plain language. The form's own wording controls."},{"href":"form-10182.html#check","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the Board checks first","text":"A problem with any of these can delay the appeal or leave an issue out of it. 1. It is signed and dated (Items 12 and 13). Sign in ink: you, or your appointed representative. Part IV 2. Every issue is listed with its decision date (Item 11). The Board may dismiss an appeal that doesn't identify the decision and the issues. The date shows the appeal is on time. Part III 3. Exactly one docket is checked (Item 10). If the Board can't tell which docket you chose, it asks you to clarify, and the clock keeps running. Part II Also check It reaches the Board within one year of the decision letter (60 days for a contested claim). Hearing request? One hearing type is checked, and for a virtual telehearing your email and representative are in Part I. Part II Evidence docket? Your new evidence is attached or calendared to arrive within 90 days. Your mailing address, phone, and email are current, so Board letters and hearing notices reach you. Part I Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 20.202(a), (f)–(g); 20.203(b) VA form 10182 Items 6–13"},{"href":"form-10182.html#map","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The parts at a glance","text":"I Personal information Items 1–9 II Board review option Item 10: pick one docket III Specific issues to be appealed Item 11, the heart of the form IV Certification and signature Items 12–13 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number or VA file number. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-10182.html#s1","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Personal information","text":"What it asks The veteran's name, file number, and date of birth (Items 1–3). If you're not the veteran, such as a surviving spouse, your own name and date of birth (4–5). Then your mailing address, with a box if you're experiencing homelessness, phone, email, and your representative's name (6–9). Why it matters The Board writes to you here: docket letters, hearing notices, and its decision. A virtual telehearing needs the email address and representative in this part. VA form 10182 Part I","sub":"Section I · Items 1–9"},{"href":"form-10182.html#s2","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Board review option","text":"What it asks 10A Direct review: no hearing, no new evidence. The form says it is often the fastest. 10B Evidence submission: send evidence with the form or within 90 days after the Board receives it. The form notes this may lengthen the wait. 10C Hearing: testify before a judge and send evidence within 90 days after. Pick one type: Central Office (in person in Washington, DC, at your own travel cost), videoconference (at a VA location), or virtual telehearing (from your own internet-connected device). Why it matters Your choice sets both the evidence the judge can consider and the line you wait in. Think about what you need: if VA misread the evidence it had, direct review fits. If the denial was for missing evidence, such as a medical opinion, you need a docket that lets you send it, or a Supplemental Claim. Changing your mind File a new 10182 with the new docket within one year of the decision or 60 days after the Board received the first one, whichever is later. You can't change once you've sent evidence or testimony for that appeal. A moved appeal keeps its original docket date. Hearings One hearing per appeal. It is informal, with no cross-examination, and is transcribed for your file. Need a new date? Ask in writing with good cause at least two weeks before. Missed it without a postponement? It is treated as withdrawn. You have 15 days to file a written motion showing good cause. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 20.202(b)–(c); 20.302; 20.303; 20.700; 20.704; 20.800(a)(2) VA form 10182 Part II","sub":"Section II · Item 10: check only one"},{"href":"form-10182.html#s3","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Specific issues to be appealed","text":"What it asks Each specific issue you disagree with (11A) and the date of VA's decision on it (11B). Column C and attached sheets hold additional issues; put the veteran's last name and file number on each sheet. Why it matters Only the issues you list are appealed. Copy each issue as the decision letter states it, such as \"Evaluation of left knee condition\" or \"Service connection for tinnitus.\" The Board reads the form liberally, but it may dismiss an appeal that doesn't identify the decision and issues. The three boxes Decision as soon as possible: asks the Board to decide even while you could still change dockets. You give up the chance to change once it decides. The form says it does not change your place in line. Extension of time: for a late appeal, check this and attach your good-cause explanation. The Board decides whether good cause exists. Additional sheets attached. If the Board can't tell If it can't tell which issues or which docket you chose, the Board contacts you. Answer within 60 days of its letter or one year of the decision, whichever is later, or those issues are not appealed. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 20.202(a), (f)–(h); 20.203(c) VA form 10182 Part III","sub":"Section III · Item 11: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-10182.html#s4","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Certification and signature","text":"What it asks Your ink signature (Item 12) and the date (Item 13), certifying the statements are true and correct to the best of your knowledge and belief. An appointed representative may sign for you. Worth knowing You have the right to an accredited representative at every stage: a Veterans Service Organization, claims agent, or attorney. VSOs help at no cost. The form points to VA's accreditation search at va.gov/ogc/accreditation.asp. Law 38 C.F.R. § 20.6 VA form 10182 Part IV and instructions","sub":"Section IV · Items 12–13"},{"href":"form-10182.html#after","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What happens next","text":"A docket letter. If the Board accepts the appeal, it writes to confirm it is on the docket. Unless the Board asks for something, you don't need to do anything while you wait. Check status at VA.gov. Your effective date is protected. A Board appeal filed within one year keeps the issue continuously pursued. So does a Supplemental Claim filed within one year after the Board decides. One lane at a time. While the appeal is pending, you can't file a Supplemental Claim or Higher-Level Review on the same issue. Moving up the line. If you're 75 or older, seriously ill, or in severe financial hardship, you or your representative can ask in writing to advance the appeal. Give the reason, the veteran's name, and the VA file number. The Board doesn't advance a case unless someone asks. Withdrawing. You can withdraw in writing before the Board decides, and still file another review if it would be on time. After the decision. Appeal to the U.S. Court of Appeals for Veterans Claims within 120 days, or file a Supplemental Claim with new and relevant evidence. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.2500(b), (c)(3), (h)(1); 20.205; 20.800(c) VA guidance VA.gov Board Appeal page"},{"href":"form-10182.html#mistakes","page":"Form 10182 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Choosing direct review when the denial was for missing evidence. The judge sees only what VA had. If you need a new medical opinion or records in the file, pick evidence submission or a hearing, or consider a Supplemental Claim. Picking the evidence docket, then sending evidence late. Evidence that arrives after the 90-day window isn't part of the record for that appeal. Send it with the form if it's ready. Writing a letter instead of using the form. The Board accepts only VA Form 10182. A letter or a different VA form isn't a Notice of Disagreement and doesn't stop the one-year clock. Leaving out an issue or its decision date. Only listed issues are appealed, and a missing date makes timeliness hard to confirm. Trying to switch dockets after sending evidence. Once you've sent evidence or testimony for the appeal, the docket can't change. Filing a second Board appeal after the Board decides. The next steps are the Court of Appeals for Veterans Claims (120 days) or a Supplemental Claim. Not asking to advance the appeal when you qualify. Age 75 or older, serious illness, or severe financial hardship can move you up, but only if you ask in writing. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.2500(c)(3); 20.202(a), (c)–(e); 20.303; 20.800(c)"},{"href":"form-2680.html","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 21-2680, aid and attendance or housebound exam, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 21-2680 (June 2026 edition), the aid and attendance or housebound exam, section by section: who fills which part, what the examiner should document, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 21-2680 is a medical exam form. You fill out the first sections, and your own doctor or clinician fills out the exam. VA uses it to decide whether someone needs another person's regular help (aid and attendance) or is housebound, for special monthly compensation or special monthly pension. This guide explains each part and why it matters. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained JUN 2026 Replaces FEB 2023 OMB number 2900-0721, exp. 06/30/2029 Who fills it You: Sections I–V. Examiner: VI–VIII VA's form page listed June 2026 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . Always get the form from VA's form page. For a spouse who needs help, also read ClaimReady's Spouse aid and attendance article. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-2680.html#rules","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What VA decides from this exam","text":"Aid and attendance Needing another person's regular help with daily life. Any one of these can show it: Corrected vision of 5/200 or less in both eyes, or a visual field of 5 degrees or less. Being a patient in a nursing home because of mental or physical incapacity. A factual need: unable to dress, bathe, feed oneself, use the toilet, or adjust a prosthetic without help; needing protection from everyday hazards; or bedridden because of the condition. Not every factor has to be present, and the need must be regular, not constant. Housebound Either a single disability rated 100% plus other disabilities independently rated 60% or more, or permanently housebound: substantially confined to home and its immediate premises because of disability, and reasonably certain to stay that way. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.351(b)–(d), 3.352(a), 3.350(b)(3), 3.350(i)"},{"href":"form-2680.html#summary","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 21-2680 asks","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. You (Sections I–V) Veteran's and claimant's identification, and relationship. Who it's for (self, spouse, parent, child) and one benefit: SMC or SMP. Whether the claimant is in a hospital now, with dates and place. Sign and date Item 15 (the date is required). 2. Who can examine A medical doctor (MD), doctor of osteopathic medicine (DO), physician assistant, or advanced practice registered nurse (APRN). Usually your own provider. 3. Diagnoses Each diagnosis with its most significant symptoms (Item 17). They must match the level of help described later. Which conditions are permanent and totally disabling (Item 18). Which conditions restrict each activity, with the reason (Item 25). 4. Daily help Hours in bed, if confined (Item 26). Help needed, night (9 PM–9 AM) and day (9 AM–9 PM): bathing, toileting, eating, transfers, medications, hygiene, walking in the home, dressing, other (Item 27). 5. Body and safety Legal blindness and corrected vision, each eye (Item 28). Nursing home care needed (29); ability to manage payments (30). Arms, legs, spine; bowel or bladder, dizziness, memory, balance (31–35). 6. Leaving home and signing How often and under what conditions the claimant leaves home (36); canes, braces, or a person needed, and how far (37). Examiner's printed name, title, signature (required), date, NPI, and facility (38–45). VA form VA Form 21-2680 (Jun 2026), summarized in plain language. The form's own wording controls."},{"href":"form-2680.html#check","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What a reviewer checks first","text":"Gaps here can mean VA orders its own exam or asks your provider to redo the form. 1. Both parts are signed. You sign and date Item 15. The examiner gives a printed name, title, signature, date, and NPI number in Items 38–42. The examiner must be an MD, DO, PA, or APRN. Section V · Sections VII–VIII 2. The diagnoses explain the help described. The form says the diagnoses in Item 17 must match the level of help in Items 26–37, and Item 25 asks which condition restricts each activity and why. A checked box with no diagnosis behind it is weak evidence. Section VI Also check One benefit is chosen in Item 13, SMC or SMP, and the right claimant box is checked. Section II Both time periods are covered in Item 27, night and day. Corrected vision is given for each eye if vision is the issue (Item 28B). Leaving home is described (Item 36) if housebound status is the issue. VA form 21-2680 Items 13, 15, 17, 25–42"},{"href":"form-2680.html#map","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The sections at a glance","text":"I Veteran and claimant identification Items 1–12 · you II–III Who it's for and which benefit Item 13 · you IV Hospitalized now? Item 14 · you V Your certification and signature Item 15 · you VI Examination Items 16–37 · examiner, the heart of the form VII–VIII Examiner's signature and information Items 38–45 · examiner ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, or medical details. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-2680.html#s1","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Veteran and claimant identification","text":"What it asks The veteran's identification, and the claimant's if different, such as a spouse or child, with the relationship and a mailing address. You can agree to email correspondence. VA form 21-2680 Section I","sub":"Section I · Items 1–12"},{"href":"form-2680.html#s2","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Who it's for and which benefit","text":"What it asks Whether the exam is for the veteran (self), a spouse, a parent, or a child, and one benefit: Special Monthly Compensation (SMC) or Special Monthly Pension (SMP). Why it matters SMC is paid with service-connected compensation or DIC; for a veteran's own SMC, the need for help must come from service-connected conditions. SMP is an added amount for people eligible for pension, and any condition can count. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.350(b), 3.351(a) VA form 21-2680 Sections II–III","sub":"Section II–III · Item 13: choose one"},{"href":"form-2680.html#s4","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Hospitalized now?","text":"Why it matters If the claimant is in a hospital, give the admission date, name, and address so VA knows where the claimant is and can account for the stay. If not, skip to Section V. VA form 21-2680 Section IV","sub":"Section IV · Item 14"},{"href":"form-2680.html#s5","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Your certification and signature","text":"What it asks You certify your statements are true and correct to the best of your knowledge and belief, then sign and date. The form marks the date required. VA form 21-2680 Section V","sub":"Section V · Item 15"},{"href":"form-2680.html#s6","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Examination","text":"Who completes it An MD, DO, physician assistant, or APRN. The form asks for detailed findings: whether the claimant is blind or bedridden, how they walk, where they go, and what they do on a typical day. What makes the exam useful Diagnoses tied to function. Each diagnosis with its most significant symptoms (17), which ones are permanent and totally disabling (18), and which condition restricts each activity and why (25). Specific help, day and night. Item 27 lists bathing, toileting, eating, transfers, medications, hygiene, walking in the home, dressing, and other tasks, separately for 9 PM–9 AM and 9 AM–9 PM. Safety. Memory loss, dizziness, poor balance, and loss of bowel or bladder control (35) bear on whether the claimant needs protection from everyday hazards. Vision with correction for each eye (28B), since the test is corrected vision. Leaving home. How often and under what conditions (36), and whether a cane, brace, or person is needed and for how far (37). This bears on housebound status. Managing money. Item 30 asks whether the claimant can manage benefit payments. See ClaimReady's competency article for what VA does with that. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.351(c), 3.352(a), 3.350(i)(2) VA form 21-2680 Section VI and examiner note","sub":"Section VI · Items 16–37: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-2680.html#s7","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Examiner's signature and information","text":"What it asks The examiner's printed name, title, signature (required), date, NPI number, and the medical facility's name, address, and phone. A penalty notice applies to knowingly false statements. Why it matters VA needs to confirm the examiner is one of the qualified provider types and be able to follow up if something is unclear. VA form 21-2680 Sections VII–VIII","sub":"Section VII–VIII · Items 38–45"},{"href":"form-2680.html#mistakes","page":"Form 21-2680 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Boxes checked with no diagnosis behind them. The form asks the examiner to tie each restriction to a condition and explain why. Only daytime help described. Item 27 asks about nights too. Night needs, such as toileting or wandering, often matter. Vision given without correction. The test uses corrected vision in both eyes. Thinking the need has to be around the clock. The need must be regular, not constant, and not every factor has to apply. Missing examiner signature, title, or NPI. VA may not be able to use the form without them. Choosing both benefits, or the wrong one. Item 13 takes one: SMC or SMP."},{"href":"form-4142.html","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a, private records release, section by section","sub":"Section-by-section guide to VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a (AUG 2024): authorizing VA to request private medical records, what each section asks, and the law behind it.","text":"These two forms work together. 21-4142 is your permission for a private doctor, hospital, or other source to send records to VA. 21-4142a tells VA who those providers are and where to find them. This guide explains each section and why it matters under public law. It explains the forms; it does not fill them out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Editions explained AUG 2024 (both) Replaces JUL 2021 OMB number 2900-0858, exp. 08/31/2027 Authorization lasts 12 months from signing VA's form page listed August 2024 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . Always get the forms from VA's form page. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-4142.html#duty","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What VA does with your permission","text":"VA's part Makes reasonable efforts to get the private records you identify and authorize: generally a first request and at least one follow-up. Doesn't pay a provider's fee for records. Tells you if it can't get a record, what it tried, and what you can do. Your part Give enough information to find the records: provider name, address, conditions, and approximate dates. Sign the authorization. You are ultimately responsible for getting private records to VA, so sending them yourself is often faster. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c), (c)(1), (e)"},{"href":"form-4142.html#summary","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 21-4142 and 21-4142a say","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. When to use them To let VA request private records for your claim. Not needed for VA medical records, or if you already have the records or will get them yourself. 2. 21-4142: permission Section I: veteran. Section II: the patient, if not the veteran. Section III: sources it covers, such as doctors, social workers, employers, and insurers. Section IV: what may be released. 3. What may be released All medical records, including mental health (not psychotherapy notes), substance use, HIV/AIDS, sickle cell, and genetic tests, and how the condition affects daily tasks and work. Past records, plus records created in the 12 months after signing. 4. Limits, time, revoking Item 12: write any limit. Blank means no limit. Expires 12 months after signing. Revoke in writing to VA and to each source. Records already sent can still be used. 5. 21-4142a: providers For each provider: name, conditions treated, dates, and full address. Room for several providers on one form. 6. Sign and send Items 13 and 14, the signature and date, are required. Someone signing for you completes Item 16. Compensation: Evidence Intake Center, PO Box 4444, Janesville, WI 53547-4444, or upload online. VA forms VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a (Aug 2024), summarized in plain language. The forms' own wording controls."},{"href":"form-4142.html#check","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What a reviewer checks first","text":"A gap here means VA can't send the request, or the provider can't find the records. 1. The 21-4142 is signed and dated (Items 13–14). Both items are marked required. Without them, the provider has no permission to release anything. Section V 2. Each provider is fully listed on the 21-4142a. Name, conditions treated, dates, and a complete street address for every provider. An incomplete address means the request can't reach them. 21-4142a Section III Also check Item 12 doesn't block what VA needs. A limit can keep out the very records that support the claim. The authorization hasn't expired. It lasts 12 months. If a claim runs longer and VA still needs records, a new one may be needed. VA records aren't listed here. They don't need this form; list them on the claim form instead. VA forms 21-4142 Items 12–14; 21-4142a Items 9–13"},{"href":"form-4142.html#map","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The sections at a glance","text":"4142 I–II Veteran and patient identification Items 1–11 4142 III–IV Sources and records covered 4142 V Authorization, limits, and signature Items 12–16 4142a Medical provider information Items 1–13 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, or medical records. Those go only on the forms you send to VA."},{"href":"form-4142.html#s1","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Veteran and patient identification","text":"What it asks The veteran's identification. Section II is only if the records are about someone else, such as a spouse or child, for example for spouse aid and attendance or a helpless child. VA form 21-4142 Sections I–II","sub":"Section 4142 I–II · Items 1–11"},{"href":"form-4142.html#s3","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Sources and records covered","text":"Sources (Section III) Medical sources, social workers, examiners VA uses, employers, insurers, and others who know about the condition. Records (Section IV) All medical records, including information on mental health (except psychotherapy notes), substance use, sickle cell anemia, communicable diseases including HIV/AIDS, and genetic testing, plus how the condition affects daily tasks and work. It covers past records and records created within 12 months after you sign. Privacy Once a source sends records to VA, they are no longer covered by the HIPAA Privacy Rule but are protected by the federal Privacy Act. A provider can't require you to sign this authorization before treating you. VA form 21-4142 Sections III–IV and authorization text","sub":"Section 4142 III–IV"},{"href":"form-4142.html#s5","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Authorization, limits, and signature","text":"Item 12: limits Write any limit on what can be released. If blank, there is no limit. Think twice before limiting: a limit can keep out records that support your claim. Items 13–16: signing The signature and date are required. If someone else signs, Item 16 asks for their relationship and full name, title, organization, and address; a court appointment needs the docket number, county, and state. How long it lasts 12 months from the date signed, unless you revoke it. To revoke, write to the VA office handling your claim (or the Board, if on appeal) and send a copy to each source. VA can still use records released before you revoked. VA form 21-4142 Section V","sub":"Section 4142 V · Items 12–16"},{"href":"form-4142.html#a3","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Medical provider information","text":"What it asks Your identification (and the patient's, if different), then for each provider: name, conditions you were treated for, dates of treatment, and full address. Why it matters This is the address list VA uses to send requests. VA must make reasonable efforts to get records you adequately identify, so a complete name and address is what turns your permission into an actual request. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(1) VA form 21-4142a Sections I–III","sub":"Section 4142a · Items 1–13"},{"href":"form-4142.html#mistakes","page":"Form 21-4142 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Using the 21-4142 for VA records. VA gets its own records. List VA facilities on your claim form instead. Using it when you already have the records. The form warns this slows processing. Send them. Not signing or dating the 21-4142. Both are required. Incomplete provider addresses on the 21-4142a. VA can't request records it can't route. A limit in Item 12 that blocks key records. Leave it blank unless you have a specific reason. Assuming VA will keep trying forever. For private records, VA generally makes a first request and at least one follow-up, then tells you if it couldn't get them."},{"href":"form-526ez.html","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 21-526EZ, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 21-526EZ (Jan 2026 edition), section by section: what each part asks, why it matters under the law, intent to file and effective dates, the duty to assist, secondary conditions, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 21-526EZ is the application for disability compensation. This guide walks through what each section asks for and why it matters under public law, so you know what you are signing and what to have ready. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained JAN 2026 Replaces NOV 2022 OMB number 2900-0747, exp. 01/31/2029 Length 15 pages: 8 of notice, 7 of form VA's form page listed January 2026 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . VA changes forms from time to time; always get the form from VA's form page or file online at VA.gov. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#date","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Protect your date first","text":"The date VA receives your claim usually controls how far back payments can go. Two rules can give you an earlier date, but only if you act in time. Intent to file An intent to file holds your date for one year. If VA receives a complete claim within that year, VA treats the claim as filed on the date it received the intent to file. You can submit one by starting the online application (a saved online application counts), by sending the intent-to-file form, or by telling VA orally. It does not need to name your conditions. ClaimReady: Intent to file The one-year window after service If VA receives your claim within one year after you separate, payments for a condition granted on a direct basis can start the day after separation (or when entitlement arose). After that year, the date is generally the later of the date VA got the claim or the date entitlement arose. Claims for increase For a rated condition that got worse, VA can go back to the date the worsening is \"factually ascertainable\" from the evidence, but only if the claim or intent to file arrives within one year of that date. If the form was incomplete If VA receives an incomplete application, such as one without a signature, the date is protected only if you send a complete claim within one year. Fictional example Dana separates in June 2025 and starts the online application in March 2026, which saves an intent to file. Dana finishes and submits the claim in August 2026. Because the claim arrived within a year of the intent to file, VA treats it as filed in March 2026, and because March 2026 is within a year of separation, a granted direct claim could reach back to the day after Dana's separation. This is a made-up illustration, not a VA decision. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.155(b)–(c), 3.400 (general rule), 3.400(b)(2)(i), 3.400(o)(2) VA form 21-526EZ page 7, \"How VA Determines the Effective Date\""},{"href":"form-526ez.html#summary","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The instructions most people skip","text":"The first eight pages of the 21-526EZ are VA's notice of what evidence your claim needs, what you must do, and what VA will do. The law requires this notice, and because it comes with the form, VA does not send a separate first letter after you file. When you sign, you certify you received it. Here it is in one page. Print this one-page summary 1. Right form? New claim, or an increase for a rating decided over a year ago: 21-526EZ. Decided before and you have new and relevant evidence, or you disagree with a decision from the past year: 20-0995 Supplemental Claim, 20-0996 Higher-Level Review, or 10182 Board appeal. Pension: 21P-527EZ. Survivors: 21P-534EZ. 2. How and where to send it Online at VA.gov (parts can prefill; you can upload evidence and track status). Mail: Department of Veterans Affairs, Evidence Intake Center, PO Box 4444, Janesville, WI 53547-4444. An accredited VSO representative can help for free. 3. FDC: you send it all now Send all private treatment records with the claim. Name your VA and other federal treatment, and Guard or Reserve records. Send anything later and VA moves the claim to the standard process. No risk to trying. 4. Standard: VA helps you get it Sign 21-4142 and 21-4142a for private records you want VA to request. You have one year from the date VA receives the claim to send evidence. If you send nothing in 30 days, VA may decide sooner, and you keep the rest of the year. 5. VA will Get federal records you identify, such as VA medical center records. Provide an exam or medical opinion if one is needed to decide the claim. Standard process: make reasonable efforts for private and state records, and tell you if it can't get them. 6. Send these forms too, if they apply Mental health: 21-0781. Unemployability: 21-8940 (and 21-4192). Vet Center treatment: 21-4142. Dependents: 21-686c; child 18–23 in school: 21-674. Aid and attendance: 21-2680, or 21-0779 for a nursing home. Housing: 26-4555. Auto: 21-4502. 7. What the evidence must show Claim Evidence must show Direct An injury, disease, or event in service; a current disability (medical evidence, or lay evidence of visible symptoms); and a link between them. Presumptive Qualifying service (POW, chronic disease after discharge, radiation, herbicide locations, Camp Lejeune, Gulf War and post-9/11 locations) and a qualifying current disability. No link needs proving. Secondary A cu"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#item1","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Fully Developed Claim or standard process","text":"Item 1 asks how you want VA to process the claim. It also offers two special programs for people still in the military. FDC program (optional, faster) You send all private treatment records with the claim. You identify federal records, such as VA medical center records, and Guard or Reserve records. VA gets the federal records and any exam or opinion it needs. If you send more evidence later, or VA finds other records it needs, VA moves the claim to the standard process. The form says there is no risk to trying. Standard process You tell VA about evidence you want it to get, and sign VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a for private records. VA makes reasonable efforts to get private records: generally a first request and at least one follow-up. VA keeps trying for federal records until they don't exist or more tries would be futile. If VA can't get a record, it tells you. You are ultimately responsible for non-federal records. Either way, VA's duty to assist applies. VA must provide an exam or medical opinion when one is necessary to decide the claim, for example when there is evidence of a current disability, an in-service event, and an indication that the two may be connected, but not enough medical evidence to decide. IDES: check this only if your military department referred you to the Integrated Disability Evaluation System. BDD: file 90 to 180 days before separation; be available for exams for 45 days after filing; send the Separation Health Assessment Part A self-assessment, copies of your service treatment records for this period, and your expected release date. Filing close to the 180-day mark gives time for exams before you leave. With fewer than 90 days left, the claim goes through the regular process. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A; 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(1), (c)(2), (c)(4)(i), (e) VA form 21-526EZ pages 1–3 and 5 (FDC, standard process, BDD)"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#map","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The 13 sections at a glance","text":"Tap a section to jump to it. If VA has decided a claim for you before, the form says only Sections I, IV (if it applies), V, and a signature are required. p. 1–8 The instructions, on one page What evidence each claim needs I Your identification Items 2–12 II Change of address Item 13 III Homeless information Item 14 IV Toxic exposure Item 15 V Your claimed conditions and treatment Items 16–17, the heart of the form VI Service information Items 18–23 VII Retired, separation, severance, and training pay Items 24–28 VIII Direct deposit Items 29–32 IX–XII Signatures: you, witnesses, alternate signer, representative Items 33–37 XIII Addendum for more conditions Page 15 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, DoD ID, or bank information. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-526ez.html#check","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Six things a reviewer checks first","text":"These are the first things a reviewer looks at on a 21-526EZ. Check them yourself before you send it. A problem here can delay the claim or cost you your date. Your identifying information is complete. Name, Social Security number, birth date, and service number in Section I, so VA can match the claim to you and your service records. Section I It is signed. No signature means it is not a complete claim. If you can't sign, use the witness or alternate signer sections. Section IX VA medical center records are listed. Name every VA medical center or military clinic where you were treated after discharge in Item 17, so VA knows which federal records to get. Item 17 Your current mailing address is right. Item 10. Exam appointments and requests for evidence come by mail. Item 10 The homeless box is checked if it applies. Item 14A or 14C. It tells VA you need a different way to be reached, and a point of contact. Section III Exposure is not a claim. Answering the toxic exposure questions in Section IV does not claim anything. Each condition must be listed in Item 16, for example \"Asthma, burn pit exposure.\" Section IV Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.160(a)(1)–(4); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(2) VA form 21-526EZ pages 9–13"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s1","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Your identification","text":"Why it matters A complete claim must identify you and give VA enough to verify your service. The Privacy Act notice on page 14 says the Social Security number is required by law. Item 4 asks whether you ever filed with VA so VA can find your existing records. Watch for Item 8 is for BDD claims only: your date, or expected date, of release from active duty. Item 11: checking the box lets VA send claim mail electronically. Use an email you check. Item 12: if you work for VA, including work-study or internships, check the box. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5101(c)(1); 38 C.F.R. § 3.160(a)(1) VA form 21-526EZ page 9 and Privacy Act notice, page 14","sub":"Section I · Items 2–12: name, SSN, prior claims, VA file number, birth date, service number, BDD release date, phone, address, email, VA employee"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s2","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Change of address","text":"Why it matters VA sends exam appointments and requests for evidence by mail. A missed letter can mean a missed exam or deadline. If the move is temporary, give both a beginning and an ending date. VA form 21-526EZ page 9","sub":"Section II · Item 13: temporary or permanent, new address, dates"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s3","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Homeless information","text":"Why it matters Fill this in only if you are homeless or at risk of losing housing, for example housing that will be lost within 30 days. The point of contact in Items 14E and 14F gives VA a person who can reach you if mail can't. Also worth knowing VA has a separate form, VA Form 20-10207, Priority Processing Request, for situations such as extreme financial hardship or terminal illness. Check VA's form page for its current edition and the situations it covers. VA form 21-526EZ page 10; VA Form 20-10207","sub":"Section III · Item 14: currently homeless or at risk, living situation, point of contact"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s4","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Toxic exposure","text":"Why it matters For many exposures, the law presumes certain diseases are connected to service, so you don't need a doctor's link. Answering Item 15 tells VA which presumptions and records to check. Page 4 of the notice lists the qualifying locations and dates, such as Vietnam and its offshore waters from January 9, 1962, to May 7, 1975, and Camp Lejeune for at least 30 days between August 1, 1953, and December 31, 1987. Under the PACT Act, when you show a disability and a toxic exposure risk activity in service but the evidence doesn't yet establish service connection, VA must provide a medical exam and opinion that considers your total exposure across deployments. Exposure is not a claim. Answering Section IV tells VA about your service; it does not claim a condition. List each condition you want decided in Item 16, and name the exposure there too, for example \"Asthma, burn pit exposure.\" Watch for If you answer \"Yes\" in 15A, complete 15B through 15E. Approximate dates, month and year, are acceptable. 15D covers more than locations: asbestos, mustard gas, radiation, SHAD, job-related toxins (MOS), and Camp Lejeune water. List every exposure period in 15E, not just the first. Read more: ClaimReady's PACT Act summary and conditions VA has found no positive association with herbicides. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1168(a) VA form 21-526EZ pages 4 and 10","sub":"Section IV · Item 15: Gulf War and post-9/11 locations, herbicide locations, other exposures, dates"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s5","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Your claimed conditions and treatment","text":"What Item 16 asks For each condition, four things: the current disability or symptoms; the exposure, event, or injury it comes from; how it relates to service; and the approximate date it began or got worse. There are 15 lines, and Section XIII adds 20 more. Why it matters A complete claim must identify the benefit sought and describe the symptoms or medical conditions it is based on. What you list sets what VA looks at. VA also considers complications of a claimed condition and related \"ancillary\" benefits without you naming them, but naming each condition clearly is the safest way to make sure it is decided. How to describe a condition Symptoms are fine. The form asks for \"disability(ies) or symptoms.\" You don't need a diagnosis to list something, for example \"ringing in ears\" or \"low back pain.\" Name secondary conditions. If one service-connected condition caused or worsened another, say so, as in the form's own example: \"Left knee, secondary to right knee.\" Secondary service connection covers conditions caused by a service-connected disability and conditions it made worse beyond their natural progress. Tie it to service. One line on what happened, such as \"heavy equipment operator in service\" for hearing loss. Give a date, even if approximate. Month and year help VA know which records to look at. What Item 17 asks VA medical centers and military treatment facilities where you were treated after discharge for the conditions in Item 16, with an approximate start date. If treatment began in 2005 or later, you don't need dates in 17B. Why it matters: these are federal records, and VA must keep trying to get them until they don't exist or more tries would be futile. If you leave a facility off, VA may not know to look. Private doctors go on VA Forms 21-4142 and 21-4142a, or send those records yourself. Forms to attach for certain claims If you are claiming Attach A mental health condition VA Form 21-0781 (in-service traumatic events). See ClaimReady's PTSD stressor guide. Individual unemployability VA Form 21-8940 (Item 17's note also lists 21-4192, the employer form) Dependents VA Form 21-686c; 21-674 for a child 18–23 in school Aid and attendance (you or your spouse) VA Form 21-2680, or 21-0779 for nursing home status. See Spouse aid and attendance. Specially adapted housing or home adaptation VA Form 26-4555 Auto allowance VA Form 21-4502 Treatment at a Ve","sub":"Section V · Items 16–17: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s6","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Service information","text":"Why it matters Dates decide the one-year effective date window, which presumptions apply, and which records VA requests. List every period of service in 20D. Other names (18): records filed under a prior name can be missed. Guard and Reserve (21–22): service connection for training periods follows different rules. For inactive duty training, it generally covers injuries, plus heart attack, cardiac arrest, or stroke, rather than any disease. Unit details help VA find records. Former prisoners of war (23): certain diseases are presumed service connected. Watch for \"Served in a combat zone\" (Item 20C) is not the same thing as \"engaged in combat with the enemy,\" which is the phrase the law uses for the special rule that lets a combat veteran's own account prove an in-service event. Background Law 38 U.S.C. § 1154(b) VA form 21-526EZ pages 4 and 12","sub":"Section VI · Items 18–23: other names, branch, component, dates, combat zone, Guard and Reserve, activation, POW"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s7","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Retired, separation, severance, and training pay","text":"Read Items 26 and 28 slowly. Checking Item 26 or Item 28 tells VA not to pay you VA compensation. If you already get compensation and check Item 26 while eligible for retired pay, the form says your compensation will be terminated. The form notes VA compensation is non-taxable and may be the greater benefit. Why VA asks Military retired pay (24–25): filing waives retired pay equal to the VA award, unless you qualify for concurrent receipt. By law, concurrent retirement pay generally applies to retirees with a service-connected rating of 50% or more, with special rules for disability (chapter 61) retirees. Separation or disability severance pay (27): VA may withhold compensation to recoup it. For disability severance pay after January 28, 2008, there is no recoupment for disabilities incurred in a combat zone or in combat-related operations. Training pay (28): you can't receive both drill pay and VA compensation for the same days. The form says keeping training pay and waiving VA benefits for those days is usually the better choice; if you check Item 28, VA adjusts the award for those days, which can create a debt. Watch for Report a lump sum you received, with the pre-tax amount. Leaving it off can create an overpayment later. Law 10 U.S.C. § 1414(a); 38 C.F.R. § 3.700(a)(1), (a)(3), (a)(5) VA form 21-526EZ pages 12–13","sub":"Section VII · Items 24–28, including two \"Do NOT pay me\" boxes"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s8","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Direct deposit","text":"Why it matters The form says the Treasury requires federal benefits to be paid electronically. Skip this section if you already have direct deposit with VA. If you don't have a bank account, the form points to the Veterans Benefits Banking Program. VA form 21-526EZ page 13","sub":"Section VIII · Items 29–32"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s9","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Signatures","text":"Section IX: your signature (required) A complete claim must be signed by you or someone legally authorized to sign for you. An unsigned form is not a complete claim. By signing, you certify the statements are true, authorize anyone with information about you to release it to VA, confirm you got the notice on pages 1–8, and confirm you sent your evidence, have none, or chose the standard process. Section X: witnesses Only if you sign with an \"X.\" A signature by mark must be witnessed by two people who sign and give their addresses, or certified another way the regulation allows, such as by a notary. Section XI: alternate signer Only if Item 33A is blank. The law lets certain people sign for a claimant who is a minor, can't give accurate information because of mental incompetence, or is physically unable to sign: a court-appointed representative, a person responsible for the claimant's care such as a spouse, an agent under a durable power of attorney, or an institution's manager. VA Form 21-0972 must be on file or attached. Section XII: representative A representative can sign only if VA Form 21-22 or 21-22a is already on file, and the form says a representative can't sign an original claim. An accredited VSO representative helps for free. Agents and attorneys may not charge for work before you get notice of VA's initial decision. Law 38 U.S.C. §§ 5101(a)(2), 5904(c)(1); 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.160(a)(2), 3.2130 VA form 21-526EZ pages 13–14","sub":"Section IX–XII · Items 33–37: your signature, witnesses, alternate signer, representative"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#s13","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Addendum","text":"Use it only if you run out of lines in Item 16. It has the same four columns. Make copies if you need more, and send it with the application. VA form 21-526EZ page 15","sub":"Section XIII · Page 15: room for 20 more conditions"},{"href":"form-526ez.html#mistakes","page":"Form 21-526EZ Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Filing without an intent to file, then taking months to finish. The date usually controls when payments can start. Starting the online form or filing an intent to file holds it for a year. Using this form for a decision from the last year. Disagreeing with a recent decision, or a previously denied claim with new evidence, generally goes through a decision review form instead. Leaving off a secondary condition because \"it isn't from service.\" A condition caused or worsened by a service-connected condition can be service connected. Write \"secondary to\" and name the condition. Waiting for a diagnosis before listing something. The form asks for disabilities or symptoms. VA can get an exam if it needs one. Answering the exposure questions but not listing a condition. Exposure is not a claim. Each condition goes in Item 16. Skipping Section IV. Without exposure details, VA may not apply a presumption that fits your service. Leaving VA clinics out of Item 17. VA must look for federal records you identify; it can't look for ones it doesn't know about. Checking Item 26 or 28 by mistake. Both tell VA not to pay compensation. Not signing, or having a representative sign an original claim. An unsigned form is incomplete. You have a year to fix it, but your date is at risk. Forgetting the extra forms. Mental health claims, unemployability, dependents, and aid and attendance each have a form VA asks you to attach."},{"href":"form-8940.html","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"VA Form 21-8940, Individual Unemployability, section by section","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Form 21-8940, Individual Unemployability (July 2024 edition), section by section: the percentage rules, marginal employment, the work history VA needs, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA Form 21-8940 is the application for total disability based on individual unemployability (TDIU or IU): compensation at the 100% rate when service-connected conditions keep you from holding a substantially gainful job, even if your combined rating is lower. This guide explains what each section asks for and why it matters under public law. It explains the form; it does not fill it out, and nothing you do here is saved or sent. Edition explained JUL 2024 Replaces JUN 2021 OMB number 2900-0404, exp. 07/31/2027 Length 7 sections, 30 items VA's form page listed July 2024 as the current revision when this guide was checked on Oct 24, 2026 . Always get the form from VA's form page or file online at VA.gov. To see whether your ratings fit the percentage rules, try ClaimReady's TDIU calculator. Print or save this guide"},{"href":"form-8940.html#rules","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What VA looks at","text":"The 21-8940 gives VA your work and medical history. VA uses it to decide two things. The percentage rules Generally one service-connected condition at 60% or more, or two or more with one at 40% or more and a combined 70% or more. Some conditions count as one: both arms or both legs, a common cause or single accident, one body system, injuries in action, or as a prisoner of war. Can't work because of service-connected conditions Your service-connected conditions alone must keep you from getting or keeping a substantially gainful job. VA does not count age or conditions that aren't service connected. Marginal work doesn't count against you Marginal employment is not substantially gainful. It generally exists when your yearly earned income is at or below the Census Bureau's poverty threshold for one person. It can also be found above that line on the facts, such as a protected job in a family business or a sheltered workshop. Below the percentages? If you are unemployable because of service-connected conditions but don't meet the percentages, the rule says the case should go to VA's Director of Compensation Service for extra-schedular consideration. Check your ratings in the IU calculator Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 4.16(a)–(b), 4.19, 3.341(a)"},{"href":"form-8940.html#summary","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What the 21-8940 asks and says","text":"Print this one-page summary 1. What it is A claim for total disability because of service-connected conditions. \"Answer all questions fully and accurately.\" Often filed with a 21-526EZ, which lists 21-8940 and 21-4192 for unemployability. 2. Disability and treatment Item 8: which service-connected conditions keep you from working. Items 9–13: doctors and hospitals in the past 12 months, with dates. 3. Work history When your disability affected full-time work, when you last worked full time, and when you became too disabled to work. Item 18: every job, including self-employment, for the last five years you worked, with hours, dates, time lost to illness, and earnings. 4. Income and benefits Earned income for the past 12 months, and current monthly income if working. Whether you left your last job because of disability (explain in Remarks), and any disability retirement or workers' comp. Jobs you applied for since becoming too disabled. 5. Education Highest grade completed. Training before and since you became too disabled to work. 6. Signature and duties Sign Item 27 (required). Two witnesses if you sign with an \"X.\" You certify you can't secure or follow a substantially gainful occupation. If granted, you must tell VA right away if you return to work, or payments after that may become a debt. Mail: Evidence Intake Center, PO Box 4444, Janesville, WI 53547-4444. VA form VA Form 21-8940 (Jul 2024), summarized in plain language. The form's own wording controls."},{"href":"form-8940.html#check","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What a reviewer checks first","text":"Drawn from the form. A gap here usually means VA has to write back and ask, which delays the decision. 1. It is signed and dated (Items 27–28). The form marks the signature required. Section VI 2. The work history is complete (Items 14–18). The three dates and every employer for the last five years you worked, with hours, dates, time lost, and earnings. VA uses it to contact employers and to judge whether past work was substantially gainful. Section III Also check Item 8 names service-connected conditions, not others. Section II Earned income (Item 20) is filled in, even if it is zero. Education (Items 23–25) is filled in. Section IV Remarks explain why you left your last job, if it was because of your disability. Section V VA form 21-8940 Items 8, 14–28"},{"href":"form-8940.html#map","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"The sections at a glance","text":"I Your identification Items 1–7 II Disability and medical treatment Items 8–13 III Employment statement Items 14–22, the heart of the form IV Schooling and other training Items 23–25 V Remarks Item 26 VI–VII Authorization, certification, signature, where to send Items 27–30 ClaimReady never asks for your Social Security number, VA file number, or income details. Those go only on the form you send to VA."},{"href":"form-8940.html#s1","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Your identification","text":"Why it matters Matches the application to your claims file. Keep your mailing address and phone current: VA may schedule an exam or ask follow-up questions. VA form 21-8940 Section I","sub":"Section I · Items 1–7"},{"href":"form-8940.html#s2","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Disability and medical treatment","text":"What it asks Item 8: the service-connected conditions that keep you from working. Items 9–13: whether you've seen a doctor or been hospitalized in the past 12 months, with names, addresses, and dates. More dates go in Item 26. Why it matters Only service-connected conditions count. Naming them, and the treatment that shows how they limit you, tells VA what to look at. If a condition isn't service connected yet, it needs its own claim. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.16(a) VA form 21-8940 Section II","sub":"Section II · Items 8–13"},{"href":"form-8940.html#s3","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Employment statement","text":"The dates (Items 14–16) When your disability began affecting full-time work, when you last worked full time, and when you became too disabled to work. These can bear on when entitlement arose. Earnings and history (Items 17–18) The most you ever earned in a year, and every job, including self-employment and military duty, for the last five years you worked: employer, type of work, hours, dates, time lost to illness, and highest monthly earnings. Use Remarks if you need more room. Now (Items 19–22) Guard or Reserve: whether your condition keeps you from military duties. Earned income for the past 12 months, and current monthly earnings if working. Work at or below the one-person poverty threshold is generally marginal, not substantially gainful. Whether you left your last job because of your disability (explain in Item 26), and whether you get or expect disability retirement or workers' compensation. Jobs you've applied for since becoming too disabled to work. Watch for VA may ask your last employers for information; the 21-526EZ lists VA Form 21-4192, the employer's form, alongside the 21-8940. Give complete employer addresses so the request reaches them. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.16(a) VA form 21-8940 Section III; 21-526EZ (Jan 2026) Item 17 note","sub":"Section III · Items 14–22: the heart of the form"},{"href":"form-8940.html#s4","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Schooling and other training","text":"Why it matters Education and training help show what kinds of work you could realistically do. VA asks about training both before and since you became too disabled to work. VA form 21-8940 Section IV","sub":"Section IV · Items 23–25"},{"href":"form-8940.html#s5","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Remarks","text":"Use it for More treatment dates (Items 10 and 13). More employers (Item 18). Why you left your last job, if it was because of your disability (Item 21A). A plain description of what your conditions stop you from doing at work. VA form 21-8940 Items 10, 13, 18, 21A, 26","sub":"Section V · Item 26"},{"href":"form-8940.html#s6","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"Authorization, certification, signature, where to send","text":"What you agree to You authorize release of information to VA. You certify you are unable to secure or follow any substantially gainful occupation, and that your statements are true and complete. If granted, you must tell VA immediately if you return to work. Payments after you start work may be treated as an overpayment you have to repay. Signing Sign Item 27 (required) and date Item 28. If you sign with an \"X,\" two witnesses who know you must sign in ink and give their addresses. Where to send Department of Veterans Affairs, Evidence Intake Center, PO Box 4444, Janesville, WI 53547-4444, or online at VA.gov. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.160(a)(2), 3.2130 VA form 21-8940 Sections VI–VII","sub":"Section VI–VII · Items 27–30"},{"href":"form-8940.html#mistakes","page":"Form 21-8940 Guide","kind":"Form guide","title":"What often goes wrong","text":"Leaving the employment history blank or partial. VA needs it to contact employers and judge past work. Use Remarks for more room. Listing conditions that aren't service connected in Item 8. Only service-connected conditions count toward unemployability. Thinking any work at all rules you out. Marginal employment is not substantially gainful, and the question is whether you can get and keep substantially gainful work. Thinking you must meet the percentages exactly. If service-connected conditions keep you from working but the percentages aren't met, the rule calls for extra-schedular consideration. Not telling VA about a return to work. The form requires it. Late notice can create a debt. Not signing. The signature is required."},{"href":"hepatitis.html","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Hepatitis claims: blood tests, risk factors, and ratings","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA hepatitis claims: the blood tests that prove hepatitis B and C, risk factors before, during, and after service, how drug use is handled, medical opinions, and how hepatitis is rated. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"Hepatitis claims work differently from most claims. VA needs a blood test that names the type of hepatitis, and it looks at every way you could have been infected, before, during, and after service. This article explains the three types VA rates, the tests that prove a diagnosis, the risk factors VA weighs, how drug use is handled, what a good medical opinion covers, and how hepatitis is rated. Based on public sources: 38 U.S.C. §§ 105 and 1110, 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102, 3.159, 3.301, 3.303, 3.957, 4.112, and 4.114, published court decisions, and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part V, Subpart iii, Chapter 6, Section 3. Medical background that does not come from VA is marked. For education only. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#start","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Three things decide most hepatitis claims","text":"Which type, proven by blood work Hepatitis A, B, and C are different diseases. VA needs lab tests that identify the type you have now. Which risk factor caused it VA lists every known risk factor in your life and weighs which one most likely caused the infection. How it affects you now The rating depends on your symptoms, treatment, and any liver damage, not on the diagnosis alone. What brings you here? I'm filing a new claim Or thinking about it Hepatitis is in my service records Or I was told I had \"hepatitis\" in service Drug use is part of my history Now or in the past I'm already service connected And want to understand my rating Filing a new claim. Get your confirming blood test results into the record, then think through every risk factor in your life, not only the ones in service. Expect VA to send you questions about risk factors. Answer every one honestly and completely. Blood tests · Risk factors · The risk factor questions Hepatitis in your service records. What it means depends on what the records say. A note of hepatitis A rarely helps on its own, while an old diagnosis of \"non-A, non-B hepatitis\" can be strong evidence for hepatitis C today. Hepatitis in your service records · Blood tests Drug use in your history. It does not end a claim by itself. VA must look at every other risk factor, and if the evidence is evenly balanced, the tie goes to you. There is also a separate route when drug use was caused by a service-connected condition. How drug use is handled · Older awards Already service connected. Hepatitis B and C are rated as chronic liver disease. Symptoms, continuous medication, weight loss, and certain treatments move the rating. Complications like cirrhosis or liver cancer are rated under their own codes. How hepatitis is rated · When 0% applies · Older awards involving drug use"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#types","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Three types of viral hepatitis","text":"Hepatitis means inflammation of the liver. VA's manual describes three main viral types for rating purposes. Older records may use the older names shown below. Type How it spreads What usually happens Hepatitis A (HAV) once called \"infectious hepatitis\" Fecal-oral route, such as contaminated food or water A short (acute) illness that seldom leaves lasting damage. Service connection needs evidence of chronic residuals. Hepatitis B (HBV) once called \"serum hepatitis\" Blood and blood products; sexual contact Usually clears after the acute phase (90 to 95% of cases in VA's manual), though the acute illness can be severe. Becomes chronic in 5 to 10%. Cirrhosis and liver cancer can develop. A vaccine exists. Hepatitis C (HCV) once called \"non-A, non-B hepatitis\" Infected blood Often no symptoms at first. Becomes chronic in about 80%. It is usually found by chance, many years later. History note from VA's manual: infectious hepatitis was common in World War II, especially after the 1942 yellow fever vaccine and in the Mediterranean Theater. VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.a"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#tests","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"VA needs blood tests that name the type","text":"A diagnosis of \"hepatitis\" alone is not enough. VA's manual calls for serology (blood tests for the virus or the body's antibodies to it) to confirm the type, and liver function tests to show how severe it is. The decision should name the type that is service connected. Hepatitis C An antibody test (anti-HCV, by EIA or ELISA) is usually first. A positive antibody alone does not prove chronic hepatitis C. A positive HCV RNA test (the virus itself) is enough by itself to confirm hepatitis C. Older records may show a RIBA test used to confirm a positive antibody. Hepatitis B HBsAg (surface antigen) shows active infection. If it lasts more than 6 months, that suggests chronic infection or carrier status. An old \"Australia antigen\" result is the same marker and is enough to confirm hepatitis B. Antibody tests (anti-HBc, anti-HBs) help show past infection or immunity. Hepatitis A Anti-HAV antibodies appear about a month after the illness and last for life. A positive result shows a past infection, not a current disease. Liver biopsy, ultrasound, and CT scans can show liver damage, but they cannot tell which virus caused it. Medical background, not from VA Since 2013, the CDC has recommended confirming a positive hepatitis C antibody with an HCV RNA test. The RIBA test VA's manual mentions is no longer available in the U.S. A positive antibody with a negative RNA test usually means a past infection that cleared, or a false positive. Reading a hepatitis B panel Pick what your lab report says. This uses the example patterns in VA's manual. Your doctor reads your actual results, and lab reports vary. HBsAg Not sure Negative Positive Anti-HBc (total) Not sure Negative Positive IgM anti-HBc Not done / not sure Negative Positive Anti-HBs Not sure Negative Positive Choose results above to see which example pattern matches. HBsAg Anti-HBc IgM anti-HBc Anti-HBs Meaning Negative Negative Negative No sign of hepatitis B; can still be infected Negative Negative or positive Positive Immune Positive Positive Positive Negative Acute (new) infection Positive Positive Negative Negative Chronic infection Medical background, not from VA In general medical guidance, immunity with a negative anti-HBc usually comes from the vaccine, and immunity with a positive anti-HBc usually comes from a past infection that cleared. Only chronic hepatitis B is a lasting disability. VA guidance M21-1 "},{"href":"hepatitis.html#risks","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Risk factors: how you might have been infected","text":"Hepatitis B and C share most risk factors. VA's manual lists the ones it treats as medically recognized, along with the kinds of records that can show them. The question is usually not whether you have hepatitis, but which exposure most likely caused it. Risk factor Why it matters Records that can help Blood transfusion before 1992 (hepatitis C) or before 1975 (hepatitis B); organ transplant before 1992; kidney dialysis Blood screening for hepatitis C did not exist until the virus was identified in 1989 and became much more effective in 1992. Hospital records from service, especially surgical reports, anesthesia sheets, and post-operative notes Tattoos, body piercing, or acupuncture with non-sterile needles Unsterilized equipment can carry the virus. Entry and separation exams that do or do not list tattoos or piercings, which can help show when they happened Accidental blood exposure, such as a needlestick or blood in the eyes or mouth Common for health care workers, combat medics, and corpsmen Personnel records showing your job, duty assignments, and any exposure reports Sharing razors or toothbrushes Direct contact with blood Rarely written down. Statements from people who served with you can help, weighed with the rest of the evidence. High-risk sexual activity Risk is relatively low but rises with multiple partners. Health assessments or treatment records for sexually transmitted infections Injection drug use or intranasal cocaine use Shared needles or straws can carry blood. Drug treatment records. See how VA handles drug use. Vaccination with a jet air gun injector VA's manual notes one documented case of hepatitis B spread this way and says spread of hepatitis C is biologically possible, though not scientifically documented. A medical opinion that links hepatitis to an air gun must discuss all possible sources and explain why the air gun is the likely one. If the evidence for a risk factor in service is about as strong as the evidence for one before or after service, the tie goes to you. This is the benefit-of-the-doubt rule. In Andrews v. McDonough (2021), the Veterans Court reversed a hepatitis C denial outright. The veteran described air gun vaccinations and dental work in service, the Board did not dispute them, VA had found no risk factors before or after service, and the evidence was at least evenly balanced. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.102 Court Andrews"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#asked","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"VA will ask you about risk factors","text":"The standard claim form does not ask about hepatitis risk factors, so VA's manual says to ask in every hepatitis claim, even when hepatitis was diagnosed in service, unless you have already given a complete history. You will likely get a letter or questionnaire asking about risk factors before, during, and after service. Answer every question, including the ones about life before and after service. VA is required to look at the whole picture, and an unanswered question can slow the claim or lead to a weaker opinion. Be honest and specific. Give dates and places where you can. A clear, consistent account is more believable than a vague one. Point to records. Tell VA about in-service surgeries, hospital stays, injuries with bleeding, or medical jobs. Your own statement is evidence. In Andrews, the court weighed the veteran's statement that he had no risk factors outside service. VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.f Court Andrews v. McDonough, 34 Vet. App. 151 (2021)"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#drugs","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"How VA handles drug use in a hepatitis claim","text":"Many veterans have drug use somewhere in their history. It matters in a hepatitis claim because of one rule in the law, but it does not end the claim on its own. The rule in the law For claims filed after October 31, 1990, VA cannot pay compensation for a disability that results from a veteran's own abuse of drugs. The regulation defines drug abuse to include using illegal drugs, misusing prescription drugs, and using substances to experience their intoxicating effects. The steps in VA's manual when injection drug or intranasal cocaine use is confirmed in service VA does not assume drug use caused it. It must look for every other risk factor too. If drug use is the only confirmed risk factor in service, VA denies service connection. This is the rule above at work. If there are other confirmed risk factors in service, VA asks a medical examiner which one is at least as likely as not the cause. VA reads the opinion: Drug use is the cause: denied. Another in-service risk factor carries equal or greater weight: granted, with the doubt resolved in your favor. The examiner cannot say which is more likely: VA weighs all the evidence and applies the benefit of the doubt if it is evenly balanced. When drug use was caused by a service-connected condition The Federal Circuit held in Allen v. Principi (2001) that the drug abuse rule does not bar compensation when the substance use itself results from a service-connected condition, such as PTSD or chronic pain. In that situation, hepatitis from the drug use may be service connected as a secondary condition. The court required clear medical evidence that the service-connected condition caused the substance use. Line of duty and substance use · Secondary service connection An older part of the regulation says infections that coincide with drug injection are not considered \"willful misconduct.\" That rule is separate. For claims filed after October 31, 1990, the drug abuse bar in the law and in 38 C.F.R. § 3.301(d) still applies, which is why VA's manual denies a claim when drug use is the only in-service risk factor. If this applies to you, talk with an accredited representative. Law 38 U.S.C. §§ 105(a), 1110; 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102, 3.301(c)(3), (d) Court Allen v. Principi, 237 F.3d 1368 (Fed. Cir. 2001) VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.g"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#older","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Older awards where drug use was the only risk factor","text":"Some veterans were service connected for hepatitis when drug use was the only apparent in-service risk factor. VA's manual has special steps when one of them asks for a higher rating. Awarded on or before Oct 31, 1990 The award was proper under the rules at the time, so service connection continues. But under a VA General Counsel opinion, the rating cannot be increased when the award was based on drug abuse. Awarded after Oct 31, 1990 VA may get a medical opinion on whether drug use caused the hepatitis. If the opinion cannot decide, or points to another in-service risk factor, service connection continues and an increase can be granted if the evidence supports it. If the evidence clearly shows drug use was the cause, VA checks whether the award is protected; if it is not, VA may propose to sever it. Protections that apply: service connection in effect for 10 years or more generally cannot be severed except for fraud or a lack of required service. Before that, VA can sever only if the award was \"clearly and unmistakably erroneous,\" VA carries that burden, and you get advance notice and 60 days to send evidence. If you receive a proposal, contact an accredited representative right away. Law Pub. L. 101-508, § 8052; 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.105(d), 3.957; VAOPGCPREC 2-1998 VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.h"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#inservice","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Hepatitis in your service records","text":"VA first checks the basics: a current disability, something in service, and some indication they are related. Then what your service records show shapes what happens next. If service records show What VA's manual says happens \"Hepatitis\" with no type, and you claim it many years later VA usually orders an exam with blood tests and an opinion on whether today's hepatitis relates to the episode in service. It may skip the opinion if the diagnosis was clear and symptoms continued since service. Lab tests confirming hepatitis A Not service connected by itself, because hepatitis A is short-term. Lab tests confirming hepatitis B Possible if chronic hepatitis B is present now and linked to the finding or risk factors in service. Hepatitis A or B in service, and hepatitis C now Not automatic. Each type is caught separately. Hepatitis C needs a medical opinion linking it to confirmed in-service findings or risk factors. \"Non-A, non-B hepatitis\" in service, and hepatitis C now Service connection is likely. If symptoms continued since service, it can be granted; if not, VA gets an opinion. \"Hepatitis\" with no type, and hepatitis C or chronic hepatitis B now May be granted with the benefit of the doubt. VA gets an opinion and any needed blood tests. The same, plus a history of hepatitis A A medical opinion is needed on whether today's condition comes from the hepatitis in service. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.159(c)(4), 3.303(b) VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.i"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#exam","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"The exam and the medical opinion","text":"For hepatitis B or C, the examiner usually completes the Hepatitis, Cirrhosis and Other Liver Conditions questionnaire (DBQ) and gives an opinion on which risk factor most likely caused the infection. What the examiner should consider All relevant evidence in your file The risk factors you reported Every risk factor the records confirm, whether you named it or not, before, during, and after service For a link to service, only risk factors confirmed by the evidence Signs an opinion may be inadequate No confirming blood test for the diagnosis Incomplete findings or symptoms Does not address every known risk factor Links hepatitis to a risk factor the record does not confirm Links it to something not medically recognized as a way to catch hepatitis If you believe the opinion missed something, you can point it out to VA, send your own doctor's opinion, or ask for a review after a decision. See Medical opinions and Supplemental Claims. VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.j–k"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#rating","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"How hepatitis B and C are rated","text":"Hepatitis C is listed under diagnostic code 7354 but rated using the criteria for chronic liver disease without cirrhosis (code 7345). Hepatitis B is rated directly under 7345. These criteria took effect May 19, 2024; older decisions used earlier criteria. 100% Progressive chronic liver disease that requires both parenteral (injected or IV) antiviral therapy and parenteral immune-modulating therapy such as interferon. Continues for 6 months after treatment ends, then VA re-examines. 60% Progressive chronic liver disease that requires continuous medication and causes substantial weight loss, plus at least two of: daily fatigue, malaise, loss of appetite, enlarged liver, itching, joint pain. 40% The same, with minor weight loss. 20% Chronic liver disease with at least one of: intermittent fatigue, malaise, loss of appetite, enlarged liver, or itching. 0% A past history of liver disease, with no current symptoms. Substantial: involuntary loss of more than 20% of your baseline weight for 3 months, with reduced ability to care for yourself or work. Minor: involuntary loss of 10% to 20% for 3 months, with digestive symptoms and reduced self-care, work, or food intake. Baseline: your documented average weight for the 2 years before the illness began; otherwise your last discharge exam weight; otherwise an ideal body weight using the method most favorable to you. Complications are rated separately. Cirrhosis, liver cancer, and other effects are rated under their own codes, but the same symptoms cannot be counted twice. If doctors recommend the 100% treatments but you cannot have them for medical reasons, VA rates under the cirrhosis code instead. Hepatitis B and other liver conditions under code 7345 cannot be combined with many other digestive ratings; VA rates the main condition and may raise it. Medical background, not from VA Most hepatitis C treatment today is taken by mouth, not injected. Whether a particular treatment meets the 100% wording is a question for VA. Ask your doctor what your treatment is and how it is given. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 4.14, 4.112, 4.114 (DCs 7312, 7345, 7354), as amended by 89 FR 19735 (Mar 20, 2024; effective May 19, 2024)"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#zero","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"When 0% applies to hepatitis C, and when it does not","text":"VA's manual says 0% is only for hepatitis C that is without symptoms and has a past history of liver disease. If the medical evidence shows Is 0% right? Even mild symptoms from hepatitis C No. You have symptoms. Liver damage on liver function tests, biopsy, or other testing No. The infection is not healed. Treatment worked, RNA tests are negative, no symptoms, and no liver damage Yes. VA's manual adds that the virus may stay dormant and flare later. What this means for you: tell the examiner about every symptom, even mild or on-and-off ones like fatigue or itching, and make sure recent lab work is in the record. Medical background, not from VA Current medical guidance generally treats a lasting negative RNA result after modern treatment (a \"sustained virologic response\") as a cure, though reinfection is possible. VA's manual takes a more cautious view. Either way, the rating follows your symptoms and test results. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.114, DC 7345, 7354 VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.6.3.l"},{"href":"hepatitis.html#example","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look","text":"Fictional example A Navy veteran served as a corpsman from 1984 to 1988. In 2023 routine screening found a positive hepatitis C antibody, and a follow-up HCV RNA test was positive. The veteran also got a tattoo two years after leaving service. Diagnosis: the positive RNA test confirms hepatitis C. In service: personnel records show corpsman duty, and the veteran describes a needlestick while treating a bleeding patient. A shipmate's statement backs that up. After service: the tattoo is a post-service risk factor, so VA must weigh it too. The opinion: the examiner reviews both and explains which is at least as likely as not the cause. If the evidence for each is evenly balanced, the benefit of the doubt goes to the veteran. The rating: treatment cleared the virus, but the veteran still reports intermittent fatigue. VA weighs that symptom and its connection to the liver disease. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"hepatitis.html#myths","page":"Hepatitis Claims","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"Any drug use means an automatic denial.\" No. VA must look at every other risk factor, and drug use caused by a service-connected condition can be a path to secondary service connection. \"I had hepatitis in service, so my hepatitis C is automatic.\" Not if the earlier finding was hepatitis A or B. Each type is caught separately. An old \"non-A, non-B\" diagnosis is much stronger evidence. \"A positive antibody test proves I have hepatitis C.\" Not by itself. A positive RNA test is what confirms it. \"I should leave out risk factors after service.\" VA is required to look for them, and leaving them out can hurt your credibility. The benefit of the doubt applies when the evidence is balanced. \"Air gun vaccinations will get my claim granted.\" They can be considered, but an opinion must discuss every possible source and explain why the air gun is the likely one. \"My hepatitis C was treated, so I'm stuck at 0%.\" Only if you have no symptoms and no liver damage. Lingering symptoms or damage can support a higher rating."},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"Not on the Agent Orange list? What \"no positive association\" really means","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to the conditions VA says have no positive association with herbicide exposure such as Agent Orange: what that means, look-alike conditions, toxic exposure exams, and how direct service connection still works. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"VA keeps a list of conditions it presumes were caused by herbicides such as Agent Orange. It also keeps a second, less known list: conditions the Secretary of Veterans Affairs looked at and decided do not get a presumption. If your condition is on that second list, your claim is not over. It means you need a different route, called direct service connection. This article explains both lists, what \"no positive association\" means in plain English, and what can help a direct claim. Based on public sources: 38 U.S.C. §§ 1113, 1116, 1168, and 1174, 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.303, 3.307, and 3.309(e), the Secretary's notices in the Federal Register, published court decisions, and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part VIII, Subpart i, Chapter 1, Section B, Topic 1.g. For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#start","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"No presumption is not the same as no claim","text":"If VA says there is \"no positive association\" between herbicides and your condition, that only takes away the shortcut. You can still show, with evidence, that your condition is related to your service. Congress wrote this into the law: nothing in the presumption statutes, including the Agent Orange law, \"shall be construed to prevent the granting of service-connection for any disease or disorder otherwise shown by sound judgment to have been incurred in or aggravated by\" service. With a presumption VA presumes the link between your exposure and your condition. You need a qualifying place and dates of service, and a diagnosis (plus any time or severity rule). You usually do not need a doctor to explain the link. Without a presumption (direct) You need the same three things as any direct claim: a current condition, an event or exposure in service, and a medical link between them. If VA concedes herbicide exposure, the second element is usually met already. The key evidence is a medical opinion about you, explaining why your exposure is at least as likely as not a cause. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1113(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(d) Court Combee v. Brown, 34 F.3d 1039, 1043-44 (Fed. Cir. 1994); Holton v. Shinseki, 557 F.3d 1363, 1366 (Fed. Cir. 2009)"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#meaning","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"What \"no positive association\" means","text":"The law used a specific test. A link between a disease and herbicide exposure counted as a positive association if \"the credible evidence for the association is equal to or outweighs the credible evidence against the association.\" VA's manual states the reverse: no positive association means the evidence for a link does not equal or outweigh the evidence against it. It is about groups of people. The finding comes from scientific studies of large populations, reviewed by the National Academy of Sciences (now the National Academies) since 1993. It is mostly \"not enough evidence.\" In the 2014 notice, most of these conditions were in the category \"inadequate or insufficient evidence to determine an association.\" That is different from proof that herbicides cannot cause them. It is not a finding about you. The notice itself says the determination \"does not in any way preclude VA from granting service connection for any disease.\" The Agent Orange Act of 1991 had the Secretary review each National Academy of Sciences report and decide, for each condition, whether a presumption was warranted. When the answer was no, the Secretary published a notice in the Federal Register explaining the science. Notices like this appeared from 1994 through 2014. That part of the law expired on September 30, 2015. The PACT Act (2022) replaced it with a general process for all toxic exposures. Under it, when the Secretary decides a new presumption is not warranted, the Secretary must still publish a notice in the Federal Register with the reasons. Lists change. Conditions once listed as having no positive association have later been added as presumptions. Bladder cancer and hypothyroidism were added by Congress in 2021, and high blood pressure (hypertension) in 2022. Prostate cancer was denied a presumption in 1994, a decision a federal court upheld in 1995, and VA added it later. Law Former 38 U.S.C. § 1116(b)(3) (Agent Orange Act of 1991, Pub. L. 102-4); 38 U.S.C. §§ 1116(b), 1173, 1174; Pub. L. 117-168, § 202 Federal Register 59 FR 341 (Jan 4, 1994); 79 FR 20308 (Apr 11, 2014) Court LeFevre v. Brown, 66 F.3d 1191 (Fed. Cir. 1995) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.1.g"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#list","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"Conditions VA says have no positive association with herbicides","text":"This is the list in VA's current manual, which draws on the Secretary's determinations. Watch the words \"excluding\": they point to conditions that are handled differently, usually because they are on the presumptive list. Search the list for a word such as colon, skin, lung, or thyroid Cancers Bone and connective tissue Brain and nervous system (including eye) Digestive: esophageal, stomach, and colorectal (including small intestine and anus) Endocrine (including thyroid and thymus) Hepatobiliary (liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts) and pancreatic Leukemia, excluding all chronic B-cell leukemias, including chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and hairy cell Nasal cavity (including ears and sinuses) Oral cavity (including lips and tongue) Pharynx (including tonsils) Pleura, mediastinum, and other unspecified sites within the respiratory system and intrathoracic organs Skin (including melanoma and nonmelanoma: basal cell and squamous cell) Reproductive (including the cervix, uterus, ovary, testes, breast, and penis), excluding prostate Other conditions Asthma Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) Circulatory disorders, excluding hypertension, ischemic heart disease (IHD), and stroke Endometriosis Eye diseases Farmer's lung Gastrointestinal, metabolic, and digestive disorders Hearing loss Immune system disorders (immune suppression, allergy, and autoimmunity) Neurobehavioral (cognitive and neuropsychiatric) disorders Neurodegenerative diseases (including ALS), excluding Parkinson's disease and parkinsonism Osteoporosis Chronic peripheral nervous system disorders, excluding early-onset peripheral neuropathy Excluded does not always mean presumed. Most of the conditions after \"excluding\" are on the presumptive list. Stroke is the exception: it is not presumptive for herbicides, but it is also not on this list, so it is not treated as a \"no association\" condition. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.1.g (wording lightly edited for reading; categories unchanged) Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.309(e); 79 FR 20308 (2014)"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#lookalikes","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"Look-alikes on opposite lists","text":"These pairs are easy to mix up. The exact diagnosis in your medical records decides which list applies. Presumed for herbicide exposure No positive association Why it matters Hypothyroidism Thyroid cancer One is an underactive thyroid; the other is a cancer of the gland. Chronic B-cell leukemias, such as CLL and hairy cell leukemia Other leukemias, such as acute myeloid leukemia The leukemia type on the pathology report decides it. Prostate cancer Other reproductive cancers, such as testicular or breast Prostate is the one reproductive cancer on the presumptive list. Lung, bronchus, larynx, and trachea cancers Pleura, mediastinum, and other respiratory sites Where the cancer started matters. A cancer that spread to the lung from somewhere else is not a lung cancer for this purpose. Soft-tissue sarcomas Bone and connective tissue cancers; the presumption excludes osteosarcoma, chondrosarcoma, Kaposi's sarcoma, and mesothelioma The sarcoma type matters. Parkinson's disease and parkinsonism Other neurodegenerative diseases, including ALS ALS has its own presumption based on any active service, not herbicides. Early-onset peripheral neuropathy Chronic peripheral nervous system disorders Early-onset neuropathy must reach 10% within one year of your last exposure. Later neuropathy can still be claimed directly or as secondary, for example to diabetes. Hypertension and ischemic heart disease Other circulatory disorders Stroke is on neither list. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1116(a)(2); 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.307(a)(6)(ii), 3.309(e), 3.318 (ALS) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.1.g; 1.C"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#direct","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"What can help a direct claim","text":"VA weighs all of the evidence, and when it is evenly balanced, the doubt goes to you. These are the pieces VA looks for. A current diagnosis, with its date. For a cancer, the primary site (where it started) and the type, from a pathology report if you have one. Your exposure. Proof of service in a place where VA presumes herbicide exposure, or other evidence that you were exposed. Describe your duties if they involved spraying, handling drums, or clearing vegetation. See Proving Vietnam service for how VA decides where you served. A medical opinion about you. The strongest opinions explain, in the doctor's own reasoning, why your exposure is \"at least as likely as not\" a cause, and they address your own history: when and how the condition appeared; other risk factors, such as smoking, family history, sun exposure, or work after service, and why they do or do not explain it better; medical or scientific literature, including studies newer than the government's reviews. Everything else that fits. Treatment records, your own statement of symptoms and when they began, and statements from people who knew you. A general article or study by itself is usually given less weight than an opinion that applies it to your facts. See ClaimReady's guides to medical opinions and talking with your doctor. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5107(b); 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102, 3.303(a), (d), 3.307(a)(6)(iii) Court Holton v. Shinseki, 557 F.3d 1363 (Fed. Cir. 2009)"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#exam","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"Will VA order an exam?","text":"Since the PACT Act, VA generally must give you a toxic exposure exam and medical opinion when you have a disability, evidence of a toxic exposure risk activity (TERA) in service, and not enough evidence yet to grant. The law has one exception: VA does not have to when it finds \"no indication of an association\" between the disability and the exposure. VA guidance VA treats this list as one of those exceptions, but only when herbicides are your only established toxic exposure. So VA may decide a claim for a listed condition without ordering a toxic exposure opinion. Your evidence can change that. VA's guidance says VA still considers an opinion when you submit competent medical or scientific evidence that suggests a link in your case, such as a doctor's letter. Other exposures count. If you also had another TERA, such as burn pits or jet fuel, the herbicide exception does not cover that exposure. VA's general duty to get an exam still applies whenever the evidence indicates your condition may be related to service. Courts have called that a low threshold. Either way, if VA denies the claim, the decision must say which element was not met. If an exam was skipped because of an exception, the decision should say so. Law 38 U.S.C. §§ 1168(a)-(c), 1710(e)(4), 5103A(d), 5104(b) Court McLendon v. Nicholson, 20 Vet. App. 79 (2006) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.1.g; VA's PACT Act claims guidance More on TERA exams"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#denied","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"If you were denied","text":"Look closely at the reasons. Two problems come up often with conditions on this list. Only the presumption was addressed If the decision explains why the condition is not presumptive but never discusses a direct link, it may not have done the full analysis. The Federal Circuit held that when a presumption does not apply, VA must still review the claim on a direct basis. The opinion says \"not on VA's list\" The Veterans Court has found opinions inadequate when they rest only on the fact that VA does not presume the link, or only on the condition's category in the national science reviews, without looking at the veteran's own risk factors and history. Your options include a Higher-Level Review, a Supplemental Claim with new and relevant evidence (such as a new medical opinion), or an appeal to the Board. Deadlines apply. See Supplemental Claims, and talk with an accredited representative, who can help at no cost. Court Combee v. Brown, 34 F.3d 1039, 1043-44 (Fed. Cir. 1994); Stefl v. Nicholson, 21 Vet. App. 120, 123-24 (2007); Polovick v. Shinseki, 23 Vet. App. 48, 52-55 (2009) Law 38 U.S.C. §§ 5104B, 5108, 7105"},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#example","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look","text":"Fictional example An Army veteran served in Vietnam in 1969 and was diagnosed with colon cancer in 2021. The veteran never smoked and has no family history of colon cancer. Exposure: service in Vietnam is shown in the records, so herbicide exposure is presumed. The presumption: colorectal cancer is on the \"no positive association\" list, so VA does not presume the link. The exam: herbicides are the veteran's only established exposure, so VA might not order a toxic exposure opinion on its own. What could help: the veteran's oncologist writes a letter that reviews the veteran's history and risk factors, cites the literature, and explains the doctor's reasoning on whether the cancer is at least as likely as not related to the exposure. VA then weighs that letter with the rest of the record, and could order its own opinion. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"herbicide-no-association.html#myths","page":"Herbicides: No Positive Association","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"VA says Agent Orange doesn't cause my condition.\" Not quite. The finding is that the evidence across large groups of people was not strong enough for a presumption. For most of these conditions, the science reviews called the evidence inadequate or insufficient, not proof of no link. \"If it's not presumptive, there's no point filing.\" The law says presumptions do not prevent service connection shown by the evidence. Your claim is decided on a direct basis. \"VA has to give me an exam for every condition.\" Not always. For a condition on this list, with herbicides as your only exposure, VA's guidance may not require a toxic exposure opinion. Submitting your own medical evidence can change that. \"A study online proves my case.\" A study can help, but VA usually gives more weight to a doctor who applies the research to your own history. \"My cancer spread to my lungs, so it's presumed.\" Not if it started somewhere else. VA looks at the primary site. \"The list will never change.\" It has changed many times. Bladder cancer, hypothyroidism, and high blood pressure were all once on the \"no association\" side."},{"href":"medical-opinions.html","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"What makes a medical opinion count in a VA claim","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to what makes a medical opinion count in a VA claim, for veterans, doctors, lawyers, agents, and VSOs: rationale, adequacy, lay statements, speculation, and common problems. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"Many VA claims turn on a medical opinion: is a condition at least as likely as not related to service? VA does not count opinions; it weighs them, and most of an opinion's weight comes from its reasoning. This article explains what VA and the courts look for, for veterans reading an exam report, doctors writing an opinion, and lawyers, agents, and service officers reviewing one. Based on public VA information: the law at 38 U.S.C. §§ 5103A and 5107(b) and 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102 and 3.159, VA's procedures manual (M21-1, Part V, Subpart ii, Chapter 1), and published court decisions. For education only. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#basics","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"What a medical opinion does","text":"Service connection needs a current disability, something in service, and a link between them. A medical opinion most often speaks to the link. It can also speak to a diagnosis, when a condition began, whether it got worse, or how severe it is. The doctor gives medical facts and reasons. VA makes the legal decision. An opinion should not say a condition \"is service connected\"; it should say whether it was caused or worsened by something, and why. The standard is \"at least as likely as not.\" That means a 50 percent or greater probability. When the evidence for and against is about evenly balanced, the law gives the benefit of the doubt to the veteran. Phrase What it means to VA \"Is due to,\" \"caused by,\" \"more likely than not\" Supports a link, above 50 percent. \"At least as likely as not\" Supports a link, 50 percent or more. This meets the legal standard. \"Less likely than not\" Against a link, below 50 percent. \"Cannot be resolved without resorting to mere speculation\" Neither for nor against. Usable only if the examiner explains why, and what is missing. \"Could,\" \"may,\" \"possibly,\" with no reasons Usually given little weight on its own. ClaimReady note, from general knowledge of court decisions. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5107(b); 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.102, 3.303 VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.1; IV.i.2.A.7"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#anatomy","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"What a well-supported opinion includes","text":"VA's guidance says a rationale should cite the medical principles used, identify the evidence in the case it relied on, and show how the conclusion was reached. Courts treat an opinion like expert testimony: it should rest on enough facts, use reliable methods, and apply them to this person. Who is giving it. Credentials, specialty, and relationship to the patient. Treating doctors get no automatic extra weight, but knowing the history can add weight. What was reviewed. Records, tests, and literature. A private doctor does not have to review the VA claims file if the opinion rests on accurate, enough facts. The history, including the veteran's own account. What happened in service, symptoms since, and later events. An opinion based on the veteran's history is credible unless that history is shown to be wrong. The opinion, in VA's terms. One clear sentence using \"at least as likely as not\" or \"less likely than not,\" for each theory asked: caused by, and, if raised, made worse by. The reasons. The medical principles and how they apply to these facts. This is where most of the weight comes from. The other side. Why contrary evidence, other causes, or an earlier opinion does or does not change the conclusion. VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.3 Court Nieves-Rodriguez v. Peake, 22 Vet. App. 295 (2008); Stefl v. Nicholson, 21 Vet. App. 120 (2007); Kowalski v. Nicholson, 19 Vet. App. 171 (2005)"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#weigh","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"How VA weighs an opinion","text":"Rule In plain words Source Reasons, not conclusions A bare conclusion is not enough. The opinion must give an analysis VA can weigh against other opinions. Court Stefl; Nieves-Rodriguez Correct facts An opinion based on an inaccurate factual premise has no weight. Court Reonal v. Brown, 5 Vet. App. 458 (1993) Read as a whole A report is read in full. It does not have to discuss every record, and missing magic words alone do not sink it. Court Monzingo v. Shinseki, 26 Vet. App. 97 (2012) Not ambiguous VA's guidance asks for opinions that are clear and detailed enough to decide the issue. VA asks for clarification of ambiguous findings. VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.3; IV.i.3.C No medical judgment from the decision maker VA decision makers cannot reject an opinion based on their own medical view. They must point to medical evidence. Court Colvin v. Derwinski, 1 Vet. App. 171 (1991); Kahana v. Shinseki, 24 Vet. App. 428 (2011) Private equals VA Private evidence is not worth less than VA evidence because of where it came from. VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.3 An adequate exam once given If VA provides an exam, it must be adequate, even if VA did not have to provide one. Court Barr v. Nicholson, 21 Vet. App. 303 (2007)"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#lay","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Lay statements and missing records","text":"A veteran or witness is competent to describe what they saw, heard, or felt, including their own symptoms. VA must consider these statements. A statement is not unbelievable just because no medical record backs it up. An opinion that rests only on the absence of a record in service, and ignores the veteran's account of an injury, is inadequate. Absence of a record counts against a claim only if the fact would ordinarily have been recorded. VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.2; V.ii.1.B.2 Court Buchanan v. Nicholson, 451 F.3d 1331 (Fed. Cir. 2006); Dalton v. Nicholson, 21 Vet. App. 23 (2007)"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#spec","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"When an examiner says it can't be answered","text":"The phrase \"cannot be resolved without resorting to mere speculation\" is not a conclusion against the claim. It is no evidence either way. VA can rely on it only if the examiner explains why, or the reason is clear from the record: for example, that medicine cannot answer it, or that specific facts are missing. The examiner should show that all procurable, relevant information was considered. If a missing record or test is the problem, VA may be able to get it. VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.A.5; IV.i.3.A Court Jones v. Shinseki, 23 Vet. App. 382 (2010)"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#problems","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Common problems in medical opinions","text":"Problem Why it matters Source A conclusion with no reasons Nothing for VA to weigh. Stefl \"No record in service\" as the only reason Ignores the veteran's account. Dalton; Buchanan Wrong dates, wrong history, or the wrong condition An inaccurate premise has no weight. Reonal Answers \"caused by\" but not \"made worse by\" when aggravation was raised Secondary service connection includes both. § 3.310(b); El-Amin v. Shinseki, 26 Vet. App. 136 (2013) Normal hearing at separation as the only reason against hearing loss VA's exam guidance says this alone is not enough. M21-1 IV.i.3.B \"Speculation\" with no explanation Cannot be relied on. Jones Answers a legal question, such as whether a stressor is verified That is VA's job, not the examiner's. Sizemore v. Principi, 18 Vet. App. 264 (2004); M21-1 IV.i.2.A.7 A single conclusory sentence that service caused it May not even show a possible link for exam purposes. Waters v. Shinseki, 601 F.3d 1274 (Fed. Cir. 2010) Have an opinion to check? Try Check an exam opinion."},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#foryou","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Tips for each reader","text":"Veterans and families Download your exam report on VA.gov and read the opinion and rationale. Compare it to the problems table. Note page numbers. Write a statement with the facts the examiner missed, in your own words. If you ask your own doctor for an opinion, share this page and the reason VA denied the claim. Doctors and clinicians Say what you reviewed and how you know the patient. Give the opinion in VA's terms, for each theory raised. Explain the medical reasons. Cite literature where it helps. Address the earlier VA opinion's reasoning directly. Describe function and limits; leave legal labels to VA. Many DBQ forms are public. What VA asks your examiner Lawyers, agents, and VSOs An inadequate VA exam is a duty to assist issue a Higher-Level Review can send back. A new private opinion goes with a Supplemental Claim. Examiners are presumed competent; a challenge must be raised. You can ask for the examiner's qualifications. In a complex or disputed case, a written request for an independent medical opinion is possible. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.328, 3.2601(g) Court Francway v. Wilkie, 940 F.3d 1304 (Fed. Cir. 2019)"},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#doccheck","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Before you sign an opinion","text":"My credentials and how I know the patient are stated. I listed the records and tests I reviewed. The history, including the patient's account, is accurate. The opinion uses \"at least as likely as not\" or \"less likely than not.\" I explained the medical reasons for these facts. I addressed contrary evidence and any earlier opinion. If another condition is involved, I addressed both cause and worsening. It is signed and dated. This checklist is not saved. Print the page to keep it."},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#example","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Reading Dana's two exam opinions","text":"Fictional example Knee opinion: \"Service treatment records … show a single sick-call visit in 2007 for a left knee strain, which resolved with rest. There is no documentation of a chronic knee condition in service … The veteran did not seek treatment until 2019.\" It rests on the absence of records, and does not mention Dana's account of knee pain that continued after the 2007 injury. It does not explain medically why a 2007 strain could not lead to the current condition. Hearing opinion: \"Hearing was within normal limits at separation … Because hearing was normal at discharge, there is no nexus. It is possible that occupational noise after service contributed.\" Normal hearing at separation is the only reason given. \"It is possible\" is a speculative alternative with no reasons. These opinions were written for teaching. They are not real VA exam reports, and spotting a gap does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#myths","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"Two favorable opinions beat one negative one.\" No. VA weighs opinions by their reasoning, not their number. \"My treating doctor's opinion wins automatically.\" No. It gets no automatic extra weight, though knowing your history can help. \"A private doctor must review my whole VA file.\" No. What matters is whether the opinion rests on accurate, enough facts. \"'Without speculation' means VA said no.\" No. It counts neither way, and VA can rely on it only if the examiner explains it. \"A nexus letter only needs one sentence.\" No. A conclusion without reasons carries little weight."},{"href":"medical-opinions.html#related","page":"Medical Opinions Guide","kind":"Guide","title":"Related on ClaimReady","text":"Supplemental Claims: what new evidence does What VA asks your examiner C&P exams: the complete guide and Toxic exposure exams (TERA) How VA weighs evidence Check an exam opinion"},{"href":"pn-checker.html","page":"Nerve Findings Checker","kind":"Tool","title":"Nerve findings checker","sub":"An independent, educational checker that lines up peripheral nerve exam findings with the 38 CFR 4.124a criteria and M21-1 guidance. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"Enter the objective findings from a Peripheral Nerves exam, one nerve and one limb at a time. The checker shows which severity level in 38 CFR 4.124a those findings line up with, which findings point higher or lower, and the nerve's published criteria. It doesn't decide a rating. Nothing you enter is saved or sent."},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Your PTSD stressor: how VA accepts that it happened","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to how VA accepts that a PTSD stressor happened: combat, fear of hostile activity, drone crews, POWs, non-combat events, credible supporting evidence, and the details to give VA. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"To service connect PTSD, VA must accept that the stressful event you describe really happened during service. VA calls this conceding the stressor. The rules depend on what kind of event it was. For some kinds, your own statement can be enough. For others, VA needs something more. This article explains each kind in plain English, so you know what VA looks for and what you can give it. Based on public VA information: the regulation 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f) and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part VIII, Subpart iv, Chapter 1, Section A, Topic 3 (topic changed Jun 30, 2025 ; article updated Mar 17, 2026 ). For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#basics","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Three things VA needs for PTSD","text":"A diagnosis of PTSD that meets the standard VA uses (DSM-5). A medical link between your current symptoms and an event in service. Credible supporting evidence that the event happened. This article is about this third part. VA decides whether the event happened. The examiner decides whether the event is enough to support a PTSD diagnosis and whether your symptoms are related to it. You do not need a diagnosis first. VA's guidance says a PTSD diagnosis is not required before VA starts checking a stressor. There is no special form required to claim a stressor. VA can learn about it from VA Form 21-0781, a written statement, your military records, your medical records, or what you tell an examiner. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f); § 4.125(a) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.a, d"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#own-words","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"When your own statement can be enough","text":"For some kinds of stressors, the law lets your own account establish that the event happened, without other proof. Two conditions apply every time: The event must be consistent with your service: the circumstances, conditions, or hardships of that service, or for a fear-based or drone stressor, the places, types, and circumstances of your service. There must be no clear and convincing evidence to the contrary. Kind of stressor Can your statement alone be enough? Source PTSD diagnosed in service Yes, if the event is related to that service and consistent with it. Law § 3.304(f)(1) Combat with the enemy Yes, if the event is related to combat and consistent with that service. Law § 3.304(f)(2) Former prisoner of war Yes, if the event is related to being a POW and consistent with it. Law § 3.304(f)(4) Fear of hostile military or terrorist activity Yes, with one more step: a VA psychiatrist or psychologist, or one under contract with VA, must confirm the event is adequate to support a PTSD diagnosis and your symptoms are related to it. Law § 3.304(f)(3) Armed drone crew duty Yes, with the same step as fear-based stressors. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.b, l Other events (non-combat) No. VA needs credible supporting evidence beyond your statement. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.g, m Personal assault, including MST Separate rules. Evidence from outside military records and behavior changes can help. Law § 3.304(f)(5) Drone crews: the regulation itself names fear of hostile activity, not drone duty. VA's procedures manual applies the same approach to armed drone crews. The fear-based rule took effect July 13, 2010; claims decided before then could not use it. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f)(1)–(5); § 3.1(y) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.b"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#combat","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Combat stressors","text":"Engaging in combat with the enemy means personally taking part in an actual fight or encounter with a military foe, hostile unit, or enemy weapon. It includes being present as a fighter or as someone doing duty in support of fighters, such as giving medical care to the wounded. What helps VA accept it A combat decoration (list below). VA presumes combat unless clear and convincing evidence shows otherwise. Other credible evidence of combat. A decoration is not the only acceptable proof. If you have a combat decoration but did not describe the event, VA's guidance says to assume the stressor is combat related and request your personnel records. Other records that can show combat Unit and organizational histories Hostile fire pay or hazard pay records Records of your military job (occupation) Statements from people who served with you Air Force Achievement Medal with \"V\" Air Force Combat Action Medal Air Force Commendation Medal with \"V\" Air Force Cross Air Medal with \"V\" Army Commendation Medal with \"V\" Bronze Star Medal with \"V\" \"C\" device on other meritorious awards Combat Action Badge Combat Action Ribbon Navy Achievement Medal with \"V\" (before Feb 1969, in place of the Combat Action Ribbon) Combat Aircrew Insignia Combat Infantryman Badge Combat Medical Badge Distinguished Flying Cross Distinguished Service Cross Fleet Marine Force Combat Operations Insignia Joint Service Commendation Medal with \"V\" Medal of Honor Navy Commendation Medal with \"V\" Navy Cross Parachutist Badge with Combat Jump Device Purple Heart Silver Star Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(d), (f)(2); VAOPGCPREC 12-1999 VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.1.f; 1.A.3.e, h, j"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#fear","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Fear of hostile military or terrorist activity","text":"This covers service where an attack was always possible, even if you were never in a firefight. You experienced, witnessed, or were confronted with an event or situation involving actual or threatened death or serious injury, or a threat to your own or someone else's physical safety, and you reacted with fear, helplessness, or horror. Improvised explosive device (IED) Vehicle-borne explosive Incoming artillery, rocket, or mortar fire Grenade Small arms fire, including suspected sniper fire Attack on friendly military aircraft What helps VA accept it Records (such as your DD Form 214) showing you served in an area of potential hostile activity. A VA or VA-contracted psychiatrist or psychologist confirming the event is adequate for a PTSD diagnosis and your symptoms are related to it. VA's guidance says to request your personnel records before or with the exam. Medals that point to the place Generally show it: Vietnam Service or Campaign Medal, Kuwait Liberation Medal, Iraq Campaign Medal, Afghanistan Campaign Medal. Generally do not: National Defense Service Medal, Armed Forces Service Medal, Global War on Terrorism Service Medal. They do not name an area. Not by itself: the Global War on Terrorism Expeditionary Medal. VA weighs it with other evidence. Along the Korean demilitarized zone (DMZ), hostile since the 1953 armistice. Service on U.S. bases elsewhere in South Korea generally has not been. Aboard ship in the offshore waters of Vietnam, or in Thailand, when you received the Vietnam Service Medal or Vietnam Campaign Medal. The list is not complete. VA looks at each case. What does not meet the rule: worrying about a future deployment, or learning that someone died far away in a hostile area. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f)(3) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.f, i, k"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#drone","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Armed drone aircraft crew duty","text":"VA's guidance treats duty as a crew member of an armed drone aircraft, such as the Predator or Reaper, the same way as a fear-based stressor. What helps: your DD Form 214 or other service records showing that duty. A VA or VA-contracted psychiatrist or psychologist must confirm the stressor is adequate and your symptoms are related to it. VA's guidance says to request your personnel records before or with the exam. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.b, l"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#pow","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Former POWs, and PTSD diagnosed during service","text":"Former prisoners of war: your own account can establish an event related to your captivity if it fits the circumstances of that service. \"Prisoner of war\" has a legal definition in 38 C.F.R. § 3.1(y). ClaimReady's Former POWs article covers the presumptions that also apply. PTSD diagnosed in service: if a provider diagnosed PTSD while you were serving, your account of a related in-service event can be enough. Your in-service mental health records matter here; they may not be stored with your other medical records, so tell VA where you were treated. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f)(1), (4); § 3.1(y) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.b"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#noncombat","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Non-combat events","text":"These are stressful events that were not combat and not fear of enemy activity. VA's guidance says they always need credible supporting evidence; your statement alone is not enough. Natural disasters A life-threatening illness of you or someone close to you Burn ward duty Graves registration duty Seeing someone harmed, not by the enemy Ship sinkings, explosions, or plane crashes in routine drills Friendly fire on a gunnery range in training Jump to what counts as credible supporting evidence and the details to give VA. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.g, m"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#mst","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Assault and military sexual trauma (MST)","text":"Claims based on personal assault, including MST, follow separate rules. They are built around the fact that many assaults are never reported. Evidence from outside military records, and changes in behavior after the event, can support the claim. VA must tell you about these other kinds of evidence before it denies the claim. ClaimReady's PTSD and MST article explains these rules, including the behavior changes VA calls \"markers.\" VA provides free care for conditions related to MST, whether or not you file a claim or have records of the event. If talking about this is hard right now, you can reach the Veterans Crisis Line any time: dial 988 and press 1. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.304(f)(5) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.B"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#evidence","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"What \"credible supporting evidence\" means","text":"When your own statement cannot establish the stressor by itself, VA looks for evidence that, taken together: documents that you took part in the event, shows you served in the immediate area at the time it happened, and supports your description of what happened. Good news in VA's guidance Not every detail has to be proven. Evidence can be enough if it implies you were personally exposed, such as records showing your unit came under attack. VA looks at the evidence as a whole. The evidence does not have to come from service records. Kinds of evidence that can help Statements from people who served with you (buddy statements) Letters or emails home, journals, and photos from the time Unit histories, ship logs, and after-action or accident reports News reports of the event Police, hospital, or civilian medical records Your service personnel and medical records VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.1.f; 1.A.3.c, m"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#details","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"Give VA enough to look it up","text":"When VA has to search military records for your event, its guidance says it needs at least four details. Use this as a checklist when you write your statement. Your checks are not saved. Minimum details for a records search What happened An event that records could document. One event per paragraph. Where it happened Base, town, province, ship, or grid area. When, within about two months For example, \"between March and April 2006.\" An exact date is not required. Your unit at that time Down to the company, battery, squadron, or ship if you can. If someone was killed or wounded, their name and unit help, but VA's guidance says they are not required if you describe the event. For well-known events, such as the Tet Offensive, VA can fill in the date and place itself. If VA writes to ask for more details, answer it. VA's guidance allows 30 days for a reply before the claim moves on. VA Form 21-0781 is the form VA uses to collect these details, but a clear written statement can also work. VA's guidance says some events are not sent for military records research because records would not show them, such as events that almost happened and mistreatment of enemy prisoners. That does not mean the event did not matter. The rules above about your own statement, and other kinds of evidence, can still apply. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.d, o, p"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#steps","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"How VA reviews a stressor, in plain steps","text":"VA works out what kind of event it is. It reads everything: your statement or 21-0781, service records, medical records, and other evidence. VA asks whether your statement alone is enough. For combat, POW, in-service diagnosis, fear-based, and drone stressors, it can be. If so, VA moves to the exam step. If not, VA gathers the evidence you pointed to and searches what is available. This is where buddy statements and records you name matter most. If it still is not confirmed, VA checks whether you gave the four details. If yes, VA can ask for a deeper search of military unit records. If not, VA asks you for them. VA's rating staff makes the final call on whether the stressor is conceded. VA decides whether an exam is needed. See C&P exams: the complete guide. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.1.A.3.c–d"},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#example","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look for Dana","text":"Fictional example Dana served in Iraq in 2006 and received the Iraq Campaign Medal. Dana describes repeated mortar attacks on the base. Dana also describes seeing a fellow soldier badly hurt in a vehicle rollover during training back in the United States. The mortar attacks are a fear-based stressor. Dana's service records place Dana in Iraq. If a VA or VA-contracted psychologist confirms the event is adequate for a PTSD diagnosis and Dana's symptoms are related to it, Dana's own statement can be enough. The training rollover is a non-combat event. Dana's statement alone is not enough. A buddy statement from someone in the unit and the accident report could provide credible supporting evidence. Dana gives the base, the approximate months, and the unit. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"ptsd-stressor.html#myths","page":"Your PTSD Stressor","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"I need a combat medal.\" No. A decoration is strong evidence of combat, but VA's guidance says it is not the only acceptable proof. \"Every detail has to be proven.\" No. Corroboration of every detail is not required, and evidence can be enough if it implies you were personally exposed. \"If my service records don't mention it, it's over.\" No. Supporting evidence can come from sources other than service records. \"I need a diagnosis before VA checks my stressor.\" No. VA's guidance says a diagnosis is not a prerequisite to start checking. \"Being deployed proves my PTSD.\" Not by itself. Service in a hostile area can support a fear-based stressor, but VA still needs a diagnosis, a VA or contract psychiatrist's or psychologist's confirmation, and a link to your symptoms."},{"href":"roadmap.html","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Your claim roadmap: the basics","sub":"Free, independent roadmap of a VA disability claim in seven stages: find your stage, the one thing that matters most there with the public law behind it, and where to start on ClaimReady.","text":"A VA disability claim moves through seven stages. Find yours to see the one thing that matters most there and where to start. Each point shows the public law or VA guidance behind it, or says when it is a general tip. For the rules explained in more depth, see Your claim, step by step."},{"href":"roadmap.html#stages","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Leaving the military","text":"Many veterans are in two stages at once, such as rated for one condition and filing for another. Use the stage that fits each condition."},{"href":"roadmap.html#s1","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Leaving the military","text":"1 You are here if: You have 180 days or less left in service, or you separated within the last year. Most important: A claim received within one year after separation can take an effective date of the day after you left service. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.400(b)(2)(i) Start here: Intent to file →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s2","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Getting ready to file","text":"2 You are here if: You have a condition you believe is related to service, and you have not filed for it yet. Most important: Send an intent to file. It gives you up to one year to file the full claim and can protect an earlier effective date. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.155(b) Start here: Claim checklist →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s3","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Filing your claim","text":"3 You are here if: You are ready to send your claim, or you are filling out the form now. Most important: File on VA Form 21-526EZ, complete and signed. An unsigned form is not a complete claim. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.155; 38 C.F.R. § 3.160 VA guidance VA Form 21-526EZ instructions Start here: Form 21-526EZ guide →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s4","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Your C&P exam","text":"4 You are here if: VA scheduled a compensation and pension (C&P) exam, or you expect one. Most important: Go to the exam, or contact VA before it to reschedule. Missing it without good cause can lead VA to decide on the record it has, or to deny the claim. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.655 Start here: C&P exams: the complete guide →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s5","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"Your decision arrives","text":"5 You are here if: VA sent you a decision letter or rating decision. Most important: Note the decision date. Most review options keep your effective date only if filed within one year of it. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h) Start here: Decode your decision letter →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s6","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"If you disagree","text":"6 You are here if: You disagree with all or part of a decision, or think a rating is too low. Most important: File within one year of the decision to keep your original effective date. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h) Start here: Lesson 6: You got a decision →"},{"href":"roadmap.html#s7","page":"Claim Roadmap Basics","kind":"Guide","title":"After your rating","text":"7 You are here if: You have one or more service-connected conditions and want to know what else to consider. Most important: Ask about benefits beyond the percentage: Individual Unemployability, special monthly compensation, a temporary 100% rating, and family benefits. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.16; 38 C.F.R. § 3.350; 38 C.F.R. § 4.29; 38 C.F.R. § 4.30 Start here: Lesson 7: Other benefits →"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Somatic symptom disorder, ADHD and other neurodevelopmental disorders, and insomnia: how VA decides service connection","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to how VA handles somatic symptom disorder, ADHD, autism and other neurodevelopmental disorders, and insomnia: what can be service connected, the exceptions, and how each is rated. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"These three groups of conditions are handled differently from most mental health claims. Somatic symptom disorder is accepted as a disability and rated on what it actually causes. Neurodevelopmental disorders such as ADHD and autism generally cannot be service connected directly, with two exceptions. Insomnia is usually treated as a symptom of another condition, and only rarely as its own rated disability. This article explains each rule, the exceptions, and what the medical evidence needs to show. Based on public sources: 38 U.S.C. § 1111, 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.303(c), 3.306, 3.310, 4.14, 4.20, 4.125(a), 4.126(d), 4.127, and 4.130, published court decisions, and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part V, Subpart iii, 13.1.i, j, and k. Medical background that does not come from VA is marked. For education only. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#start","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Three rules in one page","text":"Somatic symptom disorder is a disability VA accepts it for compensation and rates it under the code that best fits what it causes. Neurodevelopmental disorders usually are not ADHD, autism, and similar conditions begin in childhood, so VA generally does not service connect them directly. An in-service injury or abnormal worsening in service can change that. Insomnia usually belongs to another condition If something else causes it, the sleep problems are counted in that condition's rating. A separate rating is only for insomnia disorder with no other cause. What brings you here? Somatic symptom disorder Or chronic pain syndrome, fibromyalgia ADHD, autism, or a learning disorder Or another childhood-onset condition Insomnia or trouble sleeping On its own or with another condition I'm reading a decision And want to check how it was handled Somatic symptom disorder. VA accepts it as a disability. What matters most is medical evidence that describes the specific symptoms and limits it causes, because VA rates it under the code that fits that picture. Somatic symptom disorder · Physical and mental symptoms together A neurodevelopmental disorder. VA generally does not grant service connection for the condition itself. Look at whether an in-service injury such as a TBI caused the diagnosis, whether it worsened at an abnormally high rate in service, and whether another mental disorder developed on top of it. Neurodevelopmental disorders · The exceptions · A separate condition on top Insomnia. First ask what causes it. If a service-connected condition such as depression, PTSD, or pain causes it, the sleep problems belong in that condition's rating. A separate rating needs a DSM-5 diagnosis of insomnia disorder, a link to service, and no other cause. Insomnia · The two paths Reading a decision. Check which code was used and why, whether the decision explains that insomnia symptoms were counted in another rating, and whether the exceptions for neurodevelopmental disorders were considered when the evidence raised them. Questions to ask about a decision · Common misunderstandings"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#somatic","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Somatic symptom disorder","text":"Somatic symptom disorder involves physical symptoms, such as pain, along with distress or excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviors about those symptoms. VA's manual says it is widely recognized as disabling and is accepted as a disability for compensation purposes. What VA's manual says It can come from an underlying disease, such as multiple sclerosis or arthritis. Variations of it can show up in every body system. So VA rates it under the most appropriate diagnostic code for the clinical picture, which may be a mental disorder code or a physical one. VA already recognizes fibromyalgia and low back pain syndrome, which the manual calls forms of somatic symptom disorder. DSM-5 replaced the older diagnosis of chronic pain syndrome with somatic symptom disorder. What the evidence needs to show VA's manual says adequate medical evidence must identify the specific manifestations present. A diagnosis alone is not enough to rate it accurately. Which symptoms you have, and where in the body How often and how severe they are How they affect work, daily activities, and relationships Any underlying disease the symptoms come from The mental disorder part of the rating schedule lists somatic symptom disorder as diagnostic code 9421, with related codes 9422 to 9425 (other specified and unspecified somatic symptom disorders, conversion disorder, and illness anxiety disorder). Those codes use the General Rating Formula for Mental Disorders, which looks at occupational and social impairment. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.130, DCs 9421–9425 VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.13.1.i"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#overlap","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"When one condition has physical and mental symptoms","text":"Because somatic symptom disorder sits between the body and the mind, the same symptoms can look like a physical condition or a mental one. The rating rules prevent counting them twice. Rate the dominant part. When a single disability is diagnosed both as a physical condition and as a mental disorder, VA uses the code for the dominant, more disabling aspect. No double counting. The same disability, or the same symptoms, cannot be rated twice under different diagnoses. This rule is called the rule against pyramiding. Separate problems can still be rated separately. If a physical condition and a mental condition cause different symptoms, each can have its own rating. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 4.14, 4.126(d) VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.13.1.h (referenced in 13.1.i)"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#neuro","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"ADHD, autism, and other neurodevelopmental disorders","text":"Neurodevelopmental disorders begin during the developmental period. DSM-5 describes them as typically showing up early in life, with developmental deficits in several areas of functioning. VA's manual lists these as examples, but the group is not limited to them: Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD, also called ADD) Autism spectrum disorder Specific learning disorder Tic disorder Childhood-onset fluency disorder (stuttering) Intellectual developmental disorder (intellectual disability) VA treats neurodevelopmental disorders as not \"diseases or injuries\" under 38 C.F.R. § 3.303(c), so they are generally not service connected on a direct basis. The regulation itself says congenital or developmental defects, personality disorders, and mental deficiency are not diseases or injuries for compensation purposes. VA's manual applies that rule to the whole group of neurodevelopmental disorders. A separate regulation, § 4.127, says the same for intellectual disability specifically. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.303(c), 4.127 VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.13.1.j"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#exceptions","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Two exceptions in VA's manual","text":"\"Generally not\" is not \"never.\" VA's manual names two situations where service connection should be considered. An in-service injury caused the diagnosis. If the evidence clearly shows the diagnosis developed because of an injury in service, such as a traumatic brain injury (TBI), VA considers service connection for any diagnosis directly related to that injury. Example: attention and concentration problems that first appear after a documented head injury in service, with a medical opinion linking them to the TBI. The condition worsened at an abnormally high rate during service. If it progressed faster than its normal course while you were in service, VA considers service connection by aggravation under 38 C.F.R. § 3.306. Under § 3.306, a preexisting condition that increases in severity during service is presumed aggravated unless the increase is shown to be its natural progress. The presumption of soundness By law, a veteran is presumed to have been in sound condition at entry, except for conditions noted on the entry exam. VA can rebut that only with clear and unmistakable evidence that the condition existed before service and was not aggravated by it. VA's manual points to Horn v. Shinseki (2012) for this topic. Board decisions cite Horn for the two parts of that test, called the preexistence prong and the aggravation prong, and for the meaning of \"clear and unmistakable\": evidence that is undebatable. What to look for in your records Was the condition noted on your entry exam, or not? Is there a documented head injury or other injury in service, and did new symptoms follow it? Do service records show the condition getting much worse, such as new treatment, duty limits, or a medical board? Does a doctor explain whether the change was faster than the condition's normal course? Existed before service · Traumatic brain injury · Medical opinions Law 38 U.S.C. § 1111; 38 C.F.R. § 3.306 Court Horn v. Shinseki, 25 Vet. App. 231 (2012) VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.13.1.j; V.ii.2.C and E; V.iii.12.B"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#superimposed","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"A separate condition on top of a neurodevelopmental disorder","text":"Even when the neurodevelopmental disorder itself cannot be service connected, a different condition that develops in or because of service is its own claim. For intellectual disability, the regulation says disability from a mental disorder superimposed on it may be service connected. A veteran with ADHD who develops depression or PTSD from service can claim the depression or PTSD. The ADHD diagnosis does not block it. A TBI, PTSD, or other service-connected condition is evaluated on its own symptoms, so the record should separate which symptoms come from which condition where a doctor can do that. From ClaimReady's research, not the M21-1 text Board decisions also rely on VA General Counsel opinions (VAOPGCPREC 82-90 and 67-90) that separate congenital or developmental defects, which are generally stable, from diseases, which can improve or worsen, and say a defect can be subject to a superimposed disease or injury. VA's manual does not cite those opinions in this section. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.127 Background VAOPGCPREC 82-90, 67-90, as described in published Board decisions"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#insomnia","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Insomnia: a symptom or its own condition?","text":"VA's manual says to consider the evidence carefully. Insomnia is generally a symptom of another disability. It can occur on its own, but it is often part of: Another mental disorder For example, major depressive disorder or PTSD A medical condition For example, pain that keeps you awake Another sleep disorder For example, a breathing-related sleep disorder such as sleep apnea Chronic sleep impairment is already one of the symptoms listed in the General Rating Formula for Mental Disorders, at the 30% level. That is one reason sleep problems caused by a mental disorder are counted inside that disorder's rating. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.130 VA guidance M21-1 V.iii.13.1.k"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#paths","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"The two paths for insomnia","text":"VA's manual uses a simple table. Which path applies depends on whether something else causes the insomnia. Path A · If Insomnia is caused by, or is a symptom of, another service-connected condition No separate rating for insomnia on a secondary basis. VA service connects the underlying condition, if it is not already. The insomnia symptoms are included in the rating for that underlying condition. The decision should explain that insomnia was counted there because that condition causes it. No rating under a mental disorder code just for insomnia, unless the condition causing it is another mental disorder with a DSM-5 diagnosis. Path B · If all three are true Insomnia disorder on its own, linked to service A current DSM-5 diagnosis of insomnia disorder, meaning it is not tied to another disease or injury An event in service, such as a diagnosis of primary insomnia or insomnia disorder during service A medical link (nexus) between today's diagnosis and that event Then: service connection on a direct basis, rated by analogy under a mental disorder code in § 4.130, because the schedule has no code of its own for insomnia. A separate rating for insomnia disorder is only warranted if all other possible causes are ruled out and it is service connected directly. DSM-5 renamed \"primary insomnia\" to \"insomnia disorder.\" VA's manual reads both versions as requiring that other causes be ruled out, so a valid diagnosis means the insomnia is not caused by, or secondary to, another condition. Medical background, not from VA DSM-5 says insomnia disorder is not adequately explained by coexisting conditions, and it also lets a clinician note insomnia disorder alongside another mental or medical condition when the insomnia needs attention on its own. So a medical record may list insomnia disorder next to depression or pain. How that fits VA's two paths is a question for the examiner's explanation and for VA. Ask your doctor to explain what is causing your insomnia. When a condition is not listed in the rating schedule, VA may rate it under a closely related condition where the functions affected, the part of the body, and the symptoms are closely analogous. The rule avoids guesswork analogies and analogous ratings for conditions of doubtful diagnosis or not fully supported by clinical findings. For insomnia disorder, that means a mental disorder code and the General Rating Form"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#dsm","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Why the DSM-5 diagnosis matters","text":"For any mental disorder, VA requires a diagnosis that follows DSM-5 and is supported by the exam findings. If it is not, the regulation says VA returns the report to the examiner to support the diagnosis. That rule is why the exact wording matters in all three parts of this article: a DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder, a neurodevelopmental diagnosis, and a DSM-5 insomnia disorder each lead to a different result. Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.125(a)"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#check","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"Questions to ask about a decision","text":"Somatic symptom disorder: Does the medical evidence describe the specific symptoms, and does the code chosen fit that clinical picture? If the same condition has physical and mental symptoms, was the dominant part rated, without counting symptoms twice? Neurodevelopmental disorder: If the record shows a head injury or other injury in service, or a sharp worsening in service, did the decision consider those exceptions? Was a separate mental disorder on top of it considered on its own? Insomnia, Path A: If insomnia was not rated separately, does the decision say which condition it was counted in, and is that condition service connected? Insomnia, Path B: Is there a DSM-5 diagnosis of insomnia disorder, an in-service event, and a medical opinion that explains the link and why other causes were ruled out? Diagnosis: Does the exam give a DSM-5 diagnosis supported by its findings? Secondary service connection · Medical opinions · Supplemental Claims"},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#example","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look","text":"Fictional example 1: insomnia An Army veteran is service connected for PTSD and also claims insomnia. The examiner explains that the sleep problems are part of the PTSD. This is Path A. No separate insomnia rating. The sleep problems are counted in the PTSD rating under the General Rating Formula, and the decision explains that. Fictional example 2: ADHD A Marine veteran was diagnosed with ADHD as a child, and it was not noted at entry. In service, the veteran had a documented TBI from a blast, followed by new memory and concentration problems. The ADHD itself is generally not service connected. VA considers service connection for any diagnosis directly related to the TBI, and a medical opinion helps separate TBI symptoms from long-standing ADHD symptoms. These are made-up examples to show how the rules fit together. They are not real VA decisions and do not predict any outcome."},{"href":"somatic-neurodevelopmental-insomnia.html#myths","page":"Somatic, ADHD, and Insomnia Claims","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"Somatic symptom disorder isn't a real disability for VA.\" VA's manual says it is accepted as a disability for compensation. It is rated under the code that best fits the symptoms. \"ADHD can never be connected to service.\" Generally not directly, but VA considers it when an in-service injury caused the diagnosis or the condition worsened at an abnormally high rate in service. A separate mental disorder on top of it is its own claim. \"I should get a separate rating for insomnia on top of my PTSD rating.\" Usually not. When another condition causes insomnia, the sleep problems are counted in that condition's rating. \"Insomnia can't be service connected at all.\" It can, on a direct basis, when there is a DSM-5 insomnia disorder with no other cause, an event in service, and a medical link. \"Insomnia from back pain gets its own mental health rating.\" VA's manual says no mental disorder code just for insomnia when it is secondary to another disability, unless that disability is itself a DSM-5 mental disorder."},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"When your spouse needs daily help: extra VA compensation for you","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to extra VA compensation when a veteran rated 30% or more has a spouse who needs aid and attendance: the 30% rule, the three tests, evidence, effective dates, and common mistakes. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"If you get VA disability compensation and your spouse needs another person's help with daily life, is nearly blind, or lives in a nursing home, VA can pay you an extra monthly amount. This is often called special monthly spouse's compensation, or spousal aid and attendance (A&A). It is easy to miss because it depends on your spouse's health, not yours. This article explains the two things that must be true, what evidence helps, and when the extra pay can start. Based on public sources: 38 U.S.C. § 1115, 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.4(b)(2), 3.351, 3.352(a), and 3.401(a)(3), VA's published rate tables and forms, and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part VIII, Subpart iv, Chapter 4, Section A, Topic 12. For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes, but VA decides every claim. Print or save this article"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#start","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"Two things must be true","text":"The extra pay goes to you, the veteran, \"by reason of the veteran's spouse being in need of aid and attendance.\" Your spouse's condition does not have to be related to your service, or to any service. You are rated 30% or more. Your combined service-connected rating must be at least 30%. You do not need to be rated 100%. Below 30%, VA does not pay extra for any dependents, including this. Your spouse meets one of three tests. Nearly blind, a patient in a nursing home because of mental or physical incapacity, or a factual need for another person's regular help. See the three tests. Your spouse also needs to be on your award as a dependent. If you are already paid at the \"with spouse\" rate, that part is done. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1115; 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.4(b)(2), 3.351(a)(2) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.4.A.12.a–b"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#thirty","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"The 30% requirement","text":"The law allows extra compensation for a spouse, children, or dependent parents only when the veteran is entitled to compensation for disability \"evaluated as 30 per centum or more disabling.\" VA's manual applies the same line to the spouse aid and attendance amount. What counts Your combined rating for all service-connected conditions, not any single condition. Any rating from 30% to 100%. The added amount is larger at higher ratings. What does not change it Whether your spouse's condition has anything to do with military service. Whether you already get special monthly compensation for yourself. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1115; 38 C.F.R. § 3.4(b)(2) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.4.A.12.b"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#tests","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"The three tests for your spouse","text":"Your spouse needs to meet any one of these. They are the same tests VA uses for a veteran's own aid and attendance. Vision: blind or nearly blind Corrected visual acuity of 5/200 or less in both eyes, or concentric contraction of the visual field to 5 degrees or less. In plain terms, 5/200 means seeing at 5 feet what a person with normal vision sees at 200 feet, even with glasses. A 5-degree field is a very narrow tunnel of sight. Nursing home: a patient because of incapacity A patient in a nursing home because of mental or physical incapacity. For VA, a nursing home means a state-licensed facility giving skilled or intermediate nursing care, a nursing home unit in an approved State Veterans Home, or a VA nursing home unit. Assisted living or a group home may not fit this definition; if so, the factual need test can still apply. Factual need: regular help from another person Disabilities that create an actual need for another person's regular aid and attendance, judged by the factors below. Cannot dress or undress, or keep ordinarily clean and presentable. Often needs help adjusting a prosthetic or orthopedic device that they cannot adjust alone. (Ordinary items like supports, belts, or lacing at the back do not count.) Cannot feed themselves because of lost coordination of the arms or hands, or extreme weakness. Cannot attend to the \"wants of nature\" (toileting) without help. Needs regular care or help to stay safe from the hazards or dangers of daily life because of a physical or mental condition, such as dementia. Bedridden, meaning the condition itself requires staying in bed. Choosing to rest in bed, or bed rest a doctor prescribes to help recovery, does not count. Not every factor has to be present. VA looks at the functions your spouse cannot do along with their whole condition. The need must be regular; it does not have to be constant, around the clock. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.351(b)–(c), 3.352(a), 3.1(z) (nursing home) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.4.A.12.a"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#evidence","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"What evidence helps","text":"VA decides based on medical evidence about your spouse. Your spouse is not a veteran, so the evidence usually comes from their own doctors. Make sure your spouse is on your award. If not, add them as a dependent (VA uses VA Form 21-686c for this). Ask your spouse's provider to complete VA Form 21-2680. It is VA's form for an aid and attendance or housebound exam, and it has a box for a spouse claimant. You fill out the first sections; a medical doctor, doctor of osteopathy, physician assistant, or advanced practice registered nurse fills out the exam sections. If your spouse is in a nursing home, send proof that they are a patient there and why, such as a statement from the facility. VA can grant this once it confirms the nursing home status. VA may ask for more. If the evidence is not enough to decide, VA can request an examination of your spouse. What the doctor should include Each daily task your spouse needs help with, how often, and why (the diagnosis behind it). Safety: whether your spouse can be left alone, and what hazards they cannot handle, such as the stove, medications, or wandering. For vision: corrected visual acuity in each eye, and visual field results if the field is narrowed. For a nursing home: that your spouse is a patient there because of mental or physical incapacity. Law 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.351(c), 3.352(a) VA forms VA Form 21-2680 (Jun 2026 edition); VA Form 21-686c VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.4.A.12.a (note and references)"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#when","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"When the extra pay can start","text":"The general rule is the later of two dates: the date VA receives the claim, or the date your spouse's entitlement arose (when the need for aid and attendance began). There is one helpful exception. If VA grants your own disability compensation back to a date before your claim was received, the spouse amount can also be paid for any part of that earlier period in which your spouse's need for aid and attendance is shown. Fictional example Dana is rated 50%. Dana's spouse moved into a state-licensed nursing home in March because of advanced Parkinson's disease. Dana files in August with a statement from the facility. Under the general rule, the earliest the extra pay can start is the August date VA received the claim, because that is later than March. Waiting does not change what qualifies, but it can cost months of payments. This is a made-up illustration, not a VA decision. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.401(a)(3) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.4.A.12.a (references)"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#amount","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"How it is paid","text":"VA's rate tables list \"Spouse receiving Aid and Attendance\" as an added amount on top of your basic monthly rate with a spouse. It grows with your combined rating. For rates effective December 1, 2025 , the added amount ranged from about $61 a month at 30% to about $201 a month at 100%. Rates change each year, so check VA's current compensation rates. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1115(1)(E), (2) VA guidance VA compensation rate tables (effective Dec 1, 2025)"},{"href":"spouse-aid-attendance.html#myths","page":"Spouse Aid and Attendance","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"I have to be rated 100%.\" No. Any combined rating of 30% or more can qualify. \"My spouse's condition has to be from my service.\" No. The test is your spouse's need for help, whatever the cause. \"My spouse has to need care every hour of the day.\" No. The need must be regular, not constant, and not every factor has to apply. \"This is the same as my own SMC.\" No. Your own special monthly compensation is based on your service-connected disabilities. This amount is based on your spouse and is paid in addition. \"VA will add it automatically when my spouse gets sick.\" No. VA needs a claim and evidence. Because pay usually starts no earlier than the claim date, it helps to apply once your spouse meets a test. \"This is for surviving spouses too.\" Not this benefit. A surviving spouse who needs aid and attendance may get it with DIC or Survivors Pension, under separate rules. See ClaimReady's Aid and attendance topic."},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Supplemental Claims: what new evidence does, and when VA orders a new exam","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to VA Supplemental Claims: what new and relevant evidence means, matching evidence to the reason for a denial, when VA orders a new exam, and keeping your effective date. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"A Supplemental Claim asks VA to decide a claim again because you have new and relevant evidence. It works best when the new evidence speaks directly to the reason VA gave for its last decision. This article explains what counts as new and relevant, how to match your evidence to the reason for the denial, when VA's duty to help includes a new exam, and how to keep your effective date. Based on public VA information: the law at 38 U.S.C. §§ 5103A and 5108 and 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.159 and 3.2501, VA's procedures manual (M21-1), and published court decisions. For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#basics","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Supplemental Claims in five points","text":"What it is. A request on VA Form 20-0995 for VA to decide again a benefit it already decided, using new and relevant evidence. When you can file. Any time after VA sends you a decision. Filing within one year of the decision letter can protect your original effective date. The bar is not high. The law says \"new and relevant\" is not a higher standard than the old \"new and material\" rule. The evidence does not have to win the claim; it has to bear on something VA must decide. VA must help. Once VA receives a substantially complete Supplemental Claim, its duty to assist applies again, including getting the records you identify. A new exam is not automatic. VA owes a new exam or opinion only after new and relevant evidence is in, and only when it is needed to decide the claim. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5108; 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501, (a), (c); § 3.159(c)(4)(iv) VA guidance M21-1 II.iii.2.B"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#newrel","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"What \"new and relevant\" means","text":"New Evidence that was not part of the record VA had when it made the earlier decision. Sending the same records again does not count, even with a new cover letter. Relevant Evidence that tends to prove or disprove something VA has to decide. That includes a theory VA has not addressed before. If direct service connection was denied, asking for secondary service connection is itself a new theory. To be complete, the form only has to include or identify the new evidence. If you tell VA where your new records are, VA must try to get them. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.2501(a); § 3.159(c) VA guidance M21-1 II.iii.2.B.1; X.ii.2.A.2"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#dx","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When VA found no current disability","text":"Service connection starts with a current disability. If VA found none, new evidence should show one. Evidence that speaks to it A diagnosis from a doctor, in treatment records or on a Disability Benefits Questionnaire (DBQ). Recent treatment records, test results, or imaging. Your own detailed description of persistent or recurring symptoms: what they are, how often, and since when. Will VA order a new exam? VA owes one when the record shows a current disability or persistent or recurrent symptoms, something in service, and an indication the two may be linked, and does not have enough medical evidence to decide. Lay evidence counts for the symptoms part. You do not need a diagnosis before VA examines you. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A(d)(2); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4)(i)(A)"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#event","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When VA found no event, injury, or disease in service","text":"Evidence that speaks to it Service treatment or personnel records VA did not have, or that you can point to. A buddy statement (VA Form 21-10210) from someone who saw the injury or your symptoms in service. Your own statement with the who, what, when, and where. A credible statement that fits your service can show an event even when records are silent. For combat veterans, the law accepts a satisfactory account of an injury or disease in combat if it is consistent with that service. Will VA order a new exam? If the new evidence now shows something in service, and the record has a current disability or recurring symptoms and an indication of a link, VA generally owes an exam or opinion. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1154(b); 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4)(i)(B) VA guidance M21-1 V.ii.1.B.2"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#link","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When a negative opinion decided the claim","text":"This is the most common reason for a Supplemental Claim. The new evidence should answer the specific reasoning in the opinion VA relied on. Evidence that speaks to it A private medical opinion that gives its reasons and addresses why the VA opinion's reasoning does or does not hold up. See What makes a medical opinion count. Medical literature that supports a link, explained by a clinician in terms of your history. Statements about symptoms that continued from service until now. A different theory, such as secondary service connection. Will VA order a new exam? Not automatically. VA reviews a private favorable opinion for its rationale. If it is adequate, VA can weigh it against the earlier opinion and decide. If the new evidence raises the question but is not enough to decide, VA can ask for a new opinion. If you think the earlier VA opinion was inadequate and you have no new evidence, a Higher-Level Review can look for that duty to assist error instead. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4); § 3.2601(g) VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.2.A.2"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#secondary","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When another condition caused it or made it worse","text":"What VA needs A condition that is already service connected, which stands in for the event in service. Evidence that the claimed condition was caused by it, or has been made worse by it (aggravation). Both are separate ways to qualify. Raising secondary for the first time is a new theory, so it is relevant by itself. Will VA order a new exam? The exam rule includes an indication that the condition may be associated with a service-connected disability. A specific statement (for example, stomach problems since starting a medication for a rated condition), a medical treatise, or a VA rule linking the two can be enough to show a possible link. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.310(a)–(b); § 3.159(c)(4)(i)(C) VA guidance M21-1 IV.i.1.B.1 Court Allen v. Brown, 7 Vet. App. 439 (1995)"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#rating","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When the percentage seems too low","text":"Evidence that speaks to it Records or a DBQ that show findings matching the next higher level of the rating criteria. Read what that level requires first. Statements about flare-ups, frequency, and how the condition limits work and daily life. Evidence that a related benefit applies, such as special monthly compensation or individual unemployability. Will VA order a new exam? VA's guidance: an exam is ordered when the new evidence shows a picture that does not match the current rating. VA's example is a back rated 10% for pain with full motion, where new records show only treatment for pain: no new exam. If the condition is already at the highest rating its diagnostic code allows, VA can decide without an exam. Other benefits may still be worth asking about. If your condition has gotten worse since the decision, a new claim for an increase is also possible. ClaimReady note, from general knowledge: VA's public site directs increase requests to VA Form 21-526EZ. VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.2.A.2; IV.i.1.B.1"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#missed","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When VA denied it after a missed exam","text":"If you miss an exam without good cause on a Supplemental Claim, the law says the claim is denied. VA's guidance says that after the decision, telling VA you are willing to attend counts as new and relevant evidence, and VA will order the exam. VA generally accepts any reasonable explanation, such as illness, hospitalization, or a family emergency. Give it as soon as you can. Keep your address and phone number current with VA. Not answering scheduling calls and letters can count as missing the exam. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.655 VA guidance M21-1 IV.i.2.F.1"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#noexam","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"When VA decided without ever examining you","text":"VA must provide an exam or opinion when it is needed to decide a compensation claim. A court case, McLendon v. Nicholson, explains the test VA uses. All four parts must be met: A current disability, or persistent or recurrent symptoms of one. Your own description of symptoms counts. Something in service: an event, injury, or disease, or a presumptive condition in its time window. An indication they may be linked. A low bar, lower than what it takes to win. A credible statement that symptoms have continued since service can meet it. A bare \"it's related\" may not. Not enough medical evidence to decide without an exam. On a Supplemental Claim, this test applies after new and relevant evidence is in the record. If you believe the record already met the test when VA decided, a Higher-Level Review can treat the missing exam as a duty to assist error and send it back. Toxic exposure claims have their own exam rule. See Toxic exposure exams and opinions (TERA). Law 38 U.S.C. § 5103A(d); § 1168; 38 C.F.R. § 3.159(c)(4) Court McLendon v. Nicholson, 20 Vet. App. 79 (2006); Waters v. Shinseki, 601 F.3d 1274 (Fed. Cir. 2010)"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#steps","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"How VA handles a Supplemental Claim, in plain steps","text":"VA checks that the form is complete. It needs the issue and new evidence included or identified. If incomplete, VA tells you; finishing it within 60 days keeps the filing date. VA gets the evidence you pointed to. Private records need your authorization. VA treatment records you name are requested. VA decides whether the evidence is new and relevant. If not, you get a decision saying there is not enough to decide again. You can seek review of that. If it is, VA decides whether an exam or opinion is needed. Using the test above, or its guidance for rating disagreements. VA decides the claim again on all the evidence. Findings already made in your favor stay in place unless clear and unmistakable evidence shows they were wrong. Law 38 U.S.C. § 5104A; 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.104(c), 3.155, 3.2501 VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.2.A.1–2"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#dates","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Your effective date","text":"When VA receives your Supplemental Claim Earliest effective date Source Within one year of the decision letter Can go back to your original claim date (continuous pursuit). Law 38 U.S.C. § 5110(a)(2); 38 C.F.R. § 3.2500(h) After an intent to file sent within that year, and a complete claim within a year of the intent Can keep the chain to the original date. Court Military-Veterans Advocacy v. Sec'y of Veterans Affairs (Fed. Cir. 2021) More than one year after the decision No earlier than the date VA received the Supplemental Claim. VA guidance M21-1 X.ii.2.A.3 The regulation's text at § 3.2501(d) still says an intent to file applies only to initial claims. The 2021 court decision set that limit aside for Supplemental Claims, and VA's guidance follows the court. Check an effective date"},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#prepare","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Before you file","text":"Find the exact reason for the denial or rating in your decision letter. Note the favorable findings. You do not need to prove those again. Gather evidence aimed at that reason, or a new theory. List every place VA should get records, with dates. Mark the one-year date from the decision letter. Consider free help from an accredited representative. This checklist is not saved. Print the page to keep it."},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#example","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look for Dana","text":"Fictional example VA denied Dana's hearing loss claim. The VA opinion said hearing was normal at separation, so there was no link to flight line noise. Two months later, Dana filed a Supplemental Claim with a private audiologist's opinion. New: the private opinion was not in the record before. Relevant: it speaks to the reason for the denial, the link to service. The audiologist explained, with reasons, why a normal hearing test at separation does not by itself rule out noise-related hearing loss later. Exam: VA reviews the private opinion's rationale. VA might weigh the two opinions and decide, or ask for a new VA opinion. Date: Dana filed within one year, so a grant could reach back to the original claim date. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#myths","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"Sending my records again counts.\" No. Evidence already in the record is not new. \"The new evidence has to prove my case.\" No. It has to tend to prove or disprove something at issue. VA then weighs all the evidence. \"VA will always give me a new exam.\" No. VA decides whether one is needed after the new evidence comes in. \"After a year, I can't file.\" You can file any time. After a year, the effective date is usually later. \"A new doctor's letter can go with a Higher-Level Review.\" No. A Higher-Level Review cannot consider new evidence. A new opinion goes with a Supplemental Claim."},{"href":"supplemental-claims.html#related","page":"Supplemental Claims Guide","kind":"Article","title":"Related on ClaimReady","text":"What makes a medical opinion count, for veterans, doctors, and representatives What VA asks your examiner C&P exams: the complete guide Higher-Level Review and Board appeals Check an effective date"},{"href":"temp-100.html","page":"Temporary 100% Timeline","kind":"Tool","title":"Temporary 100% Timeline","sub":"Free, independent educational timeline for VA temporary 100% ratings after a joint replacement, surgery, or a long hospital stay. Nothing you enter leaves your device.","text":"See how a joint replacement, a surgery recovery, or a hospital stay of more than 21 days can lead to a temporary 100% rating under public VA regulations: the months it covers, when it can start, when payment can start, and what comes after. It explains the rules; it does not decide anything. You are looking at a fictional example. Dana had a knee replacement in March 2025. Clear and enter your own"},{"href":"temp-100.html#scen","page":"Temporary 100% Timeline","kind":"Tool","title":"Validation scenarios (for reviewers)","text":"31 fictional scenarios. Expected results were worked out by hand before the code was written, and an experienced claims representative reviewed and confirmed all 31 in October 2026. A pass here is a software check, not legal validation. # Scenario Result"},{"href":"temp-100.html#notes","page":"Temporary 100% Timeline","kind":"Tool","title":"Rating schedule notes","text":"The notes that are easiest to overlook, quoted from public sources. Prosthetic implants and resurfacing, DCs 5051-5056: current notes Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.71a, eCFR text as of Feb 27, 2026. These notes took effect Feb 7, 2021 (85 FR 76453). Note (1): When an evaluation is assigned for joint resurfacing or the prosthetic replacement of a joint under diagnostic codes 5051-5056, an additional rating under § 4.71a may not also be assigned for that joint, unless otherwise directed. Note (2): Only evaluate a revision procedure in the same manner as the original procedure under diagnostic codes 5051-5056 if all the original components are replaced. Note (3): The term “prosthetic replacement” in diagnostic codes 5051-5053 and 5055-5056 means a total replacement of the named joint. However, in DC 5054, “prosthetic replacement” means a total replacement of the head of the femur or of the acetabulum. Note (4): The 100 percent rating for 1 year following implantation of prosthesis will commence after initial grant of the 1-month total rating assigned under § 4.30 following hospital discharge. Note (5): The 100 percent rating for 4 months following implantation of prosthesis or resurfacing under DCs 5054 and 5055 will commence after initial grant of the 1-month total rating assigned under § 4.30 following hospital discharge. Note (6): Special monthly compensation is assignable during the 100 percent rating period the earliest date permanent use of crutches is established. DC 5054, hip resurfacing or replacement Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.71a, DC 5054 Note: At the conclusion of the 100 percent evaluation period, evaluate resurfacing under diagnostic codes 5250 through 5255; there is no minimum evaluation for resurfacing. Prosthetic implants: notes before Feb 7, 2021 Law 38 C.F.R. § 4.71a, eCFR text as of Feb 4, 2021. Surgeries before Feb 7, 2021 are governed by this version. Note (1): The 100 pct rating for 1 year following implantation of prosthesis will commence after initial grant of the 1-month total rating assigned under § 4.30 following hospital discharge. Note (2): Special monthly compensation is assignable during the 100 pct rating period the earliest date permanent use of crutches is established. Hospital stays over 21 days, § 4.29 VA guidance M21-1 VIII.iv.8.B.1.a, public VA manual, not binding law Provided a total, 100-percent rating cannot be assigned under other provisio"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Proving Vietnam service: medals, Haas, and the Blue Water Navy law","sub":"Free, independent educational guide to how VA concedes Vietnam service: why medals alone do not prove it, Haas v. Peake, Procopio and the Blue Water Navy law, APO numbers, and effective dates for older denials. Nothing is saved or sent.","text":"Many Agent Orange claims turn on one question that comes before any medical issue: did you serve in a place where the law presumes herbicide exposure? When VA accepts that you did, it concedes your Vietnam service. This article explains why a Vietnam medal is not proof by itself, how the courts and Congress changed the rules for Navy veterans offshore, how APO numbers fit in, and why some older denials may deserve a second look. Based on public sources: the statutes 38 U.S.C. §§ 1116 and 1116A, the regulations 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.307, 3.309, and 3.313, published court decisions, and VA's procedures manual, M21-1, Part VIII, Subpart i, Chapter 1 (version used Jul 16, 2026 ). For education only. ClaimReady checks these sources for changes every week. Sources and dates Print or save this article"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#medals","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"A Vietnam medal is not, by itself, proof you served in Vietnam","text":"A Vietnam-related medal tells VA to look closer. It does not, standing alone, prove you were in a place where VA presumes herbicide exposure. The reverse is also true: not having one of these medals does not mean you were never there. VA decides from all of your records. \"There are no recognized medals whose receipt alone connotes service in a recognized herbicide presumptive location.\" VA's procedures manual, M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.2.d. When a veteran has a medal such as the Vietnam Service Medal, the manual tells VA to look carefully through the records for travel or temporary duty (TDY) orders to a qualifying place. Many of these awards could be earned on a ship offshore, on flights, or in support roles outside Vietnam. So a medal shows you took part in or supported the war, but not exactly where you were. Medal Proves qualifying Vietnam service by itself? Why not Vietnam Service Medal No Awarded for service in Vietnam or its contiguous waters or airspace, and for some service in Thailand, Laos, or Cambodia supporting the war (July 3, 1965 to March 28, 1973). One day aboard a ship supporting operations, or one flight, could qualify. Republic of Vietnam Campaign Medal No VA's manual says no medal alone shows service in a presumptive place. VA looks for orders or other records that place you there. Armed Forces Expeditionary Medal No Covers many operations worldwide. For Vietnam it was given for service from July 1, 1958 to July 3, 1965, before the Vietnam Service Medal existed. If you have it, your orders and duty assignments show where you actually were. Republic of Vietnam Gallantry Cross (unit citation) No Same rule: no medal alone is enough. Your own orders, assignments, and records show where you were. VA used to accept the Vietnam Service Medal as proof. That changed in 2002, and the courts upheld the change in Haas v. Peake (2008). Grants VA made under the older medal rule are not taken away. How this happened VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.A; 1.B.2.d Court Haas v. Peake, 525 F.3d 1168 (Fed. Cir. 2008) Background Vietnam Service Medal and Expeditionary Medal rules: 32 C.F.R. § 578.26 (2008); Navy History and Heritage Command"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#three","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Three separate questions","text":"People often blend these together. VA answers them one at a time. Where did you serve? Did you serve in Vietnam, its inland waters, or its qualifying offshore waters, during January 9, 1962 to May 7, 1975? This article is mostly about this question. Is exposure presumed? If yes to question 1, the law presumes you were exposed to herbicides, unless there is affirmative evidence you were not. Is the condition presumed? If your condition is on the herbicide list (such as type 2 diabetes, ischemic heart disease, or prostate cancer) and meets any time or severity rules, VA presumes the link to service. If any answer is no, the claim is not over. You can still try to show exposure or a link to service with evidence, called direct service connection. A presumption is an extra route, not the only one. See Not on the Agent Orange list? What \"no positive association\" means. Law 38 U.S.C. §§ 1116, 1116A; 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.307(a)(6), 3.309(e) VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.A; 1.C"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#timeline","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"How the law changed","text":"The rules for offshore veterans moved back and forth for nearly 30 years. Knowing which rule applied when explains many older decisions. 1991 VA's manual accepts the Vietnam Service Medal Without contrary evidence, VA conceded Vietnam service if records showed the medal. 1997 Narrower VA General Counsel opinion 27‑97 Service on a deep-water ship off the coast, without duty or a visit in Vietnam, is not \"service in the Republic of Vietnam\" for the definition of the Vietnam era. 2002 Narrower VA's manual requires service on land The manual now said the medal does not prove you were \"in country.\" VA had stated the land requirement in a 2001 rule on diabetes. 2006 Wider Haas v. Nicholson, Veterans Court Ruled for the veteran: VA could not apply the land requirement to him, and the older medal rule controlled his case. 2008 Narrower Haas v. Peake, Federal Circuit Reversed. VA's land requirement was a reasonable reading of the law, and the medal rule was manual guidance VA could change. 2019 Wider Procopio v. Wilkie, Federal Circuit (full court) January 29: \"served in the Republic of Vietnam\" includes Vietnam's 12-mile territorial sea. Haas is overruled. Congress passed the Blue Water Navy law on June 25. 2020 Wider Blue Water Navy law takes effect January 1: 38 U.S.C. § 1116A presumes exposure for qualifying offshore service, with a special effective date rule for older denials. 2022 Wider PACT Act August 10: added herbicide presumptions for Thailand, Laos, Cambodia, Guam, American Samoa, and Johnston Atoll, and added high blood pressure and MGUS to the list of conditions. Court Haas v. Nicholson, 20 Vet. App. 257 (2006); Haas v. Peake, 525 F.3d 1168 (Fed. Cir. 2008); Procopio v. Wilkie, 913 F.3d 1371 (Fed. Cir. 2019) (en banc) Law Pub. L. 116-23; Pub. L. 117-168; VAOPGCPREC 27-97"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#haas","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Haas v. Peake: why the medal stopped counting","text":"The facts. Jonathan Haas served in the Navy from 1967 to 1969 aboard the USS Mount Katmai, an ammunition ship operating off the coast of Vietnam. Because of its explosive cargo, the ship did not dock in Vietnamese ports, and he never went ashore. In 2001 he claimed diabetes and other conditions from Agent Orange. VA denied the presumption because he had not served on land. 2006: the Veterans Court Held that the regulation was unclear and declined to accept VA's land-only reading. Treated the old manual rule (the medal concedes Vietnam service) as a binding rule that VA could not drop without public notice and comment, and that could not be applied against his earlier claim. 2008: the Federal Circuit Reversed. The words \"served in the Republic of Vietnam\" were ambiguous, and VA's requirement of service on land was a reasonable reading that courts must respect. The manual's medal rule was guidance to VA staff, not a rule with the force of law, so VA could change it without notice and comment. Haas could still try to prove actual exposure, but he did not get the presumption. What still holds from Haas: a medal alone is not proof of where you served. What no longer holds: the rule that you had to set foot on land. Procopio overruled Haas on that point in 2019. Court Haas v. Nicholson, 20 Vet. App. 257 (2006); Haas v. Peake, 525 F.3d 1168 (Fed. Cir. 2008), reh'g denied, 544 F.3d 1306"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#procopio","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Procopio and the Blue Water Navy law","text":"The court: Procopio v. Wilkie (2019) Alfred Procopio Jr. served aboard the USS Intrepid, which operated in Vietnam's territorial sea in 1966. He claimed diabetes and prostate cancer. The full Federal Circuit held that \"served in the Republic of Vietnam\" in § 1116 clearly includes Vietnam's 12-nautical-mile territorial sea, and said \"Haas is overruled.\" Congress: 38 U.S.C. § 1116A (2020) Passed as Public Law 116-23 on June 25, 2019. In effect January 1, 2020. Presumes herbicide exposure for service offshore of Vietnam from January 9, 1962 to May 7, 1975, unless affirmative evidence shows no exposure. \"Offshore\" means no more than 12 nautical miles seaward of a line the law draws along Vietnam's coast by map coordinates, from the Cambodian border to Con Co Island. You do not need to have stepped on land if your ship was inside that line during the period. You still need proof of where your ship was, and when you were aboard. A medal does not show this. VA checks ship records such as deck logs. VA's guidance says service in any branch aboard a qualifying ship counts, as can service with a Navy air squadron or a Marine unit assigned to the ship. Court Procopio v. Wilkie, 913 F.3d 1371 (Fed. Cir. 2019) (en banc) Law 38 U.S.C. § 1116A(a), (b), (d); Pub. L. 116-23, § 2 VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B; 1.D"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#apo","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"APO and FPO numbers","text":"An APO (Army or Air Force Post Office) or FPO (Fleet Post Office) number is a military mailing address. It can appear on medical and dental records, orders, and letters. VA's guidance tells reviewers to pay particular attention to APO and FPO numbers in service treatment and dental records, because a number may be associated with a place where the herbicide presumption applies. VA matches numbers against a historical list of Vietnam-era Pacific APO and FPO addresses. An APO number alone does not always prove Vietnam service. The Pacific APO numbers of that era served bases in many countries, including Thailand, Japan, Okinawa, the Philippines, Taiwan, and Guam, as well as Vietnam. What helps is an APO number that matches a specific installation in Vietnam at the time, on a record that ties it to you. A number that only shows mail was routed to Vietnam, or that belonged to a unit with people in several countries, may need more proof. How to check an APO number on your records Find it. Note the document (for example, a dental record or orders), the date, and your name on it. A number on a record that is yours and dated during the period carries more weight. Match it to a place and time. APO numbers were reassigned over the years, so the date matters. Tie it to you. Does the record show you were at that installation, not just your unit's mailing address? Check the region. Is it a Vietnam installation, or another Pacific location such as Thailand or Okinawa? Thailand has its own presumption with different rules. Tell VA what you found. Send copies, with your approximate dates and places. If something is unclear, say so; VA can request more records. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.2.d Background Which places Pacific APO numbers served is general historical information, not from a VA source"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#s3313","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"The special rule for non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (38 C.F.R. § 3.313)","text":"§ 3.313 is a separate regulation from 1990 for one disease: non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. If you served in Vietnam during the Vietnam era and later developed it, that service is enough for service connection. Its definition of service in Vietnam includes the waters offshore, and other places if your duty involved duty or a visit in Vietnam. VA's general counsel later read it as not covering high-altitude flights over Vietnam. It does not depend on herbicide exposure. That is why offshore veterans could use it even when the herbicide presumption required land service. Haas found the difference reasonable for that reason. Do not borrow § 3.313's definition for other conditions. For the herbicide list, the rules in §§ 1116 and 1116A and § 3.307 apply. This 1997 opinion concluded that service on a deep-water ship off Vietnam, without duty or a visit in Vietnam, was not \"service in the Republic of Vietnam\" for the definition of the Vietnam era in 38 U.S.C. § 101(29)(A). That definition gives an earlier start date (now November 1, 1955) to veterans who served in Vietnam. Haas relied on it. Read it in that context: it was not written about the Blue Water Navy law, which came later. If the start of your Vietnam era matters to your claim, ask an accredited representative how current law applies. Law 38 C.F.R. § 3.313; 38 U.S.C. § 101(29); VAOPGCPREC 7-93; VAOPGCPREC 27-97 Court Haas v. Peake"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#records","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Records that can place you there","text":"A Vietnam medal should start a closer look at your records, not end it. These are the kinds of records VA looks at. What VA's guidance says to review Every certified DD Form 214: Foreign Service in the qualifying place, or a separating station or last duty assignment there Military personnel records, including the DA Form 20 (Enlisted Qualification Record): service locations Travel or flight orders, TDY orders, and statements in performance evaluations about travel or flights Service treatment and dental records: treatment in the qualifying place, and APO or FPO numbers For ships: VA uses a separate process that checks where the ship was, such as deck logs, against your dates aboard What you can add Approximate dates and places, and what you were doing The ship's name and your dates aboard Letters, photos, or orders you kept that place you there Statements from people who served with you How VA checks your service, in plain steps VA reviews the records it has. If they show service in a qualifying place, VA documents it and moves on to your condition. If not, VA gets the rest of your records. That means your DD 214, service treatment records, and your entire personnel file, or a note that they are unavailable. VA's guidance says not to deny for lack of proof before this step is done. If you described your service only in general terms, VA sends a letter asking for specific dates and places. You have 30 days to answer. If you don't give details, VA's guidance says it will not establish herbicide exposure and will continue with the claim. If you gave specific dates and places (including dates of temporary duty) that your records don't confirm, VA can ask for research into unit and service records. Some situations have their own process: service only on a ship (VA verifies where the ship was), C-123 aircraft contact, the Korean DMZ, and places or circumstances not on any presumptive list. The details you give matter most at step 3. Specific dates, places, unit or ship names, and orders let VA look for proof; a general statement that you served \"in Vietnam\" may not be enough. VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.B.1.c; 1.B.2.b, d; 1.D"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#outcomes","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Four ways a decision may describe your service","text":"A rating decision usually explains whether VA conceded your service and why. Here is what each kind of finding generally means, and what you can do. \"Service in Vietnam is established\" VA accepted that records place you in Vietnam or its inland waters during the period. Herbicide exposure is presumed. Check: which records VA relied on, and that the dates are right. \"Qualifying offshore service is established\" VA accepted that your ship was within the offshore line under § 1116A. Herbicide exposure is presumed. Check: if you were denied for the same condition before 2020, whether the older claim date applies (see below). \"More verification is needed\" Your records hint at Vietnam service, such as a medal, but do not show where you were. What helps: the specific dates, places, ship, unit, or orders VA asks for. \"Qualifying service is not established\" VA found the evidence does not show qualifying service. Still possible: another place's presumption (such as Thailand), proof of actual exposure, or direct service connection. New records showing qualifying service can support a Supplemental Claim. These are general descriptions to help you read a decision. They are not VA's wording and do not predict any outcome."},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#older","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"Older denials and your effective date","text":"If VA denied an Agent Orange claim years ago because you never set foot in Vietnam, look closely at the reason for that denial. The effective date of a new grant is not automatically the date of your new claim, or January 1, 2020. The Blue Water Navy rule. If you filed a compensation claim for a covered disease between September 25, 1985 and January 1, 2020, it was denied because service connection was not established, and you file again for the same condition on or after January 1, 2020 and are granted under § 1116A, VA uses the date of the earlier claim. A survivor can also qualify. Nehmer. A court order with its own effective date rules for Vietnam veterans and survivors, including qualifying offshore service. It does not apply to service only outside Vietnam, such as Thailand. VA's guidance says to screen for it even when your exposure was already conceded and you claim a new condition on the list. See ClaimReady's Nehmer topic. If you were denied for no qualifying service, VA's guidance says to make sure all your service records are in the file and to check them for possible offshore service. New service records. If service records that existed then, but were not in your file, now prove qualifying service, VA can reconsider the old claim from its original date (38 C.F.R. § 3.156(c)). A law change alone can allow payment up to one year before your claim (38 C.F.R. § 3.114). Grants under the old medal rule are not taken away. They still support increases and secondary claims. What to gather: the dates of every earlier claim, each decision and the reason given, the conditions you claimed, and your ship or unit records. Effective date rules are technical, so an accredited representative can help at no cost. Law 38 U.S.C. § 1116A(c); 38 C.F.R. §§ 3.114, 3.156(c), 3.816 VA guidance M21-1 VIII.i.1.A; 1.B.2.b; 1.C; 2.B"},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#example","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"How this might look","text":"Fictional example A Navy veteran served aboard a destroyer from 1968 to 1969 and received the Vietnam Service Medal. In 2008, VA denied a claim for ischemic heart disease because the veteran never went ashore. The veteran files again in 2024. The medal alone does not show the ship was inside the 12-mile offshore line. VA checks the ship's deck logs for the dates the veteran was aboard. If the logs place the ship inside the line while the veteran was aboard, exposure is presumed under § 1116A, and ischemic heart disease is on the herbicide list. The 2008 claim was for the same condition, filed in the 1985 to 2020 window, and denied for lack of service connection. So VA would look at whether the 2008 claim date applies. This is a made-up example to show how the rules fit together. It is not a real VA decision and does not predict any outcome."},{"href":"vietnam-service.html#myths","page":"Proving Vietnam Service","kind":"Article","title":"What people often get wrong","text":"\"My Vietnam Service Medal proves I was in Vietnam.\" Not by itself. It could be earned offshore, in the air, or in nearby countries. VA looks at your records to see where you were. \"No medal means I can't prove Vietnam service.\" No. Orders, personnel and medical records, ship records, and other evidence can place you there. \"Navy veterans who stayed on the ship can't get the presumption.\" That was the old rule. Since January 1, 2020, service within the 12-mile offshore line qualifies. \"Any ship that supported the war counts.\" No. The ship must have been inside the offshore line while you were aboard, during January 9, 1962 to May 7, 1975. \"An APO number on my records settles it.\" Not always. The same series of numbers served bases across the Pacific. It helps when the number matches a Vietnam installation and a record ties it to you. \"If I'm granted now, I'm paid only from my new claim.\" Not necessarily. The Blue Water Navy rule, Nehmer, and new service records can each allow an earlier date."}];
